{
  "$schema": "https://schemastore.azurewebsites.net/schemas/json/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Skala",
          "version": "4.4.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "SK0001",
              "name": "FileIsNotFormatted",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The formatting half of Skala and the analysis half are one report. A finding that says \"this file is not formatted\" carries the formatter's own edits as its fix, so a machine reading the SARIF can apply them without a second tool invocation and a human sees formatting and analysis in one list. It is a suggestion rather than a warning because formatting is unconditional and free: the gate that cares is `formatting: clean`, not a severity."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala format` would change this file."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The file is not formatted"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0002",
              "name": "LineTooLongUnbreakable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A 200-character string literal and a deeply-qualified generic type are not the formatter's to shorten: it never breaks a token and never breaks inside a string. Reporting it at hint makes the audit possible without making the ordinary run noisy."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A line exceeds `max_line_length` and the formatter had no break point to use."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The line is over the width and nothing in it can break"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0003",
              "name": "MalformedXmlDoc",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Malformed doc comments are common in real code — an unescaped `<`, a `<br>` borrowed from HTML, a tag someone forgot to close. Skala never repairs them: a formatter that repairs documentation is a formatter that changes what the documentation says. Reporting it is the whole of the response."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `///` comment does not parse as XML, so it is left exactly as written."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The documentation comment is not well-formed XML"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.4"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0201",
              "name": "ArrangeBodyStyle",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Body shape is cleanup rather than whitespace: changing `{ return value; }` to `=> value` rewrites syntax even when it preserves behavior. The finding is produced from the arranger's actual fixed-point edit, so `verify` and `arrange` share one implementation and one effective configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would change a block body to an expression body or the reverse."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The body style is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0201.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0202",
              "name": "ArrangeVar",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Whether `var` preserves the declared type is a semantic question, especially for numeric conversions, interfaces and base types. The rule binds the initializer and rewrites only when the configured direction preserves the type."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use `var` or an explicit local type under the configured preference."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local variable type is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0202.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0203",
              "name": "ArrangeTargetTypedNew",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Target-typed `new` removes a repeated type only where the surrounding context supplies exactly that type. Binding is required so a cleanup never changes overload resolution or the constructed type."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add or remove the type in an object creation expression."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The object creation is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0203.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0204",
              "name": "ArrangeDefaultLiteral",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A default literal is safe only when its target type is known. The semantic rule proves that target before removing a written type, and can restore the explicit form when the configuration asks for it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use `default` or `default(T)` under the configured preference."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The default value expression is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0204.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "7.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0205",
              "name": "ArrangeNullPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Equality operators may be overloaded, so changing `x != null` into a pattern is not merely textual. The arranger resolves the operator and runs its diagnostic-delta safety check before keeping the rewrite."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would change the configured null-checking pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The null check is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0205.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0206",
              "name": "ArrangeEmptyString",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`skala_empty_string` chooses between the empty literal and `string.Empty`, and both values rewrite: `empty_literal` turns `string.Empty` into `\"\"`, `string_empty` turns `\"\"` (and `@\"\"`) into `string.Empty`. Binding prevents a same-named member on another type from being mistaken for the framework field."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use the configured empty-string spelling."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The empty string expression is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0206.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0207",
              "name": "ArrangeThisQualifier",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Adding or removing `this.` is safe only when the resulting identifier binds to the same field, property, method or event. The arranger compares symbols and reverts a rewrite that changes binding."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add or remove `this.` for the referenced member kind."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The instance-member qualifier is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0207.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0208",
              "name": "ArrangeRedundantBraces",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Brace cleanup rewrites the statement tree rather than whitespace. The rule preserves dangling-else ownership, directives, declarations and formatter-off regions before offering an edit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add or remove braces under the configured cleanup style."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The control-statement braces are not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0208.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0209",
              "name": "ArrangeRedundantParentheses",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Parenthesis removal is the arranger's highest-risk syntactic cleanup, so each removal is proved rather than computed: the parentheses go only when the expression printed without them parses back to the same tree. Which redundant parentheses Skala is willing to lose is a separate, configured question — `skala_parentheses_redundancy_style` and the three `dotnet_style_parentheses_in_*_binary_operators` keys decide it. It runs by default; there is no opt-in flag."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would remove redundant parentheses."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The expression parentheses are not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0209.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0210",
              "name": "ArrangeUsings",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sorting and placement are syntactic; removal is conservative and occurs only when the directive is unused in every loaded compilation containing the file. One id represents the fixed-point using block because the edits overlap and must be applied together."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would sort, place or remove using directives."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The using directives are not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0210.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0211",
              "name": "ArrangePredefinedType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The declaration and member-access preferences are independent. Binding distinguishes `System.Int32` from a same-named user type before replacing it with `int`, or performs the reverse cleanup when configured."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use a keyword or framework type name under the configured preference."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The predefined type spelling is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0211.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0212",
              "name": "ArrangeAccessibility",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The rule knows the language-defined default for each declaration kind and removes only an explicitly written modifier equal to it. It does not infer project API policy."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would omit an accessibility modifier that matches the language default."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The accessibility modifier is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0212.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0213",
              "name": "ArrangeNamespace",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Namespace form is a tree rewrite with trivia and using-directive ownership consequences. The arrangement pipeline performs it together with using placement and verifies the combined result reaches a fixed point."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use the configured file-scoped or block-scoped namespace form."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The namespace declaration is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0213.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "10.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0214",
              "name": "ArrangeTrailingComma",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A trailing comma is a token rather than whitespace, so it belongs to cleanup. The rule distinguishes single-line and multiline lists and follows their separate configuration values."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add or remove a trailing comma in a supported list."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The trailing comma is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0214.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0215",
              "name": "ArrangeStaticQualifier",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A qualifier can be removed or replaced only if the resulting reference resolves to the same static member. The rule binds both forms and the pipeline rejects any symbol change. A namespace-qualified receiver such as `Reporting.Fingerprints.Normalize(text)` becomes `Fingerprints.Normalize(text)` when the shorter expression already resolves to the same type; the static type qualifier remains when required by scope or configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add, remove or change a static-member type qualifier, or shorten a redundant namespace prefix on its type receiver."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The static-member qualifier is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0215.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0216",
              "name": "ArrangeArgumentStyle",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Adding a name requires the resolved parameter; removing one is safe only when the argument already occupies that parameter's position. The rule binds the selected overload and preserves names that carry ordering semantics."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would add or remove argument names under the configured style."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The argument naming style is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0216.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0217",
              "name": "ArrangeDiscardDeclaration",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The cleanup preference chooses between `out _` and `out var _`. Both spell the same discard, but the change adds or removes syntax and therefore belongs to arrangement rather than formatting."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would use the configured explicit or implicit discard declaration."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The discard declaration is not arranged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0217.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0218",
              "name": "ArrangePropertyPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A condition such as `!rule.Retired && rule.RequiresSemantics` describes one object state. A property pattern expresses that state together, while retaining each member name and its required boolean value."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` combines boolean member checks on the same non-null receiver into a property pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The boolean member tests can use a property pattern"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0218.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0219",
              "name": "ArrangeQualifiedReference",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A qualifier tells the reader that the short name would have meant something else. Where it would not — `new System.Text.StringBuilder()` beside `using System.Text;` — the qualifier is noise, and an AI-written file carries it in every position. The rule binds the shortened name at the reference's own position and rewrites only when it reaches the same type, so a shadowed or ambiguous name keeps its qualifier."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala arrange` would drop a namespace qualifier, or a `global::`, that the reference does not need under `skala_prefer_qualified_reference = false`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A namespace-qualified reference is not shortened"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0219.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Formatting",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0230",
              "name": "EmptyInitializer",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Empty braces read as a list somebody is about to fill in and stay in the file for years after nobody is. `new Foo { }` is two characters of ceremony over `new Foo()`. `x with { }` is more than ceremony — `with` invokes the record's compiler-generated copy constructor, so it allocates a whole instance in order to produce a value equal to the one it was given.\n\n⚠ ReSharper rates `RedundantWithExpression` a *suggestion* and `RedundantEmptyObjectOrCollectionInitializer` a *warning*; Skala reports one concept at `warning` and reading `resharper_redundant_with_expression_highlighting` therefore lowers both. The severity split is the same observation this rule's `fixIsSafe: false` records: the empty initializer is nothing, and the empty `with` is an allocation somebody may have meant."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A pair of initializer braces that sets nothing: `new Foo { }` is `new Foo()`, and `x with { }` copies a record and changes nothing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `with` expression or object initializer is empty"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0230.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0231",
              "name": "RedundantStringCall",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two of the five are allocations rather than noise. `s.ToCharArray()` copies the whole string so that `foreach` can walk what `string` already enumerates by index, and `string.Format` runs a format parser over a format that has no items. The other three are the reader's cost: a `$` promises a hole, a `@` promises something that needs escaping, and a `.ToString()` on a string promises a conversion. Each one is a sentence that turns out to say nothing, and a reader has to finish it before finding that out."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`ToString()` on a string, `foreach` over `ToCharArray()`, `string.Format` of a literal with no placeholders, an interpolated string with no holes, and a verbatim prefix that escapes nothing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The string call produces the string it was given"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0231.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0232",
              "name": "RedundantArgument",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An argument restating a default is a value that silently stops tracking the default the day it changes — the same hazard `optional-and-params-hazards` reports from the declaration side, seen from the call. `new EventHandler(Foo)` is an allocation as well as noise: the method group converts to the delegate on its own, and writing the conversion out produces exactly the same object through more text."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A trailing argument that restates the parameter's default, a delegate creation the conversion would have performed, or lambda parameter types the target already declares."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The argument or signature element is redundant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0232.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0233",
              "name": "RedundantSyntax",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each one is a token the grammar allows and the reader has to account for. None of them is a judgement call: the deletion is proved by the shape, there is no semantic component at all, and the fix is the shortest edit in the catalogue. Doc 17 § \"A large share of the 580 is cheap\" is about exactly this family — the mechanical cleanups a Rider user does not notice they rely on until they are gone.\n\n⚠ ReSharper rates `RedundantAttributeParentheses` a *hint* and rates `RedundantAnonymousTypePropertyName`, one of the other eight shapes under this id, a *warning*. Skala reports the concept at `suggestion`, so reading `resharper_redundant_attribute_parentheses_highlighting` raises the whole concept to whatever that one key says."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Nine token-level deletions: an empty attribute argument list, a single lambda parameter's parentheses, parentheses around a whole pattern, a semicolon after a braced declaration, braces around a one-argument collection element, an anonymous-type property name that restates the expression, `ascending`, a range bound that is already the default, and an empty property pattern under a type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The syntax is redundant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0233.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0234",
              "name": "RedundantCast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each one is a conversion that converts nothing, and each is read as if it might. A cast makes a reader ask what the operand was before it; explicit type arguments make them ask what inference would have chosen; an array size makes them count the elements to see whether the two agree."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A cast whose operand already has the cast's type, explicit method type arguments inference reproduces exactly, an array size that repeats the element count, and tuple component names the declared type already carries."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The cast or type argument is redundant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0234.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0240",
              "name": "RedundantControlFlow",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **`catch (X) { throw; }` is the member that matters and the other two are housekeeping beside it.** A rethrowing catch reads as error handling: it is where a reader goes looking for the recovery, the wrapping, the logging — and there is none. `throw;` preserves the stack trace, so unlike `SK2015`'s `throw ex;` there is not even a defect underneath it; the clause is inert, and every question it raises has the answer \"nothing happens here\", which is what the code would say with no `catch` at all. The other two shapes are the same sentence about ordinary control flow: a `continue;` at the end of a loop body jumps to the next iteration that was one statement away, a `return;` at the end of a void body leaves a method that was about to end, and a `default:` section that only breaks describes what a `switch` with no matching section already does."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A jump, a `default:` section, a `catch`, an empty `finally`, an `else` on a branch that never falls through, or a switch-expression arm whose value the arm below it repeats — control flow that transfers to where control was already going."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The control flow does nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0240.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0241",
              "name": "IneffectiveModifier",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A modifier that does nothing is worse than noise, because a reader cannot tell it from one that does. `sealed` on a member of a `sealed` class invites the question of which derived type was going to override it, and there is none; `abstract` on an interface member suggests the interface has members that are not; `readonly` on one member of a `readonly struct` suggests the others are mutable. Each one is answered by a keyword written a few lines above it, and deleting it makes the declaration say exactly what it meant."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A keyword that restates what the enclosing declaration has already said: `abstract` on an interface member, `sealed` inside a `sealed` type, `class` after `record`, `: int` on an enum, `readonly` inside a `readonly struct`, `scoped` on an `out` parameter."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The modifier has no effect"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0241.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0242",
              "name": "RedundantNullableDirective",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`#nullable` directives accumulate during a migration and then stay, at which point they silently override the project-level setting for a file nobody remembers exempting. The state they move is two independent settings — annotations and warnings — each of which is enabled, disabled, or inherited from the project, so a directive is redundant exactly when it moves neither. Deleting one that moves neither is the whole of the edit: the file's nullable state before it and after it are the same state."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `#nullable` directive that sets the annotation and warning state the file is already in."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `#nullable` directive changes nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0242.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0243",
              "name": "RedundantQualifier",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A qualifier is how a reader is told that the short name would have meant something else, so one that changes nothing is a false signal: `System.Text.StringBuilder` beside a `using System.Text;` says there is an ambiguity to resolve, and there is not. `base.` says louder still — it says the containing type has its own member of that name and this call deliberately goes past it. `SK0207` owns the `this.` qualifier and `SK0215` the static one; these are the two shapes the arrangement rules leave."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A namespace- or type-qualified type name the simple name already binds to, or a `base.` that reaches the same member as no qualifier."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The qualifier is redundant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0243.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0244",
              "name": "RedundantDeclaration",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **The empty finalizer is not merely redundant, and it stays in this rule at `warning` rather than moving to the performance range.** An empty `~Foo() { }` opts the type into finalization: every instance goes on the finalizer queue, survives the collection that would otherwise have taken it, and is freed a generation later — for a body that does nothing. It is kept here because the finding and the edit are the same as the other five, a declaration that adds nothing being deleted; splitting it out would have made one concept two ids for the sake of a severity, and the cost is instead in the message, where a reader will actually see it. The other five are deletions with no effect at all: the compiler generates exactly the constructor that was written, supplies exactly that constructor initializer, zero-initializes exactly that field, and dispatches to exactly the base method the override forwards to."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An empty finalizer, an empty sole constructor, an empty namespace, a `: base()` with no arguments, a member initialized to the value it already has, or an `override` that only calls the base."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The declaration adds nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0244.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0250",
              "name": "RedundantDiscardDesignation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A designation is the reader's answer to \"what is this match called\", and `_` is the answer \"it is not called anything\" written down. Deleting it removes a question rather than an answer: `o is string _` makes a reader look for the `_` further along the expression, and `o is string` does not. It is also the shape a model reaches for by habit, because `case string _:` was the only spelling C# had before type patterns arrived in C# 9 and most of the training data predates them.\n\n⚠ ReSharper rates this a *suggestion* and Skala matches it. The neighbouring inspection `skala_prefer_explicit_discard_declaration` is **not** this key: that one is the `out var _` ⇔ `out _` style choice, it is a tier-A option, and Skala already performs it through `SK0217`. Reading the two as one concept is how this id would have become a double-count."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A pattern whose match is designated `_` — `o is string _`, `o is Point { X: 1 } _` — where the designation declares nothing and the pattern means the same without it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The discard designation is redundant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0250.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0260",
              "name": "RedundantBooleanExpression",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each shape asks the reader to evaluate a boolean twice. `flag == true` is `flag` with a comparison in front of it, and the comparison is the part a reader has to check is not `=`; `!!ready` reads as a typo until it is proved not to be; `!(a == b)` makes the reader invert an operator the language already has; `found ? true : false` is a conditional whose branches are the answer to the condition it was given; and `enabled && true` is an operand that was written to be true and stayed. ⚠ The shapes matter disproportionately for generated code: a model that assembles a condition from parts emits `&& true` as the identity element of a fold, and emits `? true : false` because most of its training data predates the habit of returning the condition.\n\n⚠ Five ReSharper inspections, one concept: `RedundantBoolCompare`, `DoubleNegationOperator`, `NegativeEqualityExpression`, `RedundantTernaryExpression` and `RedundantLogicalConditionalExpressionOperand`. ReSharper rates the first four a *warning* and the last a *suggestion*; Skala reports the concept at `warning`.\n\n⚠ **Two of the five are hosted by Roslyn and three are not, and that was measured rather than assumed.** Reflecting over `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeStyle` in SDK 10.0.400 — 440 descriptors, read out of the assemblies because an error log cannot tell `Hidden` from absent — `IDE0100` (\"remove redundant equality\") covers `x == true` and `x != false`, and `IDE0075` (\"simplify conditional expression\") covers `c ? true : false`. **Both are `IsEnabledByDefault: true` with `DefaultSeverity: Hidden`**, so a plain `dotnet build` emits neither: the code-style analyzers are not loaded at all without `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild`, and even then a `Hidden` rule needs an explicit `dotnet_diagnostic.IDExxxx.severity` line before it says anything. Skala ships over them on the reasoning `SK1005` already uses for `IDE0161`. ⚠ **The other three are verified zeros, not unchecked ones**: with all 120 `IDE` ids forced to `warning` and `IDE0055`, `IDE0161`, `IDE0100`, `IDE0075` and `IDE0380` all firing on the same compilation, nothing reported `!!x`, `!(a == b)` or `x && true` — and there was no `AD0001`, so no analyzer crashed silently past them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A boolean expression that restates its own operand: a comparison against `true` or `false`, a double negation, a negated equality, a conditional returning the two boolean literals, or a `&&`/`||` operand that cannot change the result."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The boolean expression says the same thing twice"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0260.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0261",
              "name": "RedundantAttributeDetail",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`[SerializableAttribute]` and `[Serializable]` are the same attribute, and the suffix is the part the language was designed to let you leave out — writing it makes a reader check whether this is the type they know under the short name. `Inherited = true` and `AllowMultiple = false` are worse than noise: they are the defaults written out, so a reader who has learned to read the argument list as \"the decisions this attribute made\" is handed two decisions that were never taken. ⚠ Both are shapes a model reaches for, because the fully-spelled form is what the framework reference documentation shows.\n\n⚠ Two ReSharper inspections, one concept: `RedundantAttributeSuffix` (a *warning*) and `RedundantAttributeUsageProperty` (a *suggestion*). Skala reports the concept at `warning`.\n\n⚠ **Neither is hosted, and both zeros are verified rather than assumed.** Reflecting over SDK 10.0.400's analyzer assemblies gives 440 descriptors and none of them is either shape; a probe with all 120 `IDE` ids forced to `warning`, `AnalysisMode=All` and `AnalysisLevel=latest-all` reported nothing on `[SerializableAttribute]` or on `Inherited = true`, on a compilation where `IDE0055`, `IDE0161`, `IDE0100`, `IDE0075` and `IDE0380` all fired and no `AD0001` was raised. Neither shape is a compiler warning either."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An attribute written with the `Attribute` suffix the language appends for you, or an `[AttributeUsage]` argument that restates that attribute's own default."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The attribute writes out what the language already supplies"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0261.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0280",
              "name": "RedundantBaseListEntry",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`class Buffer : IList<byte>, IEnumerable<byte>` says the same thing twice, and the second saying is the expensive one: a reader who knows `IList<T>` extends `IEnumerable<T>` has to stop and work out whether the author knew it too, or whether the second entry is there because something about this type is different. Nothing about it is. ⚠ The shape belongs disproportionately to generated code, because a model that has been asked for \"a list that can be enumerated\" writes down both nouns it was given.\n\n⚠ **Narrower than ReSharper's inspection, and the difference is a correctness argument rather than a scope cut.** `RedundantExtendsListEntry` reports two shapes and `jb inspectcode` 2025.2.6 was run on both: an interface implied by another *interface* in the same list (`class C : IDerived, IBase`), and an interface implied by the *base class* (`class D : B, IBase` where `B : IBase`). Skala ships only the first. Re-listing an interface on a derived class makes that class re-implement it, so the interface mapping is recomputed there — a member declared `new` in the derived class becomes the implementation instead of the base's, and deleting the entry silently changes which method an interface call reaches.\n\n⚠ **Nothing in the SDK hosts either shape, and the zero was measured.** Reading `IsEnabledByDefault`/`DefaultSeverity` out of SDK 10.0.400's analyzer assemblies gives 442 descriptors and none of them is this; a probe with all 120 `IDE` ids and all 320 `CA` ids forced to `warning`, `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`, `AnalysisMode=All` and `AnalysisLevel=latest-all` reported nothing on `class C : IDerived, IBase`, on a compilation where `IDE0055`, `IDE0160`, `IDE0380` and `CA1051` all fired and there was no `AD0001`.\n\n⚠ `class C : object` is **not** this finding. ReSharper does not report it either — probed in the same run."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A base list naming an interface that another entry in the same list already brings in."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The base list names an interface it already has"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0280.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0281",
              "name": "RedundantRequiredMembersAttribute",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The attribute is a promise to the compiler — this constructor assigns every `required` member, so a caller need not use an object initializer. Against an empty set it promises nothing, and it is worse than noise in two directions: it tells a reader that requiredness is in play on this type when it is not, and it keeps compiling silently on the day somebody adds a `required` member that the constructor does not assign, which is the exact case the attribute exists to make loud. ⚠ Models apply it defensively, because it is the documented way to make a constructor and `required` coexist and the constructor is the part they were asked for.\n\n⚠ **Not hosted, and the zero was measured rather than assumed.** Reading `IsEnabledByDefault`/`DefaultSeverity` out of SDK 10.0.400's analyzer assemblies gives 442 descriptors and none of them is this shape — an error log could not have answered it, because a `Hidden` rule and an absent one produce the same empty log. A behavioural probe with all 120 `IDE` ids and all 320 `CA` ids forced to `warning`, `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`, `AnalysisMode=All` and `AnalysisLevel=latest-all` reported nothing on a `[SetsRequiredMembers]` constructor of a type with no required members, on a compilation where `IDE0055`, `IDE0160`, `IDE0290`, `IDE0380` and `CA1051` all fired and no analyzer crashed. `jb inspectcode` 2025.2.6 reports it on the same file, and correctly declines the type that does have a required member."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`[SetsRequiredMembers]` on a constructor of a type that declares and inherits no `required` member."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The attribute promises to set members that do not exist"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0281.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0282",
              "name": "RedundantPositionalProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`record Point(int X, int Y) { public int X { get; init; } = X; }` writes out character for character what the positional parameter already produces, and it costs a reader the same question every time: is this one different? ⚠ It belongs disproportionately to generated code — \"declare the properties\" is what most of a model's training data does, and the positional record is newer than most of that data, so the two forms arrive together.\n\n⚠ **Not hosted, measured the same way as `SK0280` and `SK0281`**: absent from all 442 descriptors read out of SDK 10.0.400's analyzer assemblies, and silent in a probe with all 120 `IDE` ids and all 320 `CA` ids at `warning` on a compilation where `IDE0055`, `IDE0160`, `IDE0290`, `IDE0380` and `CA1051` fired and no analyzer crashed. `jb inspectcode` 2025.2.6 reports it on the same file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property in a record body that restates, initializer included, the one its positional parameter already generates."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The record writes out the property its parameter generates"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0282.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK0290",
              "name": "RedundantNullableCreation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The implicit conversion from `T` to `T?` is that constructor call, written out. Spelling it makes a reader ask what the wrapper is for, and the answer where the target type is written is nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`new int?(x)` or `new Nullable<int>(x)` written where the position already imposes `int?`, so the constructor call performs the same conversion the language performs implicitly."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The explicit `Nullable<T>` construction converts nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK0290.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Cleanup",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1001",
              "name": "CollectionExpression",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`List<string> names = new List<string> { \"a\" };` says `List<string>` twice, and the second one is the compiler's job. A collection expression takes its meaning from the target type, which here is the type the author already wrote. The rule is deliberately narrow because collection expressions are the opposite of the usual modernization: they have no natural type, so `[…]` is legal in some positions and not in others, and an analyzer that fires everywhere fires where it does not compile. It fires in the three positions where the type is spelled by the author and the reader can see it — a declaration with an explicit type, a `return` in a member whose return type is written, and an assignment to a plain name — and only where the created object's type is that type exactly."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A position whose type is already written out does not need the type written again in `new`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a collection expression"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "12.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1003",
              "name": "FieldBackedProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "C# 14's field keyword preserves custom accessor logic while letting the compiler own backing storage. The fix removes the private declaration and replaces only references proven to belong to this property."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private backing field is used exclusively inside one property's get and set accessors."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a field-backed property"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "14.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1004",
              "name": "UngroupedExtensionMethods",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The static-class-of-`this`-parameters idiom exists because C# had no way to say \"these members belong to `string`\". C# 14 has one. The block says the receiver once instead of once per member, and it is the only form that can carry an extension *property*, an extension *operator* or a static extension member — so a class in the old shape is not merely more verbose, it is a shape that cannot express the members somebody will want to add to it next. ⚠ **The receiver stops being a parameter and becomes the block's subject**, which is the whole readability argument: `public static bool IsBlank(this string value)` is a static method that happens to be callable with dot syntax, and `extension(string value) { public bool IsBlank => … }` is a member of `string` as far as every reader and every call site is concerned. Both call forms keep working — measured, not assumed: `\"x\".Repeat(2)` and `StringExt.Repeat(\"x\", 2)` both compile against a block on SDK 10.0.400, so the rewrite is source-compatible in both directions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `static` class whose every member is an extension method on one receiver is the pre-C# 14 spelling of a single `extension` block, and the block is where properties, operators and static members on that receiver can also go."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use an `extension` block"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "14.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1005",
              "name": "FileScopedNamespace",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "One level of indentation across every file in the repository, for no expressive gain. The configuration this tool serves already says `csharp_style_namespace_declarations = file_scoped`; the rule is what makes that true in code an agent wrote rather than only in code the IDE touched.\n\nReSharper has no inspection id for this: it drives the conversion from `csharp_style_namespace_declarations = file_scoped:suggestion` and reports the result under `resharper_arrange_namespace_body_highlighting = hint`. Two mechanisms, two severities, one concept — so this rule maps to no key at all. docs/plan/16 § Q5."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A file with exactly one block-scoped namespace and nothing beside it can use `namespace X;`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a file-scoped namespace"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6",
                "languageVersion": "10.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1006",
              "name": "UsingDeclaration",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `using` statement whose block is the rest of the scope is a level of indentation that buys nothing: the object is disposed at the closing brace either way. Written as a declaration it loses the nesting, and in a method that acquires three things it loses three levels of it. The rule fires only where the two closing braces are the same program point, because a `using` declaration disposes when its enclosing block ends and moving that point later is a behaviour change rather than a style one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `using` block that runs to the end of its enclosing block is a `using` declaration."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a `using` declaration"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1006.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1010",
              "name": "IsNullPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`x == null` calls whatever `operator ==` the type declares; `x is null` is a pattern and always tests reference (or default) equality. On a type with a user-defined `==` the two are different programs, and on a type without one they are the same program written two ways. Preferring the pattern removes the question. It also survives a later refactor that *adds* an `operator ==`, which is exactly when `== null` silently changes meaning."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A null comparison written with an operator, on a type that does not define one."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `is null` / `is not null` instead of `==` / `!=`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1011",
              "name": "PropertyPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A property pattern combines the receiver's non-null requirement and a member condition. Restricting this rewrite to one member retains getter evaluation count and avoids reordering getters. Stable, non-ref, uncaptured locals and parameters avoid replacing two observable receiver reads with one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A stable reference's null guard followed by one member equality can be expressed as a property pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a property pattern"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1011.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1012",
              "name": "ReturningSwitchExpression",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Switch expressions express a value selected from alternatives. This rule preserves evaluation count by requiring one stable input, unique constant patterns, an explicit fallback, and identity-converted results of one type. It does not guess at numeric common-type inference, boxing, target typing or custom conversions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A chain of constant equality tests returning values of the same type can use a switch expression."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a switch expression for a returning equality chain"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1012.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1013",
              "name": "ListPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A list pattern states the shape being tested without repeating the receiver and indices. Requiring a stable receiver, an explicit null guard, a proven exact length, and side-effect-free built-in element comparisons keeps the rewrite local. Untested positions become discards, not slices or allocations."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Null, fixed-length and constant element checks on an array or string can be combined into a list pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a list pattern for guarded element checks"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1013.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "11.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1014",
              "name": "RelationalPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Pattern combinators make the range being tested explicit. A safe rewrite must keep the value being tested stable, use built-in operators, and ensure each constant is representable in the input type. The rule does not assume repeated property or field reads are interchangeable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two constant-bound integral comparisons on the same stable variable can use an and/or pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use relational and logical patterns"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1014.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1015",
              "name": "TypePatternOverCast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`if (x is Widget) { var w = (Widget)x; … }` tests the type and then converts it again, and the second conversion cannot fail — the compiler emits an `isinst` and a `castclass` where one `isinst` would do. A declaration pattern is the same program with one type check, and it makes the relationship between the test and the variable something the reader no longer has to reconstruct."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type test whose block opens by casting the same expression to the same type is one pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `is T t` instead of `is T` and a cast"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1015.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1020",
              "name": "ArgumentNullThrowIfNull",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **Retired (#281).** `CA1510` is enabled by default at `Info` in a stock SDK build and reports all three of this rule's positive fixtures with nothing configured, which is the same visibility this rule shipped at (`suggestion`). ADR-008 is host, never rebuild, so the rule is withdrawn rather than kept as a second opinion. The id stays allocated for ever (ADR-012). The BCL helper is one line instead of three, captures the argument expression itself through `CallerArgumentExpression` so the message cannot drift from the parameter name, and is annotated so the nullable analysis knows the argument is non-null afterwards — which the hand-written form only manages when the `throw` is the whole `if` body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A hand-written null guard that `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull` already is."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1022",
              "name": "SearchValues",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "SearchValues precomputes a reusable search strategy. The fix changes existing cached storage rather than allocating SearchValues at each call, and requires available framework factory and search overloads."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private static readonly character array is used only by framework span search operations."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Precompute a constant character search set"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1022.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1023",
              "name": "DedicatedLock",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "C# 13 uses the dedicated Lock API when the lock target has that type. The fix changes both the field type and its object creation, retaining the existing lock statements. It is offered only when all source references are lock targets and the framework provides the Lock API."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private readonly object field used only by lock statements can use System.Threading.Lock."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a dedicated System.Threading.Lock"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1023.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "13.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1025",
              "name": "FrozenDictionary",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "FrozenDictionary optimizes immutable lookup tables. The fix retains the original Dictionary construction and its duplicate-key checks, then freezes it. Every source reference must remain compatible with read-only lookup semantics."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private static readonly Dictionary with constant entries is used only for lookups."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Freeze a private lookup-only dictionary"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1025.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1026",
              "name": "Utf8Literal",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A UTF-8 literal avoids the temporary array allocation and runtime encoding step. The consumer must already accept a read-only byte span, and speculative binding must select exactly the same constructed method after replacement. The rule does not convert APIs that need mutable arrays."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes of a constant ASCII string can use a UTF-8 literal when passed to an existing ReadOnlySpan<byte> parameter."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a UTF-8 literal for constant ASCII bytes"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1026.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "11.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1028",
              "name": "SpanDecoding",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Span<byte> and ReadOnlySpan<byte> already describe the contiguous bytes to decode. Calling their ToArray solely for GetString copies the bytes and allocates an intermediate array. The replacement is offered only when speculative binding selects the framework's span overload, preserving the existing span/slice computation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Encoding.UTF8.GetString can consume an existing byte span without a temporary ToArray allocation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Decode a byte span without an array copy"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1028.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1030",
              "name": "NullCoalescingAssignment",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The compound form says the intent once instead of naming the target twice, and it is the form that cannot drift — a rename that misses one of the two occurrences of `x` turns the long form into a silent bug."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`x = x ?? y` is `x ??= y`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `??=`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1031",
              "name": "NullConditionalAssignment",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "C# 14 allows a null-conditional expression on the left of an assignment, so the three-line guard around a single field or property write becomes one line that says the same thing. The rewrite is exact rather than nearly exact because the feature evaluates the right-hand side only when the receiver is non-null, which is what the `if` did."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `if` whose only job is to null-check the receiver of one assignment is `?.` in C# 14."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a null-conditional assignment"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "14.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1033",
              "name": "DictionaryDoubleLookup",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`if (map.ContainsKey(key)) { var value = map[key]; … }` computes the hash, walks the bucket, throws the result away and does all of it again. `TryGetValue` does it once, and `TryAdd` is the same saving for the insert. It is filed as modernization rather than performance because it reads as a style preference and is not one: in a loop it is half the lookups, and the shorter form is also the one that cannot go stale between the two statements."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`ContainsKey` followed by the indexer, or by `Add`, hashes the key twice to do one thing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `TryGetValue` or `TryAdd` instead of `ContainsKey` and a second lookup"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1033.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1034",
              "name": "CountPropertyOverLinq",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **Retired (#281).** `CA1860` and `CA1829` are both enabled by default at `Info` in a stock SDK build and between them report all four of this rule's positive fixtures with nothing configured — `CA1860` three, `CA1829` the `Count()` one — which is the same visibility this rule shipped at (`suggestion`). ADR-008 is host, never rebuild. The id stays allocated for ever (ADR-012). `Enumerable.Count()` has a fast path for `ICollection` and `Any()` allocates an enumerator; both are strictly more work than reading the property, and both hide from the reader that the length is already known. The value is legibility more than speed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`list.Count() > 0` and `list.Any()` on something that already has a `Count`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the `Count` property, not `Count()` or `Any()`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1034.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1035",
              "name": "GenericEnumGetvalues",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The non-generic overload returns `System.Array`, so every caller casts or enumerates as `object` and boxes each member. The generic overload is typed and allocation-free per element, and it is the same call."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Enum.GetValues(typeof(T))` returns `Array`; the generic overload returns `T[]`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `Enum.GetValues<T>()`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1035.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1040",
              "name": "NullableShortForm",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`T?` is the spelling every other nullable in the file already uses, so the long form reads as though it meant something different. It is also the form older samples and generated code produce, which is why an assistant writes it: the training data is full of `Nullable<int>` and the language stopped needing it in C# 2."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A bound `System.Nullable<T>` is written in long form where `T?` names exactly the same type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `T?` instead of `Nullable<T>`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1041",
              "name": "CompoundAssignment",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Naming the target twice is the shape a rename can half-finish: `total = subtotal + 1` compiles and is a different program, and nothing in the long form says which of the two occurrences was meant. The compound form cannot drift, and it is the form C# has had since version 1 — the long one is what a model writes because most of its training data predates the habit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An assignment whose right-hand side is a binary operation on the assignment target itself, where the compound operator says it once."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a compound assignment"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1042",
              "name": "MergeableIf",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The second `if` adds a level of nesting and no branch: there is no path where the outer condition holds and the inner one does not lead anywhere else. `&&` short-circuits, so the merged form evaluates exactly what the nested one did, in the same order — and it stops the nesting counting against the `SK7006` depth metric for a structure that never had two branches."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `if` whose entire body is another `if` with no `else` is one condition written as two."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The nested `if` statements can be combined"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1042.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1043",
              "name": "ForLoopIsWhile",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`for` promises a bound and then declares none, so a reader has to check both semicolons to find out there is nothing in them. The rewrite is exact rather than merely equivalent: with no incrementors, `continue` jumps to the condition in both forms, and nothing else in a `for` is being used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `for` with no initializer and no incrementor is a `while` written with two empty clauses."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `for` loop is a `while`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1043.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1044",
              "name": "NullOrEmptyCheck",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The hand-written form states the check twice and gets its order wrong easily — `x.Length == 0 || x == null` dereferences null before it tests for it. `string.IsNullOrEmpty` also carries `[NotNullWhen(false)]`, so the compiler's null analysis learns from the call what it cannot learn from the pair."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A null test on a string followed by an emptiness test on the same string, or the negation of both."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `string.IsNullOrEmpty`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1044.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1050",
              "name": "PatternMatchingOverTestAndCast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`var b = x as Binary; if (b != null)` converts, stores, and then asks whether the conversion worked — three steps for one question the language has had an answer to since C# 7. `x is Binary b` is one `isinst`, one variable, and a relationship the reader does not have to reconstruct from two adjacent statements. The same holds for `(x as T) != null`, which allocates a conversion result nobody wants in order to test a type, and for `!(x is T)`, which reads as the negation of a question rather than as the question the code is actually asking."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A conversion followed by a null check, a safe cast used only as a type test, or a negated type check is a pattern."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use pattern matching instead of a test-and-cast"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1050.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1051",
              "name": "SimplifiedPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Patterns compose, so they also accumulate. `SK1011` and `SK1014` introduce property and relational patterns and nothing tidies them afterwards, which is how a modernization rule creates its own debt: a `not` added to an existing `not` during a refactor, or a `not (> 5)` written because the condition it replaced was `!(x > 5)`. Neither survives being read aloud, and both have an exact spelling that does."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A run of `not` that cancels itself, or a `not` in front of a relational pattern that the operator already spells."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Simplify the pattern"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1051.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1052",
              "name": "MergedConditionalAccess",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`x != null ? x.Y : null` names the receiver three times to say one thing, and the thing it says is what `?.` was added to the language for. The rewrite also reads the receiver once instead of twice, which is the point at which it stops being cosmetic: two reads of the same path is a shape where a reader has to check that nothing changed in between, and the conditional access removes the question."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A conditional whose only job is to guard a member access against null is a `?.`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Merge the `?:` into a conditional access"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1052.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1053",
              "name": "DiscardOverUnreadLocal",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A name is a claim that something will be referred to again. When nothing refers to it, the name is a small lie the reader has to check: they look for the second use, do not find it, and only then know the call was there for its effect. `_` says that in the first character, and it says it to the compiler too — the local stops being allocated and stops being something a later edit can accidentally read."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A named local that exists only to receive a value nobody reads — a return value kept for the call's effect, or an `out var` the caller does not want."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a discard"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1053.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1054",
              "name": "InlineOutVariable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The inline form has been available since C# 7 and the separated form is still what a model writes, because most of its training data predates it. The separated form also states a lie for one statement: `int value;` claims a variable exists and is meaningful before anything has put a value in it, and a reader has to look down one line to find out that it does not. `SK1033` already reports the `TryGetValue` shape this most often appears in."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A bare local declaration whose only purpose is the `out` argument in the statement below it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Inline the `out` variable declaration"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1054.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1060",
              "name": "IndexFromEnd",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`items[items.Count - 1]` names the collection twice and subtracts, and the reader has to check that both names are the same collection before believing the expression means \"the last one\". `items[^1]` says it, names the receiver once, and cannot be written with the wrong collection on one side — the copy-paste defect this shape produces is exactly `a[b.Count - 1]`, which compiles and is wrong. The subtraction is also the arithmetic the compiler was going to emit anyway: `^1` lowers to `Count - 1`, so the rewrite costs nothing and removes the only place a person can get it wrong."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An element access whose index counts back from the collection's own `Count` or `Length` is an index-from-end expression."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use an index-from-end expression"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1060.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1061",
              "name": "NameofExpression",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`nameof` is the only spelling of a name that a rename touches. `typeof(Widget).Name` and `Colour.Red.ToString()` compute at run time what the compiler already knows, and a literal `\"count\"` handed to `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`'s `paramName` is a name the compiler was never asked about at all — so it survives the rename that makes it wrong, and the first person to see the mistake is whoever reads the exception. This is disproportionately a generated-code problem: a model writes the string because the pre-C# 6 dialect it learned had nothing else to write."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A name produced at run time — `typeof(T).Name`, an enum member's `ToString()` — or written as a literal in a position that is defined to hold an identifier."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `nameof`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1061.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "6.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1062",
              "name": "EscapeFreeStringLiteral",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Escape-dense literals — Windows paths, embedded JSON, regexes — are where a stray backslash changes the meaning of the string and nothing says so. `\"\\\\d+\\\\.\\\\d+\"` and `\"{\\\"id\\\":1}\"` have to be decoded by the reader before they can be checked against whatever they are supposed to match, and the decoding is where the mistake gets made. A raw string removes the escaping entirely, and the shortest correct delimiter is the one that says the content has no quotes to worry about."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An escaped or verbatim literal a raw string would write without escapes, a raw string whose delimiter is longer than it needs to be, or an escape sequence that is simply a character."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the string literal form that needs no escapes"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1062.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1063",
              "name": "InterpolatedStringForm",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`string.Format(\"{0} of {1}\", done, total)` makes the reader carry two numbers across a comma to find out what goes where, and the compiler will not tell them when the arguments are swapped. The interpolated form puts the value where it is printed. The two smaller shapes are the same argument applied to interpolation itself: `$\"{count.ToString()}\"` renders through a string nobody keeps, and `$\"a{\"b\"}c\"` is a hole holding something that was never a variable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `string.Format` whose holes are numbered, a `ToString()` inside an interpolation hole, or a string literal sitting in a hole instead of in the text."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the interpolation form that says what it means"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1063.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "6.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1064",
              "name": "UnsignedRightShift",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`(int)((uint)x >> n)` is three operations spelling one, and two of the three are the casts that sign extension bugs come out of — a reader has to check that the widths match before believing the round trip is lossless, and the compiler will not check it for them. `x >>> n` is the operation, at the operand's own width, with nothing to get wrong."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A cast to the unsigned type, a right shift, and a cast back is the unsigned right shift operator."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `>>>`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1064.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "11.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1070",
              "name": "TupleDeconstruction",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Deconstruction arrived with tuples in C# 7 and is the form the language was given the tuple for; a model writes the `Item1`/`Item2` reads because most of its training data is older than both. The longhand form also loses the one thing the tuple was carrying — that the pieces belong together — and `var a = t.Item1;` names the variable twice while naming what it holds not at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Consecutive `var` declarations read every element of one tuple in order, which is a deconstruction written out longhand."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Deconstruct the tuple instead of reading it element by element"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1070.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1071",
              "name": "WithExpressionCopy",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A copy that respells every member is a copy that silently drops the next member added to the type: the constructor call still compiles, still passes the new parameter *something*, and nobody looking at the copy site can see that a field stopped travelling. `with` is the shape that cannot go stale, because the members it does not name are the ones it copies."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A record is copied by calling its own constructor back with its own members, which is what `with` is for."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Copy the record with a `with` expression"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1071.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1072",
              "name": "RedundantSpreadElement",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SK1001` introduces collection expressions; this is the cleanup that follows one. An array built at the point of a spread lives for the length of one expression and exists only to be enumerated back into the collection that is being built beside it — the allocation, the copy and the `..` are all ceremony around a list of elements that could simply have been written there. A modernization rule without its cleanup family produces churn."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An array is created inside a collection expression only so that a spread element can take it apart again."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The spread of a freshly created array is its elements"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1072.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "12.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1073",
              "name": "CachedEmptyInstance",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`new EventArgs()` allocates an object with no state, on an event that may be raised in a loop, where `EventArgs.Empty` is already there. The three struct cases cost nothing at run time and are a readability finding instead: `Guid.Empty`, `TimeSpan.Zero` and `CancellationToken.None` say what the value *is*, where `new Guid()` says only how it was made. Every one of them is a name a model writes the long way round because the long way round is what most of its training data does."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A stateless framework value is constructed where the framework already holds one: `new EventArgs()` is `EventArgs.Empty`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the framework's cached instance"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1073.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1080",
              "name": "OfTypeOverFilterAndCast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`OfType<T>` is defined as \"test each element against `T` and yield the ones that pass, typed\" — which is exactly what a `Where` holding a type test followed by a `Cast<T>` does, written across two operators and two iterators. The long form reads as two decisions, so a reader has to hold the filter in mind while checking that the cast agrees with it; and the two can silently stop agreeing, at which point `Cast<T>` throws on an element the filter was supposed to have removed. It is in the modernization range rather than the performance one because the saving — one iterator object and one delegate hop per element — is the smaller half: `OfType<T>` names the operation, and naming it is what stops the two halves drifting apart. It is also a shape a model writes because most of its training data predates the habit of reaching for `OfType`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`xs.Where(x => x is T).Cast<T>()` is `xs.OfType<T>()`, which is the operator the chain was spelling out."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the LINQ operator that says what the chain means"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1080.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1081",
              "name": "RedundantSequenceCall",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Both shapes are a call whose result is provably the thing that went in. `Enumerable.Cast<T>` opens with `if (source is IEnumerable<T> typed) return typed;`, so on a receiver already typed `IEnumerable<T>` it is not a cheap call — it is a call that returns its own argument by reference. A materializer applied directly to another materializer's result is the same idea one step out: the inner collection is created, copied and dropped inside a single expression, where nothing else in the program can ever hold it. Neither is a matter of taste, which is why this reports at `warning` while the rest of the range suggests: the code says an operation is happening and no operation happens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Cast<T>` on a sequence that is already `IEnumerable<T>` returns the very same object, and a `ToList` whose only consumer is another `ToList` copies a copy."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Remove the LINQ or collection call that does nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1081.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1082",
              "name": "IndexerOverElementAt",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`ElementAt` exists so that a bare `IEnumerable<T>` can be indexed at all — it walks the sequence when it has to and reaches for `IList<T>` when it can. On a `List<T>` the second branch is the only one that ever runs, so the call is a generic entry point wrapped around the indexer the receiver already declares. The indexer is shorter, needs no `using System.Linq`, and says *the collection has positions* rather than *find me the element in that position*, which is the distinction that matters when the next reader asks whether the receiver is a sequence or a list."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`list.ElementAt(i)` is `list[i]`: the language has syntax for the operation the LINQ extension is standing in for."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the indexer"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1082.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1083",
              "name": "ForeachOverIndexedFor",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The index in this loop is not information. It is not printed, not compared, not passed anywhere and not used to reach a second collection — it exists to move a cursor the language already knows how to move. What the long form adds is three places to make an off-by-one error, in the bound, in the comparison operator and in the increment, and every one of them is silent. `foreach` deletes all three. It is a shape a model writes far more often than a person does, because the indexed loop is what most of the code written before `foreach` became the default looks like."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `for` over `0..Count` whose index is only ever used to index that one collection is a `foreach` written the long way."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `foreach` over an indexed `for`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1083.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1084",
              "name": "LoopFilterAsQuery",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The loop header says *what is visited* and the body says *what is done*, and a body that is nothing but a guard has put half of the first sentence into the second. Moving the guard to the sequence leaves each construct saying one thing, and it puts the condition next to the thing being filtered rather than one indent below the thing being processed. It is the smallest of this range's rewrites and the one most likely to be declined on taste, which is why it ships at `hint`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `foreach` whose entire body is one `if` is a filter and a loop wearing one construct; the filter can move to the sequence."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a LINQ operator for part of the loop"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1084.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1090",
              "name": "ComputedProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An auto-property that can only ever hold one compile-time constant is a constant with storage bolted to it: the type carries a field for it, every instance pays for the field, and every constructor writes the same value into every one of them. `=> expr` says the same thing with no field and no write, and the compiler folds the constant at each read. The `SK1xxx` premise applies directly — the auto-property-with-initializer shape is what most of the training data contains, because it is the shape that has been idiomatic longest."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A get-only auto-property is initialized to a compile-time constant and assigned nowhere else, so it is that constant."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a computed property instead of an auto-property holding a constant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1090.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "6.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1091",
              "name": "PrivateAutoProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `private` auto-property has no external contract to preserve: nothing outside the type can bind to it, so the property exists only to wrap a field the compiler generated anyway. What the wrapper costs is not the two accessor calls the JIT inlines away — it is that the field is invisible. `readonly` cannot be applied to it, `ref` cannot be taken of it, an `Interlocked` update cannot be written against it, and every one of those is a tool the author gave up without being asked. ⚠ **This is `SK1003` from the other side and the two cannot both fire on one declaration.** `SK1003` starts from a private field with a hand-written property over it and folds the field into the `field` keyword; this starts from a private auto-property with no field at all and turns it into one. `SK1003` requires two bodied accessors and a separate field declaration, neither of which an auto-property has."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `private` auto-property generates a backing field, a getter and a setter to do what a field does directly."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The private auto-property is a field"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1091.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1092",
              "name": "TupleLiteral",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`(string name, int age)` is a value type, allocates nothing, and carries element names the reference `Tuple` has no room for. This is squarely the `SK1xxx` premise: `Tuple` predates `ValueTuple` by six years, so a model writes the older dialect because the older dialect is most of what it has read."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local is initialized with `new Tuple<…>(…)` or `Tuple.Create(…)` and is only ever read element by element, so a tuple literal is the same value without the allocation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a tuple literal instead of constructing a `Tuple`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1092.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1093",
              "name": "CastInDeclaration",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`var handle = (IDisposable)new FileStream(path, FileMode.Open);` writes the type in the one place it reads least. The cast is not converting anything — the conversion is implicit and the compiler emits nothing for it — it is there purely to talk `var` into inferring a wider type. Moving the type to the left of the `=` states it once, at the place a reader looks for it, and removes an expression that looks like a runtime check and is not one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `var` declaration's initializer is a cast that only widens, so the type belongs in the declaration and the cast can go."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Express the cast in the declaration instead"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1093.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1094",
              "name": "NullableAnnotationSyntax",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ This rule exists because of the retirement. A codebase leaving ReSharper is a codebase that has to drop its `JetBrains.Annotations` dependency, and every `[CanBeNull]` in it is a nullability fact that either moves into `?` or is lost — silently, because the attribute stops meaning anything the moment nothing reads it. No other tool reports these, and after the switch-off nothing will. The compiler's own annotation is also strictly stronger: it is checked at every call site rather than by a tool the build does not run."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `JetBrains.Annotations` `[CanBeNull]` or `[NotNull]` states in an attribute what the nullable context can state in the type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use nullable reference type syntax instead of an annotation attribute"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1094.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1100",
              "name": "SingleUseTemporary",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`var result = Compute(); return result;` is an extract-variable that was never undone. The name adds a line and says nothing the expression did not, and a reader still has to check that nothing happens between the two lines before they can read them as one. ⚠ **The adjacency requirement is the whole safety argument rather than a narrowing of it.** Inlining a temporary is normally unsafe because it moves the evaluation point — everything between the declaration and the use would start running before the initializer instead of after it. With nothing in between there is no order to change, so the initializer may have any side effect it likes and the rewrite is still exact. That is what lets this ship as a fix applicable without review, rather than as the \"must require a pure initializer\" rule the issue proposed. ⚠ **Nothing in the .NET SDK reports this shape.** `IDE0059` is the neighbouring diagnostic and its subject is a value that is *overwritten or never read*; measured on a `net10.0` probe with `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true` and every code-style severity raised, `var t = Compute(); return t;` drew `IDE0008` and nothing else."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local is initialised and the very next statement hands it straight back, so the name stands for an expression that is used once, immediately."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local is a name for the expression on the next line"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1100.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1101",
              "name": "SplitDeclarationAndAssignment",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`int count; count = Read();` is the C-era habit that a model has read a great deal of, and it costs a reader the one guarantee the joined form gives for free: that the variable is never observable before it holds something. The rewrite is the smallest in this batch — two lines become one, no expression moves and no scope changes. ⚠ **Nothing in the .NET SDK reports it.** Measured on a `net10.0` probe with `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`, `AnalysisMode=All` and every code-style diagnostic raised to `warning`, `int x; x = 3; return x;` produced `IDE0130` and `CA1822` and nothing about the split. ⚠ **It needs no semantic model, and that is a fact about C# lookup rather than a shortcut**: an identifier written immediately after `T x;` in the same statement list resolves to that local, because a simple name finds the innermost enclosing declaration and nothing can be declared between two adjacent statements. So the rule runs under `--load=loose`, which is the mode an agent's scratch file is analysed in and exactly where this shape arrives."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local declared without an initializer is assigned by the very next statement, so two lines carry one declaration."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local is declared on one line and assigned on the next"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1101.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1102",
              "name": "LocalFunctionBeforeJump",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A local function declaration is not an executable statement, so the jump is the last thing the block does no matter where it is written. Moving the jump above the run puts the page in the order the program is in. ⚠ **Hoisting is what makes the move sound, and it is a stronger fact than \"the jump is last\"**: a local function may be called from above its own declaration, so `return Inner();` moved above `int Inner() => 7;` still binds. ⚠ **This is the only rewrite in the batch that cannot produce `CS0136`, because it introduces and removes no name at all** — nothing but the jump statement changes position, and a jump declares nothing. ⚠ **Nothing in the .NET SDK reports the shape.** On the same `net10.0` probe with every code-style diagnostic raised, a method holding a local function before its `return` drew `IDE0062`, `IDE0061` and `IDE0060` — all about the function, none about where it sits."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A block whose last statement is a jump has one or more local function declarations written directly above it, so the declarations read as work done before the block is left."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local function is declared before the jump that ends the block"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1102.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1103",
              "name": "SharedBranchTail",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Duplication inside one member is where it is least visible and most likely to drift, because the two copies sit close enough to look like one. Moving the shared tail below the `if` states it once and leaves each branch holding only what actually differs. ⚠ **An early jump inside a branch is safe, and working that out is what let the rule ship.** The worry is `if (c) { if (d) break; Log(); } else { Other(); Log(); }` — the hoisted `Log()` looks as though it becomes reachable on the `break` path. It does not: the statement lands directly after the `if`, and every control transfer that skipped it inside the branch — `return`, `throw`, `break`, `continue`, `goto`, `yield break` — skips the position after the `if` exactly as it skipped the position before it. That is why no jump appears in the guards. ⚠ **Nothing in the .NET SDK reports the shape**, measured on the same `net10.0` probe: shared trailing statements in `if`/`else` drew only `CA1822`, `CA1002` and `CA1062`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `if` and the `else` block end with the same run of statements, so the shared tail is written twice and can drift apart in one place only."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Both branches end with the same statements"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1103.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1110",
              "name": "ConstantForwardingOverload",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two declarations, two doc comments and two places to change say one thing: the last argument has a default. The optional parameter says it once, in the signature, where a reader looking at the method finds it — and where the caller's tooling shows it, which the forwarding overload never does. It is also a shape a model produces constantly, because optional parameters postdate a great deal of the C# in its training data and writing the second overload is what the older dialect required."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A non-public overload whose whole body forwards to a longer one, passing its own parameters straight through and a constant for the extra, is an optional parameter written as a second method."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Collapse the forwarding overload into an optional parameter"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1110.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1120",
              "name": "ReflectiveTypeTest",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `is` operator is one IL instruction, and more to the point it is the spelling every C# reader recognises and the one that composes with a pattern — `x is T t` binds the value that the reflection call would need a separate, unchecked cast for. ⚠ **Nothing in the shipped toolchain reports either shape**, measured rather than assumed: compiled at `AnalysisMode=All`, `AnalysisLevel=latest-all` and `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`, with the whole `Style` and `CodeQuality` categories raised to `warning`, both shapes produce no `IDE*` and no `CA*` diagnostic. ⚠ **The instrument was verified in the same build rather than trusted**: planted shapes produce `IDE0059` and `IDE0090`, so the zero beside them is a zero from clean analysis and not from analysis that never ran — and at the default `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true` alone, *no* `IDE*` rule appears at all, because they ship enabled and `Hidden`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`typeof(T).IsInstanceOfType(x)` and `typeof(T).IsAssignableFrom(x.GetType())` both spell `x is T` as a `Type` load and a virtual call."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A reflection call asks what `is` answers"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1120.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1121",
              "name": "MergeableTry",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The nested form arrives by accretion — a `finally` added around a statement that already handled its own errors — and it hides which handler covers what: a reader has to hold two protected regions in mind to answer a question about one. One `try` with its handlers and its `finally` side by side says the same thing in the shape the language has for it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An outer `try`/`finally` whose block holds nothing but a `try`/`catch` is one `try` statement."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `try`/`catch` is the whole body of a `try`/`finally`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1121.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1122",
              "name": "ReorderedAnonymousType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Anonymous types unify on the *ordered* list of member names and types, so a pair that differs only in order is a pair of types. Nothing at the point of use says so: both are `var`, both print the same members, and the cost appears later as a dictionary with two key types, a `Distinct` that removes nothing, a `Union` that concatenates, and an assignment that does not compile for a reason the error message states in terms of two generated names."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two anonymous object creations in one member with the same member names and types but a different order, which makes them two runtime types."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A nearby anonymous type has the same members in another order"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1122.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1123",
              "name": "MergedPropertyPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`{ A: p or q }` is the same predicate with the subject written once, and it is the form the property extends to — a third alternative adds a word rather than a clause. It is also the shape a reader can check: two property patterns side by side have to be compared member by member before it is clear that only the value differs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`x is { A: p } or { A: q }` asks one question about one property and spells it as two questions about two objects."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two `or` alternatives test the same property"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1123.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1130",
              "name": "ConstantPatternOverSequenceEqual",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "C# 11 added the one thing that made spans awkward to read — a `ReadOnlySpan<char>` or `Span<char>` can be matched against a constant string pattern, and the compiler lowers that to the very `MemoryExtensions.SequenceEqual` call the source was already making. So the call and the pattern are the same program, and only one of them composes: a pattern goes into a `switch` arm, joins an `or`, and negates as `is not` without the reader having to find the `!` in front of a long receiver. `bare.SequenceEqual(\"world\") || bare.SequenceEqual(\"worldViewProjection\")` is two calls where `bare is \"world\" or \"worldViewProjection\"` is one question."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`span.SequenceEqual(\"abc\")` is `span is \"abc\"`: C# 11 matches a `ReadOnlySpan<char>` against a constant string directly."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A character span is compared to a constant by a call"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1130.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "11.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1131",
              "name": "AnonymousMethodWithParameterList",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The anonymous method is C# 2.0 syntax that C# 3.0 replaced with the lambda, and twenty years of event subscriptions, `List<T>.Sort` comparisons, `ThreadStart` bodies and timer callbacks still carry it — which is exactly the material a model writes from. Nothing about it is wrong, but it is a second spelling of one concept, and it is the spelling every other tool and every newer feature has moved away from: `static`, `async`, attributes on parameters, default values and natural types all arrived for lambdas first or only. Rewriting it keeps the parameter list and the block verbatim and changes two tokens, so the reader sees the dialect the rest of the file is written in."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`delegate(int a, int b) { … }` is `(int a, int b) => { … }`: the C# 2 anonymous method with an explicit parameter list is the same program as a statement-bodied lambda."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An anonymous method is written with the `delegate` keyword"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1131.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1132",
              "name": "TupleElementByPosition",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A model that has just written `(int Count, string Name)` and consumes the value a few lines later falls back on `Item1`/`Item2`, the `System.Tuple`-era spelling that dominates its training data. `r.Item1` compiles, is correct, and tells the reader nothing, while the name that would have told them sits in the signature. The element's name and `ItemN` are two fields over one storage slot of one `ValueTuple`, so the fix is a rename of one written token and changes no program."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`t.Item1` where the tuple's first element is called `Count`: the name that would tell the reader what the value is sits in the type and is not used."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A named tuple element is reached by its position"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1132.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK1133",
              "name": "CollectionExpressionSpread",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`int[] ids = source.ToArray();` names the array type twice — once in the declaration and once in the method. A collection expression takes its type from the position, so the copy says only what it copies. It is the fluent half of what `SK1001` does for `new T[] { … }`, and the shape a model writes because `.ToArray()` is what most of its training data does. The fix writes `[..xs]` with no space after the `..`: the formatter keeps a spread exactly as it is written (SK-DIV-0009), so that is the spelling that stays. Parentheses around the receiver are dropped, because a spread's operand is a whole expression and none was found that needs them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`xs.ToArray()` or `xs.ToList()`, where the type it returns is the type the position already names, is `[..xs]`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A framework copy into the written type is a spread"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK1133.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Modernization",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "14.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2001",
              "name": "ConstantRangeComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Fixed-width integral types cannot represent values outside their declared ranges. Testing an endpoint can therefore be redundant even where the compiler does not issue CS0652. This initial rule uses exact ranges rather than treating nullable annotations as runtime guarantees. It reports without removing expressions that may throw or have side effects."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A relational comparison against an integral constant is always true or false over the operand type's complete range."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Comparison is fixed by the integral type's range"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2002",
              "name": "DiscardedPureResult",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Pure calls do not update their receiver. Discarding the returned string or calculation often leaves the input unchanged where a normalization or assignment was intended."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A bare call discards the result of a method marked System.Diagnostics.Contracts.Pure or a known immutable string transformation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A pure method's result is discarded"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2003",
              "name": "FloatingPointEquality",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Binary floating-point arithmetic rounds its results. A mathematical equality can therefore differ from exact machine equality. An appropriate tolerance depends on the domain and scale; the rule reports the comparison without inventing an epsilon or replacing it with Equals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Exact equality or inequality directly compares a computed float or double arithmetic result."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review exact equality of floating-point arithmetic"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2004",
              "name": "IncompleteEqualityContract",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Typed and object-based consumers can otherwise disagree about equality. A class can compare equal through `IEquatable<T>` while its object equality and hash code still use identity; structs can similarly diverge from their fallback field comparison."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type implements `IEquatable<Self>` without overriding Equals(object)."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Typed equality has no matching object equality"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2005",
              "name": "ReadonlyStructMutation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Calling a mutating struct method through a readonly field can modify a defensive copy and discard that change. The rule requires same-file evidence of a direct instance-field assignment or increment, rather than treating every non-readonly method as mutating."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A void struct method that directly writes an instance field is called through readonly field storage."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not mutate a copy of a readonly struct field"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2007",
              "name": "CollectionModifiedDuringEnumeration",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The BCL collections carry a version counter, and `MoveNext` throws `InvalidOperationException` the moment it sees the version move. Because the loop the rule reports has no `break`, no `return` and no `throw`, the enumerator is guaranteed to be advanced at least once after the mutation — including past the last element, where `MoveNext` still runs to return `false`. So this is not a risk that a particular input might hit: it is a throw on every execution that reaches the mutation. The standard repair is to enumerate a snapshot, which is what the fix writes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `foreach` over a `List<T>`, `Dictionary<,>` or `HashSet<T>` whose body adds to or removes from the same collection."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection being enumerated is modified inside the loop"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2008",
              "name": "CapturedLoopVariable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Closures capture a variable rather than the value it had when the delegate was created. Delegates retained by the list can therefore all see the loop's final value instead of one value per iteration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A delegate added to a persistent `List<TDelegate>` captures a variable declared and incremented by an enclosing for loop."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A stored delegate captures a changing loop variable"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2008.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2009",
              "name": "EnumSwitchMissingMembers",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A switch that silently falls through when a new enum member is added turns a closed decision into an accidental default. Listing every value makes the compiler-visible decision explicit and makes future additions show up at the switch that needs a policy."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A switch over a non-flags enum has neither every declared value nor a catch-all arm."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An enum switch omits declared members"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2009.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2010",
              "name": "ImplicitStringCulture",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ambient culture can change comparison outcomes across machines or requests. The correct policy may be linguistic or ordinal; spelling that policy explicitly keeps it independent of an accidental process setting."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A string.Compare overload omits a culture/comparison argument, or parameterless ToLower/ToUpper is used directly in equality."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A string comparison has an implicit culture policy"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2011",
              "name": "InheritedValueTypeEquals",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The inherited implementation uses object-based fallback comparison and can require boxing. Implementing a deliberate equality contract gives collections and direct comparisons a consistent typed path."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A call on a concrete struct binds to System.ValueType.Equals(object) instead of a typed or overridden equality method."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A struct uses inherited ValueType.Equals"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2011.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2012",
              "name": "SelfAssignmentOrComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The compiler already reports many field/local self-operations but leaves property spellings unchecked. Arbitrary getters or setters can have deliberate behavior, so this rule requires a non-virtual source auto-property and a stable identical receiver. A report asks for review rather than deleting an operation or inventing the intended target."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The same known automatic property is used on both sides of an assignment or built-in comparison."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review a self-operation on an automatic property"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2012.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2013",
              "name": "ExceptionConstructedNotThrown",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Writing `new InvalidOperationException(\"...\");` where `throw new InvalidOperationException(\"...\");` was meant is a guard clause that does nothing. The method continues on the path the author intended to abort, and the failure surfaces later somewhere unrelated. There is no legitimate reason to construct an exception as a standalone statement: an exception object that is neither thrown, returned, assigned nor passed anywhere has no observable effect beyond its own allocation. The fix is the missing keyword."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`new SomeException(...)` is an expression statement, so the exception is allocated and thrown away."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exception is constructed and then discarded"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2013.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2014",
              "name": "EmptyCatchSwallowsException",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An empty catch converts an exceptional path into apparent success and erases the only evidence of the failure. If ignoring an exception is deliberate, the code should say why; otherwise it should handle, log or propagate it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A filterless catch block contains no statement or explanation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An empty catch silently discards an exception"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2014.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2015",
              "name": "RethrowLosesStackTrace",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`throw ex;` inside `catch (Exception ex)` assigns a fresh stack trace to the exception, so every frame between the original throw site and this `catch` is erased. The result is a production log that points at the handler rather than at the fault, which is the one piece of information the log existed to carry. `throw;` re-throws the same exception with its trace intact. The two forms look interchangeable and are not, which is why this is worth a rule rather than a review comment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Re-throwing the caught exception by name restarts its stack trace at the `throw`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`throw ex;` resets the stack trace"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2015.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2016",
              "name": "InterpolatedLoggerMessage",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Interpolating before the logger sees the message eagerly renders values and discards their property names. A stable template preserves structured fields for filtering and aggregation and avoids rendering work when the log level is disabled."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Microsoft.Extensions.Logging call receives an interpolated message instead of a stable message template."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A logger message is interpolated before it is logged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2016.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2017",
              "name": "WrongArgumentNameInException",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`paramName` is an ordinary string, so `throw new ArgumentNullException(\"vlaue\")` compiles and ships. What it produces at runtime is a message, an `ArgumentException.ParamName` and a log line all naming a parameter the method does not have, which sends the reader of the failure to the wrong argument. The same slip is easier still on the rest of the family, where `paramName` is positional and sits next to the message. `nameof` is the repair: it is the same string, checked by the compiler, and it cannot go stale under a rename."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `ArgumentException`-family constructor is handed a `paramName` string literal that names no parameter in scope, and exactly one parameter in scope is one edit away from it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The exception names a parameter that does not exist"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2017.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2030",
              "name": "NanComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "NaN is unordered with everything including itself, so `x == double.NaN` is false even when x is NaN and `x != double.NaN` is true even when it is. The check written to catch the bad case is the shape that cannot catch it. `double.IsNaN` is the operation that was meant, and the rewrite is mechanical."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A floating-point value is compared with == or != against a constant NaN, which is the one comparison guaranteed never to answer the question it was written to ask."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Detect NaN with IsNaN rather than equality"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2031",
              "name": "UnusedValueParameter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The setter's whole contract is to consume `value`. One that runs statements and never reads it stores something else, or stores nothing, and the caller has no way to see that the write went nowhere. The repair is a decision about what the setter was meant to store, so the rule reports and does not rewrite."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property, indexer or init accessor body does work and never mentions `value`, so every assignment through it is discarded while appearing to succeed."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not discard a setter's value parameter"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2032",
              "name": "RedundantSuppressFinalize",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The call is copied out of the canonical Dispose pattern into types that never had a finalizer. It costs nothing at runtime and reads as though the type has one, which sends the next reader looking for a finalizer that is not there. Deleting the statement is the whole repair."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` is called from a sealed class that declares no finalizer and derives directly from object, so there is no finalizer anywhere to suppress."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Remove GC.SuppressFinalize from a type with no finalizer"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2032.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2033",
              "name": "StackallocInLoop",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Stack memory allocated in a method is reclaimed when that method returns, not when the block ends. A `stackalloc` a loop repeats therefore grows the frame by an amount the loop bound decides, and the overflow it eventually causes is a process kill with no catchable exception and no diagnostic naming this line. No fix is offered: hoisting the buffer out of the loop changes its lifetime and whether iterations share it, which is a decision about the program rather than a rewrite of it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `stackalloc` sits in a loop body, condition or incrementor, so the frame grows by one buffer per iteration and none of them is released until the method returns."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not stackalloc inside a loop"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2033.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2034",
              "name": "EscapedKeyword",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`@class`, `@new` and `@event` are legal and cost the reader a stop every time. The `@` on a reserved keyword is never a disambiguation the language asked for; it is the only spelling available once the name was chosen, so the finding is about the name. No fix is offered: renaming a declaration means renaming every reference, which is a refactoring rather than a text edit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A declaration is named after a reserved C# keyword and can only be written with an `@` escape, so every mention of it reads as a verbatim string, a typo or a keyword until the reader checks."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not name a declaration after a reserved keyword"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2034.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2040",
              "name": "UnintendedReferenceComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type that overrides `Equals(object)` has written down what equality means for it, and `==` on that type does not use it: with no `operator ==` anywhere on the chain the comparison falls back to reference identity, so two instances the type itself calls equal compare `false`. It does not fail, which is why it survives review and outlives the test suite."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`==` on a source class that defines value equality and declares no `operator ==`, so the comparison is by reference and cannot mean what it says."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The comparison is by reference where value equality was meant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2041",
              "name": "BaseEqualityCallIsIdentity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The override exists to replace identity with value semantics, and `base` is identity — the one thing it was written to stop doing. `base.GetHashCode()` inside `GetHashCode` returns the runtime's per-instance number, so two equal objects hash differently and the type is unusable as a dictionary key however carefully `Equals` was written. `base.Equals(other)` inside `Equals` returns reference equality, so the override answers the question it was overriding. It is a copy-paste result and a generated-stub result, and it reads as diligence."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An equality member calls `base.Equals` or `base.GetHashCode` where the base is `System.Object`, so the delegation it was written to perform returns reference identity and the identity hash."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`base.Equals` or `base.GetHashCode` resolves to `object`'s"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2042",
              "name": "HashCodeOverUncomparedMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The hash code contract runs in one direction only: equal objects must hash the same. A hash computed over a member that equality ignores breaks it head-on — two objects differing only in that member are equal and land in different buckets, so the second one inserted into a dictionary hides neither and finds neither, `Remove` returns `false` while `Count` still counts it, and a freshly built key never matches the entry it names. ⚠ **The reverse direction is not a defect and is deliberately not reported.** An `Equals` that compares more members than the hash reads only makes the hash weaker: every equal pair still hashes the same, which is all the contract asks. That direction is a collision-quality observation, and shipping it as a correctness finding would be one more rule that is usually right."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`GetHashCode` reads an instance member that none of the type's `Equals` methods compares, so two instances the type calls equal can hash differently."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The hash code reads state that equality ignores"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2042.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2043",
              "name": "MutableHashCodeMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An object whose hash changes after it was stored is lost inside its own dictionary. The bucket was chosen with the old hash and every lookup computes the new one, so the entry is present and unreachable: `Remove` returns `false`, `Count` still counts it, `ContainsKey` says no and enumeration says yes. Nothing throws and nothing logs, and the report that comes back is that an item \"disappeared\" — which sends the reader to the collection rather than to the key."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`GetHashCode` on a class reads an equality-relevant instance member that can still be assigned after construction."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The hash code depends on state that can change"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2043.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2044",
              "name": "InconsistentEqualityMembers",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each is one decision written down once and not the second time, and each leaves two spellings of the same question giving different answers. A typed `Equals(Self)` that no interface advertises is invisible to `EqualityComparer<T>.Default`, to `Dictionary<Self, …>` and to every `where T : IEquatable<T>` constraint, so the type is compared through the object overload it was written to avoid — boxing on a struct, and identity on a class that has no override. A type that defines `==` and also orders itself, without `<` and `>`, offers half an operator set and sends the reader to `CompareTo` to find out whether the other half exists."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type declares one half of an equality or ordering contract and not the other: `Equals(Self)` with no `IEquatable<Self>`, or `==` and `IComparable` with no relational operators."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The equality members are inconsistent with each other"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2044.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2050",
              "name": "IntegerDivisionFraction",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Asking for a fractional type is the author saying the fraction matters, and integral division has already thrown it away by the time the conversion runs. The truncation happens inside the operator, not at the assignment, so nothing in the surrounding code shows it. ⚠ The finding is independent of the checked context: no overflow is involved and the result is the same whether the project sets CheckForOverflowUnderflow or not. The fix casts the dividend to the fractional type, which changes the computed value — that is the point, and it is why the fix is not safe to apply unreviewed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A division evaluated in an integral type is converted to float, double or decimal, so the fractional part is discarded before the conversion that asks for it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Integer division feeds a fractional result"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2050.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2051",
              "name": "FixedResultArithmetic",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`x * 1`, `x | 0` and `x & 0` compute nothing. Written by hand they are usually the residue of an edit that removed the interesting operand, and written by a model they are usually a placeholder that was never filled in — which is why the range matters more for generated code than for code somebody typed. ⚠ Every case reported here holds identically in a checked and an unchecked context: an identity cannot overflow and a constant result cannot either, so the project's CheckForOverflowUnderflow setting never changes the answer."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A built-in integral arithmetic or bitwise operation has a constant operand that makes the operation either the identity or a constant."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The arithmetic has a result its constant operand already fixes"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2051.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2052",
              "name": "MaskedShiftCount",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ C# masks a shift count, it does not clamp it: `x << 32` on an `int` shifts by `32 & 31`, which is zero, so the expression is the identity rather than the zero almost everybody expects. On a `long` the mask is 63, so the same text is a genuine 32-bit shift. The rule therefore has to know the promoted width of the left operand and cannot be decided from syntax — a `byte` left operand promotes to `int` and masks with 31. There is no fix: rewriting `x << 33` to `x << 1` preserves the defect while making it look deliberate, and widening the operand guesses an intent the rule cannot read."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A shift's constant count is outside the range of its promoted left operand's width, so C# masks it and the expression shifts by a different amount."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The shift count is masked to a different count"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2052.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2053",
              "name": "NonnegativeSizeComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`items.Count >= 0` is true for every value the framework can produce and `items.Count < 0` is true for none, so a guard written that way admits everything or nothing — and it is almost always a `> 0` that was typed wrong. ⚠ This is not SK2001. SK2001 folds a comparison the operand *type's* range decides, and a count is an `int`, whose range decides nothing at all about zero; the extra fact here is the framework contract that a count is never negative, which narrows the lower bound from `int.MinValue` to `0`. There is no fix, for the same reason SK2001 has none: the rule can prove the comparison is decided and cannot know whether the intended bound was `0` or `1`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A collection count or a length is compared against a constant that the count's own non-negativity already decides, so the comparison is always true or always false."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The size comparison is decided by a count never being negative"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2053.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2054",
              "name": "SignedModulusEquality",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ C# gives `%` the sign of the dividend: `-5 % 2` is `-1`, not `1`. So `x % 2 == 1` is false for every negative odd `x`, and the parity check that reads as obviously correct is wrong on half its domain. ⚠ `x % 2 == 0` is **not** reported and must never be: zero has no sign, so comparing against it is correct for both signs — a rule that reported it would be wrong about the one spelling that is right. There is no fix, because the repair depends on what was meant: `!= 0` for a parity test, `Math.Abs(x % n) == k` for a magnitude test, and `x % n == k || x % n == -k` where both signs are wanted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `%` on a possibly-negative signed operand is compared with `==` or `!=` against a non-zero constant, so the test fails for every value of the wrong sign."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A signed modulus result is compared for equality with a non-zero value"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2054.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2060",
              "name": "AssignmentInCondition",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`if (x = y)` compiles only where the assigned type is `bool`, which is exactly why the mistake survives the compiler and why the rule can exist at all. The branch then runs whenever `y` is true, and the value the author meant to compare against is silently overwritten. ⚠ **The signal is that the assignment is the *entire* condition.** `while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)` assigns inside a condition too, and is correct and common; there the assignment is an operand of a comparison, not the condition itself. Wrapping the assignment in its own parentheses — `if ((ok = TryLoad()))` — is the convention C programmers have used for forty years to say the assignment was meant, and the rule honours it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The whole condition of an `if`, `while`, `do` or `for` is a simple assignment, so the condition stores a value and then tests what it stored rather than comparing anything."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The condition is an assignment, not a comparison"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2060.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2061",
              "name": "IdenticalOperands",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`a && a`, `offset - offset`, `bits ^ bits` — one side was meant to name something else and a copy-paste left it naming the first. The result is a constant the compiler is happy to fold, and the check that was written to catch a case cannot catch it. ⚠ **The six comparison operators are not examined, and measuring rather than remembering is what settled that.** `csc` reports `q == q`, `q < q`, `this.g == this.g`, `b.v == b.v`, `Box.Which == Box.Which` and `a == a` on a string as `CS1718`, on by default; the only comparison shape it leaves silent is a property access, and `SK2012` already owns that. There was nothing left over, so the operators went. ⚠ **The rule must still know the operand's type, which is why it is semantic.** `x - x` on a `double` is a NaN-preserving zero, not a constant nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Both operands of `&&`, `||`, `&`, `|`, `^`, `-`, `/` or `%` are the same side-effect-free expression, so the operator answers a question about nothing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Both operands of the operator are the same expression"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2061.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2062",
              "name": "RepeatedCondition",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In an `if`/`else if` chain nothing at all runs between one condition and the next: reaching the second means the first was false, so a second condition identical to the first is false too and its body is dead. No compiler reports it, because the code is perfectly well formed — it simply does something other than what it looks like. ⚠ **The comparison is structural, not textual.** `if (a && b)` and `if (b && a)` are different conditions and neither is reported for the other; two conditions that merely share a sub-expression — `if (x.Kind == A) … else if (x.Kind == B)` — are different conditions too. Only a whole condition equal to a whole earlier condition, token for token and ignoring layout, is a finding."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One `if`/`else if` chain tests the same side-effect-free condition twice, so the later branch is unreachable and the case it was written for is never handled."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A later condition in the chain repeats an earlier one"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2062.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2063",
              "name": "MisleadingOperatorSequence",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`x =- 1` assigns negative one. `x -= 1` subtracts one. C# has no `=-` operator, so the compiler splits the two characters silently and the reader's eye does not. ⚠ **The whitespace is the entire signal, and it is the only signal there is.** `x = -1` and `x =- 1` are the same program and differ only in layout, so nothing semantic can separate the deliberate from the mistyped; what separates them is that somebody wrote the `=` and the sign as one token and then put a space before the number, which is how `-=` is spelled and not how a negative literal is. A rule that ignored trivia here would have nothing left to look at."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `=` is written hard against a following unary `-`, `+` or `!` and separated from the operand — `x =- 1` — so the pair reads as the compound operator `-=` and is not one."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The operator sequence reads as a different operator"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2063.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2064",
              "name": "NonShortCircuitBoolean",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`if (node != null & node.Ready)` evaluates `node.Ready` whichever way the null check went, so the check protects nothing and the `NullReferenceException` it was written to prevent is thrown anyway. One character separates it from the code that works. ⚠ **The rule is semantic because the operand type decides everything.** `&` and `|` on integers and on `[Flags]` enums are the *only* way to write those operations, and reporting `flags & Mask` would be a catastrophe rather than a false positive; the rule therefore fires only where both operands are non-nullable `bool` and the operator is the language's own, never a user-defined one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `&` or `|` joins two `bool` operands whose right side has no side effect, so the non-short-circuiting operator evaluates a guard's right half that the guard exists to skip."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use && or || rather than & or | on booleans"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2064.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2070",
              "name": "LogTemplateArgumentCount",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The rendered event is wrong in both directions and neither direction throws. A hole with no value is emitted as the literal text `{OrderId}`, so the field a dashboard queries is never populated and the message reads as a bug report about itself; a value with no hole is attached under a fabricated positional key nobody queries, so the data is present, unfindable and paid for. ⚠ **The rule is deliberately Serilog-only.** `CA2017` already covers `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` — measured against `LoggerExtensions`, `ILogger.BeginScope` and `LoggerMessage.Define`, handling `{{` escapes, `{X,10:N2}` alignment and declining a non-implicit `params` array — and unlike `CA2241`, `CA2253` and `CA2254` it is **on by default**, with no `AnalysisMode` required. ADR-008 hosts `CA*` rather than rebuilding them, so what Skala implements is the half with no host at all. Serilog is where the concept is unowned, and it is also where the shape is commonest, because Serilog's own API encourages templates far richer than `ILogger`'s."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Serilog logging call's constant message template declares a number of holes that is not the number of values the call passes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Serilog message template has a different number of holes than the call supplies values"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2070.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2071",
              "name": "LogTemplateDuplicateProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The rendered line looks right, which is what makes this one survive review. The structured event is the part that is wrong: one key cannot hold two values, so a sink keeps one of them and the other is gone, and which one survives is the sink's business rather than the author's. The field a dashboard filters on is then silently either the first value or the last. ⚠ **Nothing hosts this, for any logging framework.** `CA2017` counts *holes* against arguments, so `logger.LogInformation(\"{X} then {X}\", a, b)` is arity-correct and it stays silent — measured in a probe project, at default analysis level and at `AnalysisMode=All`, rather than read from documentation. That is why this rule covers `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` as well as Serilog, where `SK2070` deliberately does not: the concept has no host anywhere, so ADR-008 has nothing to defer to. There is no fix, because the second property's name is the thing the author has to choose and no edit can choose it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A logging call's constant message template uses one property name for more than one hole, so the structured event cannot carry both values."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The structured log template names the same property twice"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2071.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2072",
              "name": "InvisibleCharacterInLiteral",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A zero-width space, a non-breaking space or a right-to-left override pasted into a literal is invisible in the editor, in the diff and in the review, and it changes what the program does — a key that never matches, a header that never parses, a path that is not the path anybody read. The escape sequence is the same value written so a person can see it, which is why the fix is mechanical and safe. ⚠ **Two classes of character, one severity, and that is a decision rather than an oversight.** A right-to-left override is the \"Trojan Source\" class — source that reads as one program and compiles as another — and a stray non-breaking space is a typo. The message says which one it found; the severity does not, because splitting them would spend a second permanent id (ADR-012) to encode a ranking, and a repository that wants the harder line writes `dotnet_diagnostic.SK2072.severity = error` without needing one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A string or character literal holds a zero-width, bidirectional or control character written as itself rather than as an escape sequence."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The literal contains an unescaped invisible character"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2072.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2073",
              "name": "CaughtExceptionNotLogged",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every one of these APIs takes the exception in its own parameter, and that parameter is what a sink treats as an exception: it is where the stack trace, the inner chain and the type end up as structure rather than as a sentence in a message. Left empty, the entry says something went wrong and carries nothing anybody can act on, which is the whole reason the line was written. ⚠ **This is `SK7xxx` on the issue and `SK2xxx` here.** An entry that cannot be diagnosed from is a defect in the program's observable behaviour rather than a maintenance cost, and the same argument that puts `SK2014` — a `catch` that swallows without logging or rethrow — in the correctness band puts this next to it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An error-level logging call inside a catch clause does not give the logger the exception the clause caught, although the overload that takes one exists."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The caught exception is not passed to the logger's exception parameter"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2073.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2080",
              "name": "DuplicateInitializerKey",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The three initializer forms fail in three different ways and none of them is what the author wrote. `{ { k, v }, { k, w } }` calls `Add` twice and `Dictionary<K, V>.Add` throws `ArgumentException` at construction — a field initializer doing that takes the type's constructor with it. `{ [k] = v, [k] = w }` assigns through the indexer, so the second write silently wins and `v` is evaluated and thrown away. `new HashSet<T> { a, a }` returns a set of one where two were written. In every case the object at the end of the initializer is not the object on the page, and the key is right there to compare."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two entries of one set or dictionary initializer — or of one collection expression — carry the same constant key, so the collection ends up smaller than it was written or the construction throws."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The set or dictionary initializer repeats a key"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2080.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2081",
              "name": "CollectionPassedToItself",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`set.UnionWith(set)` does nothing. `set.ExceptWith(set)` is `Clear()` written so that nobody reads it as `Clear()`. `set.SetEquals(set)` is `true` with a hash walk in front of it, `set.IsProperSubsetOf(set)` is `false`, and `list.AddRange(list)` doubles the list in place. Every entry in the table is a call whose result does not depend on what the collection holds, which is never why anybody writes one. The defect underneath is almost always a single identifier — the second collection was meant to be a different one — and that is also why there is no fix: naming the right collection is the repair, and the analyzer does not know which one it is."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A set, list or array is given to one of its own members as the second collection, where the answer is fixed, the call does nothing, or the collection is emptied or doubled."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection is passed to its own method as the other collection"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2081.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2082",
              "name": "OverwrittenCollectionElement",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A block of `map[…] = …;` statements is how a lookup table gets built by hand, and it is where one key gets written twice: the first value is computed, stored and discarded by a later statement, and nothing in the compiler notices because both writes are legal. Either a key is wrong or a line is redundant, and which of the two it is decides what the program was supposed to do — which is also why there is no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One entry of a list, array or dictionary is assigned twice in a contiguous run of element writes, so the first value is computed and thrown away."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection element is written twice with nothing reading it in between"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2082.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2083",
              "name": "ProvablyEmptyCollection",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The population step was removed, or was never written. The loop reads as working code, the compiler has nothing to say about it, and every test over it passes, because a body that never runs never fails. The repair is the missing step, which is why there is no fix: deleting the loop would silence the rule and keep the defect."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local collection is created empty, is never referred to except as the subject of a foreach, and is then iterated — so the loop body cannot run."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection iterated here is provably empty"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2083.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2090",
              "name": "FinalizerCanThrow",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An exception that escapes a finalizer is not caught anywhere. The runtime tears the process down from the finalizer thread, and the stack it prints names the finalizer queue rather than the code that put the object on it — so the one failure mode with no recovery path is also the one with the least diagnostic value. There is no `try` a caller can write that helps, because on that thread there is no caller."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `throw` can leave a finalizer, where nothing catches it and the process ends."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The finalizer can throw"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2090.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2091",
              "name": "ThrowInFinally",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `finally` runs while an exception is unwinding, and a `throw` from it does not join that exception — it replaces it. The failure that explains everything is destroyed, and the log is left holding the cleanup's complaint about a state the original failure created. The report names the one failure nobody can diagnose from, which is why this is worth a rule rather than a review comment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `throw` written inside a `finally` replaces whatever exception was already in flight."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exception is thrown from a `finally` block"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2091.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2092",
              "name": "NullReferenceExceptionCaught",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `NullReferenceException` is a dereference that should not have happened, and naming it in a `catch` converts that bug into a control-flow path the program is expected to take. The handler also covers a region far larger than the one dereference that was meant, so the next null anywhere under the `try` is silently absorbed by a recovery written for a different one. The repair is a null check at the dereference, not a handler around the region."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `catch` clause names `NullReferenceException` as the type it handles."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`NullReferenceException` is caught"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2092.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2093",
              "name": "CaughtExceptionDiscarded",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`catch (SqlException error) { throw new ImportException(\"the import failed\"); }` keeps the word \"failed\" and throws away the stack trace, the message, the error code and the cause. The caller is handed a sentence it cannot act on, and the frame that actually broke is no longer in the process. It looks like diligence, which is why it is more common than either the empty catch `SK2014` reports or the `throw ex;` restatement `SK2015` reports — and the repair is one argument."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `catch` names the exception, throws a different one, and never passes the first to the second."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The handler discards the exception it caught"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2093.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2100",
              "name": "IneffectiveThreadStatic",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Thread-static storage is per-thread storage of a *static* slot. On an instance field there is already one slot per object and the runtime ignores the attribute entirely, so the isolation the author asked for was never there. ⚠ The initializer case is worse than nothing and is the one people actually hit: the initializer runs in the static constructor, once, on whichever thread reaches the type first. That thread sees the value and every other thread sees `default`. Nothing throws, nothing warns, and the field reads correctly in the debugger on the thread that happens to be attached — which is why the bug survives review and is found from a null reference on a background thread three weeks later."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A field marked `[ThreadStatic]` is either not `static`, in which case the attribute does nothing at all, or is `static` with an initializer, in which case only the first thread to touch the type sees the initialized value."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`[ThreadStatic]` is applied where it cannot take effect"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2100.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2101",
              "name": "PureAttributeOnVoid",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The two accepted attributes fail for two different reasons and both fail. The BCL's `[Pure]` promises no visible state change; a `void` method that makes no visible state change and returns nothing has no observable effect at all, so either the annotation is wrong or the method is dead. JetBrains' `[Pure]` means the return value must be used; a `void` method has no return value, so the annotation is inapplicable by its own definition. ⚠ The annotation is load-bearing in Skala specifically: `SK2002` reads it to decide whether a discarded result matters, so an annotation that cannot be true feeds a second rule bad input."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `void` method carries `System.Diagnostics.Contracts.PureAttribute` or `JetBrains.Annotations.PureAttribute`, neither of which can be true of a method that returns nothing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`[Pure]` is applied to a method that returns nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2101.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2102",
              "name": "DebuggerDisplayMissingMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The expression is bound by the debugger at inspection time, not by the compiler, so a rename breaks it silently and it is discovered while debugging something else, at the moment it is least welcome. It is the same string-to-member binding `nameof` exists to remove, and the same class of defect as an `ArgumentException` naming a parameter that is not there. There is no fix: only the author knows which member was meant, and a rule that guessed would rename the display to whatever happened to be nearby."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `{…}` hole in a `[DebuggerDisplay]` string on a type declaration names a single identifier that is not a member of that type or of anything it inherits or implements."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `[DebuggerDisplay]` string names a member that does not exist"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2102.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2103",
              "name": "DuplicatedAttribute",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ This is the one of issue #269's four sub-concepts the compiler is silent about, and the silence is the point. An attribute applied to a target it does not declare is `CS0592`; applied twice when `AllowMultiple` is false it is `CS0579`; naming a member that does not exist it is `CS8776` for the nullable-contract attributes and `SK2102` for `[DebuggerDisplay]`. For an `AllowMultiple` attribute the repetition is *legal* — it is simply indistinguishable from writing it once — so it survives review, and what it costs depends on who reads the metadata: a duplicated `[InlineData]` runs one test case twice under a name that says it ran once, and a duplicated `[SuppressMessage]` is dead metadata that the next person tries to work out the difference between."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One declaration carries the same `AllowMultiple` attribute more than once with provably identical arguments, so nothing distinguishes the second application from the first."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The attribute is applied twice with the same arguments"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2103.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2110",
              "name": "TostringCanReturnNull",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "String interpolation, `string.Concat`, every structured logger and most of the BCL call `ToString()` and assume the result is a string. A null comes back as a `NullReferenceException` or a silently absent value somewhere else entirely, and the type that caused it is named nowhere in the stack. ⚠ **The rule exists only where the compiler is silent, and that boundary was measured rather than assumed.** A probe built with the .NET 10.0.400 SDK reports `CS8603` on `public override string ToString() { return null; }` in a nullable-enabled context — so the rule declines there, because ADR-008's answer to a diagnostic the platform already ships is to use it. What the probe found the compiler saying nothing about is the other two shapes: `public override string? ToString() => null;`, which is legal and warning-free because `object.ToString()` is itself annotated as returning `string?`, and the same `return null;` under `#nullable disable`. Those are what this reports."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An override of `object.ToString()` returns a null constant on some path."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`ToString()` returns null"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2110.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2111",
              "name": "InertNullSuppression",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`!` is the strongest claim a C# author can make — *I know better than the compiler here* — and every reader has to stop and work out what was known. Where nullable warnings are switched off at that position, or where the operand is a non-nullable value type, there was never a warning to suppress: the operator is decoration, it survives every migration untouched, and it makes the file look as though its nullability was thought about when it was not. ⚠ **Not hosted by `IDE0080`, and that was measured, not assumed.** `CSharpRemoveUnnecessaryNullableWarningSuppressionsDiagnosticAnalyzer` ships in the .NET 10.0.400 SDK, and a probe carrying all three shapes reported nothing from it — with `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`, `AnalysisMode=All` and `dotnet_diagnostic.IDE0080.severity = warning`, and with `IDE0090` and `IDE0059` firing from the same analyzer set on the same build to prove the code-style analyzers really ran. `dotnet format analyzers --diagnostics IDE0080` reported nothing on the same files while `--diagnostics IDE0090` reported the control."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `!` stands where no nullable warning could be issued, so it hides nothing and reads as though a decision were made."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The null-forgiving operator suppresses nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2111.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2112",
              "name": "NullableLocalNeverNull",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The reverse direction of the nullable migration. A `?` the flow analysis proves unnecessary is a null check every subsequent line has to justify for nothing, and it trains the reader to stop believing the annotations — which is the whole value of having them. The declaration is the one place the claim can be corrected, and for a local it can be corrected without asking anybody, because nothing outside the method can see it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local is declared `T?`, is assigned once with a value the compiler proves non-null, and is never assigned again."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local is declared nullable and is never null"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2112.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2113",
              "name": "NullForgivenServiceResolution",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`GetService<T>()` returns null for a service nobody registered, and the `!` is the author asserting that somebody did. When the assertion is wrong the program does not fail at the resolution — it fails at the first use, with a `NullReferenceException` that names neither the service type nor the container. `GetRequiredService<T>()` is the same call with the assertion moved into the framework: it throws an `InvalidOperationException` naming the service type, at the line that asked for it. The `!` is not redundant here, which is why `SK2111` declines it; it is the wrong answer to a real warning."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`GetService<T>()!` suppresses the warning that says the service may not be registered; `GetRequiredService<T>()` throws where the mistake is."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The service resolution is null-forgiven instead of required"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2113.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2120",
              "name": "BitwiseOperationOnPlainEnum",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An enum written `enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }` numbers its members consecutively, so `Red | Green` is `0 | 1`, which is `Green`, and `Green | Blue` is `1 | 2`, which is `3` and no declared member at all. The value then fails every `switch`, every `Equals` and every `ToString`, and it does so silently: the operation is legal C#, the compiler says nothing, and the wrong value is only noticed downstream where nothing points back here. ⚠ **This is the use site, not the declaration.** `CA1027` asks a declaration whose values already look like a bit set to carry `[Flags]`, and `CA2217` asks a `[Flags]` declaration to stop declaring values that are not powers of two. Neither can be reached from an enum that was never given explicit values, so neither says a word about the code that combines one — which is the only place the mistake is visible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `|`, `&`, `^` or `~` is applied to an enum whose members carry no explicit values, so they are numbered 0, 1, 2, ... and no bit of the result means anything."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A bitwise operator combines members of a consecutively numbered enum"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2120.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2121",
              "name": "AlwaysSucceedingAsOperator",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`d as Base` where `d` is a `Derived` yields `d`, always. The operator reads as a question — *is this a `Base`?* — and there is no question: the widening is the same one an assignment would perform. What it costs is the null check that usually follows, which the next reader takes for a type guard and which is really a null guard on a value the code may already have proved non-null. ⚠ **This is the half of issue #1 the compiler does not already own, and it is the only half.** The always-*false* cases are compiler diagnostics: `s is int` and `d is Unrelated` are `CS0184`, `d as Unrelated` is `CS0039`, and an unreachable type pattern in a `switch` is `CS8121` — the last two are errors, not warnings. `v is int` on an `int` is `CS0183`. What is left over is this operator, which the compiler permits in silence because it is well defined."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The operand already converts to the tested type implicitly, so `as` can only return the operand and the `null` it appears to guard against is the operand’s own `null`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `as` operator tests a conversion that always succeeds"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2121.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2130",
              "name": "ForwardStaticInitializer",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Static field initializers are emitted into the static constructor in the order the fields are written down. An initializer that names a field below it therefore runs before that field has been given its value, and reads zero, or null. Nothing throws, nothing warns, and the value is wrong for the life of the process — the field is simply never right, so there is no first failure to bisect to. ⚠ **Three neighbouring shapes look identical and are all correct, which is why the rule is exact about the construct rather than about the direction of the reference.** A static *property* is a method that runs when it is called, so a getter naming a field below it reads whatever that field holds at that moment. A static *method* is ordered against nothing. And a **static constructor runs after every field initializer**, so a read from there sees a fully initialized type. Only a field initializer is ordered against other field initializers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A static field's initializer reads another static field of the same type that is declared after it, and field initializers run in declaration order, so the read sees `default` rather than the initialized value."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A static field initializer reads a field declared below it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2130.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2131",
              "name": "UnassignedGetOnlyProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **This is the one shape of issue #24 the compiler leaves entirely alone, and the narrowing was measured rather than argued.** A probe built at `AnalysisMode=All` reports `CS0649` for a `private`, an `internal` and a `private readonly` field that nothing assigns, so the field half of the concept belongs to the compiler and there is nothing to add. A `public` field draws no `CS0649` and cannot: any consumer outside the compilation may write it, so the claim is not decidable here either. What remains is the property half, and it is decidable for a reason worth stating — **a property with only a `get` accessor and no initializer can be assigned from nowhere but a constructor of its own declaring type**, every part of that type is in this compilation because a type cannot be split across assemblies, and a source generator's part is compiled source like any other. \"Nothing assigns it\" is therefore a fact this analysis establishes rather than a guess about callers, which is the difference between this rule and the field half it declines."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property with only a `get` accessor, no initializer and no constructor that assigns it can be written from nowhere, so it holds `default` for the life of every instance."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The get-only property is never assigned"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2131.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2132",
              "name": "MismatchedBackingField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The copy-paste defect in hand-written properties: the second property is written by duplicating the first and one of the two field names is not changed. It is silent in every direction — the type compiles, the property runs, the getter returns somebody else's value and the setter writes over it — and a test that exercises one property at a time can pass while both are wrong. ⚠ **The reason this needs a rule at all is that nothing else looks**: the compiler has no opinion about which field an accessor reaches for, and a reviewer reading two nearly identical five-line properties is reading exactly the shape human attention is worst at."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property that has a conventionally named backing field of its own reads or writes a different field — one that conventionally backs another property of the same type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The accessor uses another property's backing field"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2132.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2133",
              "name": "UnimplementedPartialMethod",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **A `partial` method with no implementation is legal, and erasing it is the feature — so the declaration on its own is never the finding.** The call is. When no implementing declaration exists the compiler removes the defining declaration *and every call to it*, and argument evaluation goes with the call, so work written into an argument silently does not happen. ⚠ **Requiring a call is also what makes the rule decidable rather than merely narrow**: a classic `partial void` may carry no accessibility modifier and is therefore implicitly `private`, so every caller it can ever have is inside the declaring type, which is in this compilation in full. \"Nothing calls it\" and \"something calls it\" are both facts here, not guesses about the rest of the world — which is the difference between this rule and the field half of #24, and it is why an unimplemented hook nobody has taken up is left alone."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `partial void` with no implementing declaration is erased together with every call to it, arguments included, so a call that reads as a hook does nothing at all."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The called `partial` method has no implementation"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2133.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2134",
              "name": "InstanceWriteToStatic",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The write reads as per-instance initialization and is not. There is one slot: two objects that were meant to carry different values carry the same one, and two threads that construct at the same time lose an update between them. ⚠ **It survives review because the line is short and looks local** — `count++` in a constructor is four characters that describe a per-object act and perform a process-wide one — and it survives testing because a test that constructs one object never sees it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An instance constructor, method or accessor assigns a static field of its own type, so the last instance to run the line wins for every other instance and every thread."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Instance code writes to its own type's static field"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2134.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2140",
              "name": "OverriddenParameterDefault",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A default value is not part of the call — it is baked into the call site at compile time, from the *static* type of the receiver. Through a base-class or interface reference the base's default is the one that is written into the IL, and the override's is dead text; through the derived type the override's wins. The same member therefore behaves as two different methods depending on which reference the caller happens to hold, and nothing in either declaration says so. ⚠ **`params` behaves differently from a default and the difference was measured rather than assumed.** An *override* does not get to change it at all: `params` dropped on an override still expands through the derived type, and `params` added on an override whose base has none expands through neither reference — both call sites were compiled and both answers read off the build. Expansion is decided by the base, always, so an override's own `params` keyword is dead text in both directions and there is nothing to report. An *interface implementation* is the opposite: it inherits nothing, so an implementation that drops the interface's `params` gives `((IPlain)x).Accept(\"a\", 1, 2)` compiling and `x.Accept(\"a\", 1, 2)` failing with `CS1501`, from one object. That asymmetry is the finding. ⚠ **The compiler is silent on every one of these.** It emits `CS1066` when an *explicit* interface implementation carries a default, because such a member can never be called with optional arguments at all — that case is hosted and this rule declines it. For an override, and for an implicit interface implementation, there is no diagnostic at any warning level; the shape was compiled and the build read, not assumed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An override or an implicit interface implementation declares a different default value, or a different `params` modifier, than the member it overrides or implements."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The override declares a parameter default or `params` the call site will not use"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2140.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2141",
              "name": "RestatedCallerInfoArgument",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Caller-info substitution happens only when the argument is *omitted*. Supply one and the compiler steps back silently: the parameter still has a value, the call still compiles, and the diagnostic the parameter was added to carry is now whatever the caller typed. For `[CallerLineNumber]` and `[CallerFilePath]` that is a fabricated source location — a log line, an assertion message or an exception that names a place the code is not — and it is the one class of wrong data that survives review indefinitely, because nobody re-derives a line number by hand. ⚠ **This rule and `SK0232` share a span and must not disagree about it.** `SK0232` excludes caller-info parameters from its redundant-argument scan precisely because passing `null` to one is the *opposite* of redundant: omit it and the compiler substitutes a name, so the argument is the only thing keeping the value null. The two are disjoint by construction rather than by filter — `SK0232` never looks at a caller-info parameter, and this rule reports only arguments that restate the substitution exactly or fabricate a location, never `null`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An explicit argument is passed to a `[CallerMemberName]`, `[CallerLineNumber]`, `[CallerFilePath]` or `[CallerArgumentExpression]` parameter, suppressing the substitution the parameter exists for."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The argument replaces what the caller-info attribute would have supplied"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2141.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2142",
              "name": "OverwrittenParameter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A parameter that is written before it is read is an argument the caller computed, passed, and never had used. Either the body was meant to use it and an assignment was put in the wrong place, or the author wanted a local and reused the parameter's name to avoid inventing one — and those are opposite defects with the same shape. The reason this is worth a warning rather than a hint is that the call site still reads as though the value matters: every caller is doing work whose result is thrown away one line into the callee, and nothing at the call site can show that."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Every path through the member assigns the parameter before anything reads it, so the value the caller passed is discarded unused."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The value passed for the parameter is overwritten before it is read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2142.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2143",
              "name": "CrosswiseArgumentOrder",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two parameters of the same type next to each other are the one place a transposed call compiles and runs. Nothing at the call site marks it, the types agree, the tests that pass both values through symmetric code stay green, and the failure surfaces somewhere else entirely. The only evidence available is the names: when the argument sitting in `destination` is a variable called `source` and the argument sitting in `source` is called `destination`, the author's own naming says which way round they meant it and the call says the other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two adjacent arguments of the same type are named after the two parameters they fill, in the opposite order."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two arguments are passed crosswise to the parameters they are named after"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2143.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2150",
              "name": "ImplicitStringSearchCulture",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SK2010` covers the comparison half of the culture question; this is the search half, and it fails in a way comparison does not — it returns an *offset*. A `LastIndexOf(\"-\")` that finds a different index on a Turkish machine than on an invariant one does not throw and does not compare unequal: it hands back a number that a `Substring` then slices on, so the defect surfaces as a truncated identifier three call frames away. The ambient culture is a process setting nothing in the file mentions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A string IndexOf, LastIndexOf, StartsWith or EndsWith call takes a string and no StringComparison, so the ambient culture decides where the match is."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A string search has an implicit culture policy"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2150.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2151",
              "name": "InvariantCultureComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`StringComparison.InvariantCulture` is culture-*stable*, not culture-*free*. It still walks the collation tables, so it costs an order of magnitude more than `Ordinal` and it still answers `true` for sequences that are not the same string — a zero-width joiner and an empty string compare equal under it. For the things equality is actually asked about — identifiers, dictionary keys, protocol tokens, file names — `Ordinal` is what was meant. ⚠ **This is the mirror image of `SK2010`, and the harder one to see.** `SK2010` reports a policy that is *missing*; this reports one that is *stated and wrong*, which reads as deliberate and therefore survives review."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An equality-shaped string operation is given StringComparison.InvariantCulture, which still runs the full linguistic comparison it was reached for to avoid."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A string equality test asks for invariant culture where ordinal was meant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2151.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2152",
              "name": "PlatformDependentPathComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two paths differing only in case are one file on Windows and on a default macOS volume, and two files on Linux. Folding them together on Linux is the failure a path comparison may never have — it serves one file's answer for another — and it is invisible on the developer's machine, appearing only in the container. ⚠ **Skala's own answer to this is already in the tree and the rule is written from it**: `SarifWriter.PathComparison` is `OperatingSystem.IsLinux() ? StringComparison.Ordinal : StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase`, and `CacheKeyPathTests` asserts the correct answer on all three platforms rather than skipping on two of them. So the guidance is not \"stop ignoring case\" — it is \"compare the path the way this file system compares it\", chosen at run time."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A path-valued string is compared with a hard-coded case-insensitive StringComparison, which is right on Windows and macOS and wrong on Linux."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The path comparison assumes the file system's case rules"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2152.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2153",
              "name": "QueryableDegradedToEnumerable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Once one operator binds to `System.Linq.Enumerable`, the provider has already been asked to enumerate everything it has: the `Where` that was meant to become a `WHERE` clause becomes a full table read, and every operator after it runs on the wire's output. Nothing fails, nothing warns, and the only symptom is a query that got slower in proportion to the table. It is the most expensive silent mistake in data-access code, and it is normally introduced by passing a `Func<T, bool>` where an `Expression<Func<T, bool>>` was needed — a change of one word at a declaration far from the call."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A deferred LINQ operator binds to Enumerable on a receiver whose static type is IQueryable, so the rest of the query stops translating and runs in the client."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An IQueryable is silently degraded to IEnumerable"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2153.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2154",
              "name": "SortWithoutOrdering",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Comparer<T>.Default` throws `InvalidOperationException` on the first comparison it is asked to make when `T` implements neither interface. The call compiles cleanly, and it needs *two* elements to fail — so an empty list and a single-element list both sort successfully, which is what the unit test contains and what production does not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A sort or ordering falls back to Comparer<T>.Default for a type that implements neither IComparable<T> nor IComparable, and no comparer is passed."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The sort has no ordering to use"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2154.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2160",
              "name": "StaticClockRead",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A static clock read is a dependency the signature does not mention and the call site cannot replace. Code that depends on it can only be tested by waiting — which is what produces the `Thread.Sleep` that `SK8005` then reports — and by tests that pass in one time zone and fail in another. `TimeProvider`, .NET 8's answer, turns the read into an ordinary injected dependency: the test supplies the instant and the production path supplies `TimeProvider.System`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A member reads DateTime.Now, DateTime.UtcNow, DateTime.Today or the DateTimeOffset equivalents from a static, so no caller and no test can give it a different time. Ships disabled; turn it on per path."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The clock is read from a static"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2160.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2161",
              "name": "UnspecifiedDateTimeKind",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`new DateTime(2026, 1, 2)` has `Kind = Unspecified`, which is not a time zone but the absence of one. Nothing goes wrong while the value is only compared with others of the same unstated zone; it goes wrong where something turns it into a fixed moment, because that conversion takes the missing offset from whichever machine is running. ⚠ **The two conversions assume opposite things about the same value**: `ToUniversalTime()` treats an `Unspecified` value as local and subtracts the machine's offset, `ToLocalTime()` treats it as UTC and adds it. One value, one `Kind`, two contradictory readings — which is why round-tripping correctly on the author's machine is not evidence of anything, and why the failure surfaces in one region, twice a year, in rows another process wrote."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A DateTime built by a constructor that takes no DateTimeKind — so its Kind is Unspecified — is passed to ToUniversalTime, ToLocalTime or a DateTimeOffset conversion, each of which has to invent the offset it was never given."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `DateTime` has no time zone and is converted as if it had one"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2161.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2162",
              "name": "ImplicitDateParseCulture",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`DateTime.TryParse(\"01/02/2026\", out var when)` returns `true` on every machine and a different day on some of them: the second of January in one culture, the first of February in another. Unlike a comparison that merely orders two strings oddly, the wrong answer here is a perfectly valid date, so nothing downstream can detect it — the record is filed under a day that never happened. ⚠ `SK2010` makes this argument for string comparison; dates are the case where the wrong answer still type-checks, still validates and still round-trips."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A DateTime, DateTimeOffset, DateOnly, TimeOnly or TimeSpan TryParse is called on an overload with no IFormatProvider parameter, so the text is read in the process's ambient culture."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The date or time `TryParse` has an implicit culture"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2162.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2163",
              "name": "WallClockElapsed",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`DateTime.Now` and `DateTime.UtcNow` track civil time, which is adjusted; they are not monotonic. An NTP correction moves the clock by the accumulated drift, in either direction, at a moment nobody chose, and `DateTime.Now` additionally jumps an hour twice a year. A duration measured across either is wrong by that amount and can be negative, so a guard written to catch a slow operation reports a fast one, a retry backoff computed from it collapses, and a timeout either fires at once or never. `Stopwatch` reads a monotonic counter that none of this touches, and the rewrite is mechanical."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A duration is computed by subtracting an earlier clock read from a later one, using a clock that an NTP correction or a DST transition can move backwards."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elapsed time is measured with the wall clock"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2163.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2164",
              "name": "SideEffectInAssertion",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`[Conditional(\"DEBUG\")]` does not make the condition true in release; it deletes the call site, arguments and all. `Debug.Assert(pending.Remove(id))` therefore removes the entry in every debug run and in no release run. The bug exists only in production, vanishes under a debugger, and survives the test suite intact — because the suite is a debug build, so the effect is there and the assertion passes. It is the rare defect that running the code more carefully makes less visible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An argument of a call to a [Conditional]-attributed method — Debug.Assert and Trace.Assert above all — assigns, increments, awaits or mutates a framework collection, so the effect happens only in the builds where the call survives."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The assertion's expression has side effects"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2164.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2170",
              "name": "MisleadingBodyIndentation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "```\nif (stale)\n    Reload();\n    Publish();\n```\n`Publish()` runs every time. Nothing about the program says so, and everything about the picture says the opposite — which is the whole failure: the reader's eye takes indentation as structure and C# does not. ⚠ **This is the one half of the concept the compiler leaves alone, and that was measured rather than assumed.** The other half — an empty statement standing in for a body, `if (x); { … }` — is `CS0642`, on by default, and it reaches further than doc 17 supposed: it covers `if`, `else`, `lock`, `do`, `using` and `fixed` outright, and covers `while`, `for` and `foreach` exactly when a block follows the `;`, which is precisely the shape that misleads. So this rule declines an empty body followed by a block and reports one followed by anything else — `while (x)`, then `;` on its own line, then a statement aligned with the `;` is a real finding the compiler is silent about. ⚠ **The first draft declined every empty body and a sabotage proved the exemption unreachable**, because `while (x) ;` puts the `;` on the header's own line, which the line test already declines. ⚠ **Indentation is not structure anywhere in C#, so nothing but a formatter is looking at it**, and Skala is a formatter: the rule reads the leading whitespace of three lines and compares them as strings, which is a question no semantic model can be asked."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `if`, `else`, `while`, `for`, `foreach`, `lock`, `using` or `fixed` has a single unbraced statement as its body, and the next statement is indented as far as that body or further, so the layout claims a block the grammar does not give."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The indentation says the statement is in the body and it is not"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2170.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2171",
              "name": "VariableLengthHexEscape",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`\\x` is the only escape in C# whose length is not fixed: the compiler takes up to four hex digits, and it does not stop at the ones the author meant. `\"\\x41B\"` is one character, U+041B — not `A` followed by `B`. Append a letter to a string that ends in `\\x41` and its last character silently changes; the diff shows one character added and the program changed two. ⚠ **`\\u` has a fixed length of four and cannot do this**, which is why the fix is a spelling change and nothing else. ⚠ **The other two inspections this concept was drawn from are not here, and both were measured out.** `1l` is `CS0078` — \"the 'l' suffix is easily confused with the digit '1'\" — on by default, and a probe confirms it fires on `1l` and on `1lu` and stays quiet on `1ul`. A `char` argument widening to an `int` parameter is a different question about overload resolution, not about how a literal reads, and it is not this rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `\\x` escape in a string or character literal is written with one, two or three hex digits, so how many characters it consumes depends on the character that happens to come next."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `\\x` escape's length is decided by what follows it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2171.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2172",
              "name": "ForgivenIsOperand",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`x! is string` and `!(x is string)` differ by the position of one character and answer opposite questions, and the first is the one that compiles silently in every context. ⚠ **The `!` is doing nothing there, and that is measured rather than argued.** `is` never issues a nullability warning about its own operand — a pattern match is exactly the test that makes a null operand safe — so there is no warning for the `!` to suppress. A probe compiled both spellings under `#nullable enable`: `if (s! is object) { }` followed by `s.Length` reports `CS8602`, and so does the same code with the `!` removed, at the same position. ⚠ **That refutes the reason this rule was first going to ship a fix.** The suppression was assumed to carry forward into the flow state, making its removal unsafe; it does not, and the fix is fixless for the other reason instead — the repair is a guess about which program was meant."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A null-forgiving `!` stands immediately before an `is`, where it suppresses nothing and where the two tokens read as an inverted type test."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `!` before `is` reads as a negated `is`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2172.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2173",
              "name": "NegatedEmptyPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`{ }` matches every non-null value, so `not { }` matches every null one. The word on the page is `not`, the reader takes it as \"has something\", and the pattern means \"has nothing\". `x is null` says it in four characters with no inversion to hold in your head. ⚠ **`{ }` matches a boxed struct and a non-null `Nullable<T>` as well as a reference, and the rewrite keeps all of that** — `is null` on a `T?` is `!HasValue`, which is exactly what `is not { }` was already testing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `not { }` pattern with no type, no positional clause, no subpatterns and no designation matches exactly the null values, so it is `null` spelled in a way that reads as its opposite."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`is not { }` is a null check written backwards"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2173.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2174",
              "name": "UnparenthesisedPrecedenceMix",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`a << b + 1` shifts by `b + 1`. `mask & offset + 1` masks against `offset + 1`. `a | b & c` is `a | (b & c)`. Every one of those is what the grammar says and none of them is what most readers say when asked, because the shift and bitwise operators sit between arithmetic and comparison in a table nobody has memorised. ⚠ **The parentheses cost nothing and settle it**, which is the whole argument — there is no defect here to find, only a sentence that can be written so it cannot be misread. ⚠ **The boundary against `SK0209` is settled by two facts, one per value of `skala_parentheses_redundancy_style`, and settling it was the condition on shipping this at all.** At the default `remove_if_not_clarifies_precedence`, `ParenthesesRedundancy.MayRemove` refuses a binary operand of a shift or a bitwise operator, because `resharper_parentheses_non_obvious_operations` names exactly those, and every pair of parentheses this rule adds is around such an operand, so the arranger will not take one back. At `remove` — ReSharper's \"Always\" — the arranger strips them as the oracle does, and this rule is silent: the repository has said it does not want them. ⚠ This text used to say \"settled by construction\" and that the arranger refused \"unconditionally\"; at `remove`, `skala fix` and `skala arrange` undid each other on every run (#394). A CLI test now runs fix, arrange and fix again at each value and asserts a fixed point."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An operand of `<<`, `>>`, `>>>`, `&`, `|` or `^` is itself an unparenthesised binary expression from a different precedence family, so how the expression groups is a fact about the C# grammar rather than a fact on the page."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The shift or bitwise operand relies on precedence to group"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2174.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2180",
              "name": "ForeachElementDowncast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`foreach (Derived d in items)` over an `IEnumerable<Base>` is not a filter and not a check: C# writes an explicit conversion into the loop for you, and the loop stops with an `InvalidCastException` on the first element that is something else. The cast is the one operation in C# that a `foreach` performs and does not spell — `var` shows nothing, the declared type reads as documentation, and every review of the line reads it as the element type rather than as an assertion about it. ⚠ **This is the part of issue #2 that is neither a compiler diagnostic nor a value-lattice problem.** The impossible conversions in that issue are `CS0030` — probed at `AnalysisMode=All`, `(Sealed)derived`, `(IUnrelated)sealedValue` and `(Sealed)unrelatedInterface` are all **errors**, so that code never reaches a linter. What is left of the issue after the compiler has taken its share is a downcast that is *possible* and unannounced, and only the `foreach` form is decidable without reasoning about which values reach the site."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The loop variable's type is narrower than the sequence's element type, so the language inserts a cast the source does not show and the loop throws on the first element that is not of that type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `foreach` casts every element down to a narrower type"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2180.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2181",
              "name": "GetTypeOnAType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`t.GetType()` where `t` is a `Type` answers a question nobody asked. It returns `System.RuntimeType` for every input, so the value is non-null, is a `Type`, and compares equal to itself — which is why it survives every test that checks for null and every test that checks two calls agree. The failure is silent and constant: a registry keyed on it has one key, a `switch` over it has one arm reachable, and a log line prints `RuntimeType` where a type name was meant. ⚠ **The compiler says nothing at all**, verified at `AnalysisMode=All`: `t.GetType()` and `typeof(Widget).GetType()` produce no diagnostic of any kind, and neither does any `CA*` rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The receiver's static type is `System.Type`, so `GetType()` returns the runtime type of the `Type` object — `System.RuntimeType` — rather than the type it describes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`GetType()` is called on something that is already a `Type`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2181.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2182",
              "name": "TypeComparedByName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The comparison looks like a type test and is a string test. Rename `Order` and every tool in the toolchain updates every reference except this one, which keeps compiling and starts returning `false` — the worst shape a defect can have, because the symptom is a branch that stops being taken rather than an exception anybody can trace. In the other direction `Type.Name` is not unique: any `Order` in any referenced assembly, in any namespace, matches. ⚠ **Nothing upstream implements this** — the SonarSource idea it comes from is an unspecified proposal — so the specification here is entirely Skala's, and the whole of it is the resolution test below."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`x.GetType().Name == \"Order\"` binds a type by a string the compiler never checks, so a rename makes it silently false and any other assembly's `Order` makes it silently true."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type is identified by comparing its name to a string"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2182.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2183",
              "name": "StaticMemberViaDerivedType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Leaf.Count` and `Root.Count` are the same slot. Written the first way the line says otherwise: it reads as `Leaf`'s counter, and the reader who goes looking for the declaration on `Leaf` does not find it. The mistake costs something the day a second type derives from `Root` and the two are found to share the value nobody thought they shared, and again the day `Count` moves — because a qualifier naming a type that does not declare the member is the one reference the compiler will happily keep resolving to somewhere else. ⚠ **Neither the compiler nor the SDK reports it, and that was probed rather than assumed.** At `AnalysisMode=All` with `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild`, `Leaf.Count`, `Leaf.Read()`, `Leaf.Limit` and `Leaf.Total` produce nothing; `CA1000` and `IDE0002` were each raised to `warning` and stayed silent on all four."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The qualifier names a derived type and the static member is declared on a base, so the line reads as though the derived type owned state that is shared with every other type in the hierarchy."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A static member is reached through a type that inherits it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2183.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2184",
              "name": "HiddenBaseInterfaceOverload",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A derived interface that declares any overload of a name takes the whole name: the base interface's overloads of it are removed from the lookup before overload resolution ever runs, so the call binds to the derived one even where the base one is the better match. ⚠ **This was run rather than reasoned about.** With `IChild : IParent`, `IParent.M(string)` and `IChild.M(object)`, `c.M(\"literal\")` executes `IChild.M(object)` while `p.M(\"literal\")` on the same object executes `IParent.M(string)` — the same argument, the same instance, two different methods, and **no diagnostic of any kind** from the compiler or from any `CA*` rule. That is the shape issue #49 describes as \"redirect existing calls with no diagnostic anywhere\", and it is the only part of the issue that is not already an error."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An overload declared on a base interface is applicable to these arguments and is a better match than the one the call binds to, and it is unreachable because the derived interface declares the same name."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The call skips a better overload the derived interface hides"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2184.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2190",
              "name": "StructKeyWithoutEquality",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`EqualityComparer<T>.Default` for a struct that implements nothing falls back to `ValueType.Equals` and `ValueType.GetHashCode`, which walk the fields with reflection when the layout is not blittable. The cost lands on every insert and every lookup for as long as the collection lives, and it lands boxed. ⚠ **The line where it happens contains no comparison to read.** A `Dictionary<Point, int>` names no method, so there is nothing at the use site for a reviewer to notice, and the declaration it comes from looks like an ordinary small struct — which is why the finding belongs where the collection is built rather than where the struct is written."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `Dictionary`, `HashSet` or `ConcurrentDictionary` is constructed over a source struct key that declares no equality member and is given no comparer, so every hash and every comparison goes through `ValueType`'s reflection fallback."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The struct key has no equality of its own"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2190.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2191",
              "name": "ReadonlyReceiverMutation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A non-`readonly` member reached through a readonly-context receiver makes the compiler insert a defensive copy: the method runs, the fields change, and the object the caller is holding does not. Nothing throws, nothing warns, and the value read back afterwards is the one from before the call. ⚠ **AI-generated code produces this shape by default**, because a model writes `struct` without `readonly` and writes `in` on a parameter as a performance habit, and the two together are exactly the defect."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A struct method that writes its own instance fields is called through an `in` parameter, a `ref readonly` local or a `foreach` variable, so the write goes to a copy and is discarded."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The struct is copied before the mutation lands"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2191.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2192",
              "name": "SpanReferenceComparison",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The operator reads like string equality and answers a question about addresses. Two spans over identical text compare `false`, and the same span compared with itself compares `true`, so the shape passes whatever test was written for it first and fails on the data. ⚠ **`span == \"abc\"` is the case that misleads hardest**: the implicit conversion from `string` makes it look like ordinary text comparison and it is a memory comparison against a freshly made span over the literal, which can never be equal."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`==` or `!=` binds to `Span<T>`'s or `ReadOnlySpan<T>`'s operator, which is true only when both spans point at the same memory and length."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`==` on spans compares memory locations"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2192.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2193",
              "name": "ImmutableArrayCollectionInitializer",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The collection-initializer form is sugar for `Add` calls on whatever `new` produced, and for this type `new` produces `default` — a struct around a null reference. The line reads like list construction, compiles without a word, and throws on the first element every single time it runs. It is a trap on one type specifically, which is what makes it survive being read."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`new ImmutableArray<T> { … }` calls `Add` on the default value of the struct, which wraps a null array, so the first element throws `NullReferenceException`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`ImmutableArray<T>` is built with a collection initializer"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2193.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2194",
              "name": "MutableCapturedPrimaryParameter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Capture itself is the feature: a parameter read from a member body becomes a hidden field, and that is what primary constructors are for. What the language gives no way to say is that the field is `readonly`. The moment a body writes the parameter, the type carries mutable state with no declaration, no modifier, no name a reader can search for, and no visibility to any tool that reasons about fields — and the write reads as an assignment to a parameter, which is local by every habit a reader has."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A member body of a class or struct with a primary constructor assigns one of its parameters, so the compiler-generated capture field is mutable instance state that appears in no declaration."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The captured primary constructor parameter is assigned"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2194.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "12.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2200",
              "name": "OverwrittenFieldInitializer",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The initializer is dead work on every allocation, and that is the smaller half. The larger half is that two values are written down for one field and only one of them is ever true, so a reader who looks at the declaration to learn the default learns a value the program never holds. ⚠ **Which of the two was meant is a real question and the finding does not answer it.** What it asserts is narrower and checkable: the value at the declaration is unreachable. Deleting it is the edit that makes the code say what it does; keeping it and deleting the constructor's assignment is the other repair, and only the author knows which."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private instance field is given a value at its declaration and every constructor that runs field initializers assigns it again before anything reads it, so the declared value never survives an allocation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The field initializer is overwritten by every constructor"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2200.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2201",
              "name": "AnonymousUnsubscription",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **This is not a heuristic. It is always true.** Delegate removal compares invocation-list entries by target and method, and an anonymous function written at one syntax site is a different instance from one written at any other — so `changed -= (s, e) => Redraw();` cannot remove the handler that `changed += (s, e) => Redraw();` added, however identical the two lines read. The subscription survives, the publisher keeps the subscriber alive for as long as it lives, and the line written to prevent the leak documents it instead. It is the shape that survives review precisely because it looks like the cleanup it is not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`-=` or `-` is applied to an event or a delegate with a lambda or an anonymous method on the right, and a delegate instance created here can never equal one created anywhere else, so the removal removes nothing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The unsubscription passes an anonymous function"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2201.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2202",
              "name": "ConditionalInvocationSideEffect",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`logger?.Log(sequence++)` reads as a log call that also advances a counter, and it advances the counter only while `logger` is not null. ⚠ **The null test is about the receiver, and that is how a reader takes it**; the arguments are inside its reach as a consequence of precedence rather than because anybody asked for it. The result is intermittent by construction, which puts it in the worst class of bug to reproduce — the counter is correct in every test that configures a logger, and wrong in the one deployment that does not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An assignment, a `++` or a `--` sits inside the part of a `?.` or `?[` expression that runs only when the receiver is not null, so the modification happens on some runs and not on others."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The modification sits inside a conditional invocation"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2202.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2210",
              "name": "InvalidConstantIndexOrRange",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Three shapes, and what they have in common is that the length never enters the arithmetic. `x[^0]` is one past the end whatever `x` holds — `^0` is `Length - 0`, not the last element — and it throws on a full collection and an empty one alike; it is the language wart that catches everyone once, and `^1` is what was meant. A range whose start is fixed above its end, `x[3..1]` or `x[^1..^3]`, is rejected by `Range.GetOffsetAndLength` before any length is consulted. A negative constant index throws from every positional indexer. ⚠ **This is not SK2001 and not SK2053.** SK2001 folds a *comparison* the operand type's range decides and SK2053 one a count's non-negativity decides; neither looks at an element access at all, and no type range or count contract says anything about `^0`. The fact this rule adds is the indexing contract itself: the offset `^0` denotes, the ordering `Range` requires of its endpoints, and the non-negativity every positional indexer requires of its argument — each provable from the constants on the page with no knowledge of the length whatsoever. There is no fix, for SK2001's reason: the rule can prove the access throws and cannot know whether `^1`, `1..3` or `0` was intended."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A constant index or range that no collection length can make valid, so the element access throws for every input."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The constant index or range cannot be valid"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2210.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2211",
              "name": "UnchangingLoopCondition",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **An infinite loop is usually the point, so \"the loop has no exit\" is the wrong question to ask.** `while (true)` is the event loop, the reactor and the retry pump, and a rule that reported it would be reporting the shape every server is built out of. The narrower and answerable question is whether the condition can ever come out differently: a loop whose condition reads only locals and parameters, none of which the body writes, evaluates the same expression to the same value on every pass. Combined with a body that cannot `break`, `return` or `throw` its way out, the loop is decided before it starts — it runs zero times or it hangs — and the increment that was meant to be there is missing. That is a defect no `while (true)` is, and it is provable from definite-assignment alone, with no value reasoning and no lattice."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Every local and parameter the loop condition reads keeps its value for the whole loop, and nothing in the body can leave it, so the loop either never runs or never stops."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Nothing the loop condition reads is changed by the loop"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2211.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2212",
              "name": "SingleIterationLoop",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A body that always `break`s or `return`s consumes exactly one element and stops. Either the jump is in the wrong place — it belongs under a condition that is missing — or the loop is, and what was wanted is `FirstOrDefault`, a single `if`, or an index. The shape survives review because it reads as a loop and is one only in the grammar: the word `foreach` promises repetition the body has already ruled out. ⚠ **The question is settled by control flow, not by looking at the last statement**, and that is what makes it decidable rather than a heuristic. It is also what gets `break` right inside a nested `switch`, where the `break` binds to the switch and leaves the loop running — the reading a syntactic rule gets wrong, and the one this rule has a fixture for. There is no fix: which of the two readings was meant is exactly what the rule cannot know, and `S1751` marks its own quick fix infeasible for the same reason."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Every path through the loop body ends in a jump out of it, so the loop is an `if` that has been given the shape of a loop."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The loop body cannot run more than once"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2212.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2213",
              "name": "IndexOfComparedToPositive",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`IndexOf` answers two questions at once: whether the value is there, and where. `> 0` conflates them, and the element at position 0 falls down the gap — present, and reported absent. It survives every test whose fixture does not begin with the needle, which is most of them, and fails the day a caller passes a string that starts with what it is looking for. `>= 0` is the test for presence and `Contains` says it without an index at all. ⚠ **This is the carve-out SK2053 names and declines.** SK2053 proves a comparison from the framework contract that a count is never negative; `IndexOf` is the framework member whose negative result is *meaningful*, which is why SK2053's own false-positive note excludes it and why the two can never report the same expression. SK2001 is further away still: it decides a comparison from the operand type's range, and `IndexOf` returns an `int`, whose range settles nothing about zero."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A search that reports absence as `-1` is tested with `> 0`, which also rejects a match at the first position."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `IndexOf` result is compared to a positive number"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2213.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2220",
              "name": "DeadConditionalCall",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`[Conditional(\"DEBUG\")]` deletes the call site in every compilation that does not define `DEBUG`. Writing that call inside `#if !DEBUG` therefore produces a statement that runs in no configuration whatever: where `DEBUG` is undefined the directive admits it and the attribute removes it, and where `DEBUG` is defined the directive excludes it before the compiler sees it. The two mechanisms cancel. Somebody wrote both, so somebody meant the call to happen — and it never does, in any build, on any machine."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A call to a [Conditional(\"X\")] method stands inside a preprocessor branch that was taken because X is not defined, so the compiler deletes the call here and the build that defines X never compiles the statement at all."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The conditionally compiled call sits under a directive that deletes it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2220.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2221",
              "name": "UnsafeAccessorTargetMismatch",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`UnsafeAccessorAttribute` is bound by the runtime, not by the compiler. The member name is a string, nothing checks it, and a typo compiles cleanly and then throws the first time the accessor is called — usually in production, because the accessor is on a path the tests do not take. It is a compile-time-knowable fact reported at run time, which is the trade an analyzer exists to undo.\n\n⚠ **Every shape this rule reports was confirmed by running it, not by reading the documentation.** On .NET 10, a name the target does not declare throws `MissingFieldException` — *Field not found: 'Target._buffer'*; a `Field` kind naming a member that is a method throws the same; and `UnsafeAccessorKind.Constructor` with `Name = \"Create\"` throws **`BadImageFormatException`, \"Invalid usage of UnsafeAccessorAttribute\"**, which is harsher still. The correctly spelled field accessor and the unnamed constructor accessor both succeeded in the same run, so the probe was measuring the rule's subject rather than a broken harness. That is what justifies `error` severity."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An [UnsafeAccessor] declaration names a member the target type does not declare, or names a field where the target has a method, so the runtime binds nothing and the first call throws."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `[UnsafeAccessor]` names a member its target does not have"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2221.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2222",
              "name": "PartiallyCheckedOperatorSet",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ \"This type has no checked operator\" is an observation; \"this type has checked on some operators and not others\" is a defect, and the difference is the entire specification. A type that declares no `checked` operator has simply not opted into C# 11's user-defined checked arithmetic, and `checked` around it means what it always meant — there is nothing to report and no way to know whether overflow matters here. A type that declares `operator checked +` and not `operator checked -` has stated, in its own source, that overflow on this type is meant to trap; and then `checked(a - b)` wraps silently on the line below the `checked(a + b)` that throws. The evidence for the author's intent comes from the type itself, so the rule never has to guess."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type declares a user-defined `checked` operator for one of its arithmetic operators and not for another that has a checked form, so inside one `checked` block overflow throws on one operator of that type and silently wraps on the next."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The type declares `checked` for some of its operators and not the rest"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2222.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "11.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2230",
              "name": "SqlFragmentsRunTogether",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The statement was split across two lines to keep it readable and the space that separated the two halves went with the line break. Nothing catches it: it compiles, it is a perfectly good `string`, and the failure arrives at execution as a syntax error from the database naming a token the source file does not contain. ⚠ **The reason this is worth a rule rather than a test is where the failure lands.** A query built this way is usually on a branch — the paging clause, the optional filter, the retry path — so it is the one call the smoke test does not make, and the defect ships. ⚠ **`SK5001` reports the opposite half of the same subject and the two are disjoint by construction.** `SK5001` fires only when a value that crossed a trust boundary reaches the SQL; this rule fires only when every fragment is a written literal, and a literal is never tainted. A query can be injectable, or malformed, or both, and each finding comes from a different rule with a different fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two string literals are concatenated and the join fuses a word: `\"select * from users\" + \"where id = 1\"` is `... usersWHERE id = 1`, which no database will parse."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The concatenated SQL fragments run together"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2230.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2231",
              "name": "CommandParameterNotSupplied",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The marker and the binding are two edits in two places and only one of them was made. It is the same join a format string and its arguments make, against a different grammar, and it has the same failure shape: the compiler sees a `string` on one side and a method call on the other and has no reason to relate them. ⚠ **Nothing upstream implements this** — the SonarSource issue it comes from is an open idea rather than a shipped rule — so the whole of the specification is the restriction list below. ⚠ **`SK5001` does not overlap it.** `SK5001` needs a tainted value reaching the SQL and says nothing about a constant; this rule requires the text to be *entirely* constant before it will read it, so no string can satisfy both."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A command's text is a constant naming `@a` and `@b`, the same method adds `@a`, and `@b` is never supplied — which the provider reports at execution and nothing reports at build."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The command's SQL names a parameter nothing supplies"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2231.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2232",
              "name": "AssemblyLoadedOutsideItsContext",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The override exists to place an assembly in *this* context. `Assembly.LoadFrom` places it in the default one and `Assembly.LoadFile` places it in a brand new anonymous one, so in both cases the context whose resolver was asked ends up not holding the assembly it resolved. The symptom is the one the isolation was bought to prevent: the same assembly is now present twice, and a type from one copy will not cast to the identically-named type from the other — `InvalidCastException` with a message that prints the same type name on both sides. ⚠ **This is the sound core of `S3885`, and the broad reading of that rule is deliberately not implemented.** \"`Assembly.Load` should be used\" reported everywhere would report every plugin host in existence: `LoadFrom` against a path is exactly right when the default context is where the assembly belongs, and which context an assembly belongs in is intent, not a fact in the file. Inside a `Load` override the intent *is* stated — by the override existing — so this is the one position where the question has an answer."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `AssemblyLoadContext.Load` override returns `Assembly.LoadFrom` or `Assembly.LoadFile`, which loads into some other context and leaves this one empty."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The load context's own resolver loads outside it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2232.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2233",
              "name": "MistakenTypeArgument",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The API states which type it needs in its own documentation and enforces it with a run-time `ArgumentException`, and `typeof(…)` states which type it was given right there in the source. Both halves of the contradiction are visible at build time and the compiler relates neither to the other, because `Type` is `Type`. ⚠ **The failure is total, not conditional**: there is no input on which `Enum.GetValues(typeof(Widget))` succeeds, so this is not a risk being flagged but a line that cannot work. That is what makes it worth an `error`-adjacent severity on a rule that never guesses. ⚠ **`SK2181` is the neighbouring half and does not overlap.** `SK2181` reports `GetType()` called on something that is *already* a `Type` — the wrong *operation*. `SK2182` reports a type identified by comparing its name to a string — the wrong *test*. This one reports the wrong *type*, in a position where the API says what the right one would have to be."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Enum.GetValues(typeof(Widget))` on a type that is not an enum, `Attribute.GetCustomAttribute(m, typeof(Widget))` on a type that is not an attribute, `Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(IWidget))` on an interface — each throws on every input."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `Type` passed cannot satisfy what the API asks for"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2233.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2240",
              "name": "WithExpressionRewritesAll",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`p with { X = a, Y = b }` on a `record Point(int X, int Y)` is `new Point(a, b)` spelled longer: nothing of `p` survives it. The cost is not the ceremony, it is what happens next — add a member to the record and the `with` silently starts carrying that member across from `p`, which is the one value the author had already decided not to keep. The constructor call cannot do that: adding a parameter is a compile error at every call site, which is the whole point of writing it that way. ⚠ **The question is decidable and is asked as a set comparison, not as a shape match**: the names assigned by the initializer against the positional parameters of the record's primary constructor. That is why the rule is `Semantic` — the parameter list is a fact about the symbol, not about the page."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `with` expression assigns every positional parameter of the record it copies, so it constructs a wholly new value through the copy constructor and reads as a copy of the old one."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `with` expression rewrites every member"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2240.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2241",
              "name": "MalformedRegexPattern",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`new Regex(\"(\")` compiles, ships, and throws on the line that first executes it. The pattern is a constant, so whether it parses was decided when it was typed — nothing about the program's inputs can change the answer — and the only thing standing between the defect and the user is whether a test happened to reach that line. ⚠ **Finding it without running it is the entire value of the rule, and the measurement says nothing else does.** A probe built outside this repository, with empty `Directory.Build.props`/`.targets` above it, reports nothing at all on `new Regex(\"(\")`, `new Regex(\"[a-\")`, `new Regex(\"*abc\")` and `Regex.IsMatch(s, \"(\")` — in the SDK's default state, and still nothing with `AnalysisMode=All`. ⚠ **The oracle is `Regex` itself, not a parser of this rule's own.** The analyzer constructs the pattern with the same options the call passes and reports what the constructor threw, so the rule cannot disagree with the runtime it is predicting. Parsing is not matching, so no pattern can make the analyzer backtrack."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A compile-time-constant pattern is passed to a `System.Text.RegularExpressions` API that will reject it, so the call throws an `ArgumentException` the first time it runs."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The regular expression pattern does not parse"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2241.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2242",
              "name": "DeferredArgumentCheck",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A method containing `yield` does not run when it is called. The call returns an enumerator and the body starts at the first `MoveNext`, which means the guard at the top of it — the one written precisely so that a bad argument is rejected *early* — is the one part of the method guaranteed not to be early. The `ArgumentNullException` surfaces inside a `foreach` somewhere else entirely, with a stack trace naming the consumer rather than the caller that broke the contract, and a caller that never enumerates never sees it at all. ⚠ **`yield` anywhere in the body makes the whole method lazy**, including the statements above the `yield`, which is what makes this decidable rather than a heuristic: there is no execution in which the guard runs at call time. The repair is the two-method shape — an eager wrapper that validates and returns, and a private iterator that yields."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An iterator method validates an argument before its first `yield`, so the exception is not thrown by the call that passed the bad argument but by whatever later enumerates the result."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The argument check does not run until the result is enumerated"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2242.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK2290",
              "name": "DiscardedOutParameter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `out` parameter is a second return value the callee is obliged to assign, and the body usually does real work to produce it — a lookup, a parse, a computed span. When *every* call site spells `out _`, that obligation is paid and the result is thrown away at every point in the program that could have read it, so the computation is dead across the whole call graph. ⚠ **This is the callee's finding, and it is a different fact from `SK6040`'s.** `SK6040` looks at one call site and says the *variable* declared there is never read; this looks at the declaration and says *no* caller anywhere reads the value. Neither implies the other, and only the second says the method's own body is computing something nobody wants — which is the difference between tidying a call and deleting a computation.\n\n⚠ **`private` is not a taste; it is what makes the question decidable.** A `public` method's callers live in assemblies that do not exist yet, so \"every caller discards it\" is unanswerable — that is ReSharper's `.Global` half, and it is excluded rather than attempted ([#324](https://github.com/Rikarin/SKALA/issues/324))."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `private` method computes a value into an `out` parameter that every call site in the compilation writes to a discard, so the work behind it is dead."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every call discards this `out` parameter's value"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK2290.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Correctness",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3001",
              "name": "AsyncVoidMethod",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `async void` method cannot be awaited, so its caller cannot know when it finished and cannot observe its exceptions. An exception thrown after the first `await` is raised on whatever synchronization context resumed the method, which in a console or server process means an unhandled exception that terminates it. `async Task` gives the caller both. The one legitimate use is an event handler, whose delegate type returns `void` and whose exceptions the framework expects to handle, so the rule is silent there."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `async void` method that nothing subscribes as an event handler."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`async void` outside an event handler"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3002",
              "name": "BlockingOnAsync",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Blocking a thread on a task is the classic ASP.NET and WPF deadlock: the continuation needs the synchronization context the blocked thread is holding, and neither side moves again. Even where it does not deadlock it consumes a pool thread for the duration, which under load is how a server stops responding to anything at all. It also re-wraps the exception in an `AggregateException`, so the `catch` a reader expects to fire does not. `await` does none of these things."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`.Result`, `.Wait()` or `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on a `Task` or `ValueTask`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Blocking on an async call"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3003",
              "name": "MissingConfigureAwait",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Library code can choose not to capture its caller's synchronization context. The existing skala_configure_await_analysis_mode policy determines whether this inspection applies; disabled, missing, invalid and UI mode do not require missing configuration. Explicit ConfigureAwait choices are respected. Scheduling policy is an author decision, so this rule reports without an automatic fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A bare framework Task or ValueTask is awaited where EditorConfig explicitly selects library ConfigureAwait analysis."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Configure awaited tasks in library code"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3004",
              "name": "CancellationTokenNotForwarded",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A cancellation token that stops at the first frame is a cancellation that does not happen. The caller sees the token accepted, assumes the operation is cancellable, and gets a request that runs to completion after the client has gone — which under load is how a server keeps working on answers nobody is waiting for. The chain has to be complete to be worth anything, and the place it breaks is always a call that offered the parameter and was not given it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A method takes a `CancellationToken` and calls something that offers an optional one without supplying it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `CancellationToken` is accepted and not passed on"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3005",
              "name": "FireAndForgetTask",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Discarding a task means the caller observes neither completion nor failure. An exception can surface later as an unobserved fault while the synchronous method has already reported success."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A task-producing invocation is used as a bare statement in a synchronous body."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A task is discarded in synchronous code"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3007",
              "name": "TaskReturnedFromUsing",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Returning a task out of a `using` disposes the resource at the `return`, not when the task finishes. The task is then running against a disposed stream, connection or scope, and what the caller gets is an `ObjectDisposedException` from somewhere with no visible connection to the `using` — or, worse, a task that completes because the operation had already finished, so the bug reproduces only under load. Awaiting inside the block is the whole repair, and because the method already returns a task, adding `async` changes its signature not at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A non-`async` method returns a task built from a `using` variable, so the resource is disposed before the task completes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `Task` that uses a `using` resource is returned instead of awaited"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3009",
              "name": "SharedLazy",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Lazy<T> is thread-safe by default. Passing false or LazyThreadSafetyMode.None explicitly removes that protection; storing that instance in static state warrants review of its access policy. Adding synchronization changes factory execution and exception behavior, so there is no automatic fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A static Lazy<T> field initializer explicitly disables thread safety."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review explicitly unsynchronized Lazy in static state"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3009.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3020",
              "name": "NullReturnedFromTaskMethod",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `Task` return type invites the caller to write `await Something()`, and awaiting null is a `NullReferenceException` whose stack names the caller rather than the method that produced it. An `async` method cannot do this — the compiler wraps whatever it returns — so the shape exists only where the signature was written by hand, and there `return null;` looks exactly like every other \"nothing to do\" in the file. `Task.CompletedTask` is the completed task the caller was promised."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A method whose declared return type is `Task` or `Task<T>` and which is not `async` returns `null` or `default`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The non-`async` `Task` method returns null"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3021",
              "name": "SpinLockInReadonlyField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SpinLock` is a mutable struct and its state *is* the lock. `Enter` is not a `readonly` member, so reading a `readonly` field to call it makes the compiler take a defensive copy: the lock is acquired on a temporary and released on a temporary, and nothing is ever excluded. It compiles, it runs, `lockTaken` comes back true every time, and the race the lock was written to prevent is exactly as it was — with the code above it saying otherwise. Removing `readonly` is the only repair."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A field of type `System.Threading.SpinLock` is declared `readonly`, so every caller takes the lock on a defensive copy."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `SpinLock` is stored in a `readonly` field"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3030",
              "name": "AsyncIteratorNotEnumerated",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` handed to a plain `foreach` does not compile, which is why the mistake takes this shape instead: the iterator is invoked and the result discarded. That compiles, warns about nothing, and does nothing — an async iterator does not start until something enumerates it, so not one statement of the method's body executes. The call reads as work being done and no work is done, which is the failure that survives every test that only checks for an exception."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A method returning `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` is called as a whole statement and the result dropped, so the iterator's body never runs."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The async iterator is enumerated without `await foreach`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3031",
              "name": "AsyncOnlyToAwait",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `async` method compiles to a state machine, an allocation and a resumption path. Where the entire body is one `await` whose result is returned unchanged, all of that exists to give the caller a task the method was holding already. Returning it directly removes the machinery and does the same work."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The method's whole body is `return await X()`, so the `async` state machine exists only to hand back a task the method already had."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The method is `async` only to return an awaited task"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3040",
              "name": "LockOverSynchronizationPrimitive",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every reference type carries a monitor, so `lock (semaphore)` compiles. It gives the author a monitor they did not mean, taken over an object whose own waiting happens somewhere else entirely: a thread parked inside `semaphore.Wait()` holds nothing the monitor knows about, and a thread inside `lock (semaphore)` excludes nobody who calls `Wait`. The two mechanisms do not compose, and where the primitive takes its own monitor internally the mistake is a deadlock rather than a no-op. The code above it reads as though the object were guarded, which is the expensive part."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `lock` statement is taken over an object that is itself a synchronization primitive — a `SemaphoreSlim`, a `ReaderWriterLockSlim`, or anything below `WaitHandle`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `lock` is taken over a synchronization primitive"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3041",
              "name": "NonAtomicVolatileUpdate",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`volatile` buys visibility and orders nothing else. The read and the write of `counter++` are still two instructions with a window between them, so two threads read the same value and one update is lost — exactly as they would without the keyword. What makes this worth a warning rather than a hint is the keyword itself: it is the mark of an author who thought about threading and concluded wrongly, so the next reader trusts the field and does not look again."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`++`, `--` or a compound assignment is applied to a `volatile` field, which is a read and a write and not one operation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The compound operation on a `volatile` field is not atomic"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3042",
              "name": "IncorrectDoubleCheckedLocking",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The idiom exists to skip the lock on the common path, and the read it skips the lock for is the outer one. Without `volatile` nothing orders that read against the writes the constructor performed, so a second thread can see a non-null reference to an object whose fields it cannot see yet, and use it. The lock is real, the inner check is real, and the object is still half-built. ⚠ It works on x86 and x64 by accident of those processors' ordering and stops working on ARM64, which is where a great deal of .NET now runs — so this is not a fragile shape, it is a shape with a platform on which it is already wrong."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A check-lock-check initialization reads its field outside the lock and the field is not `volatile`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The double-checked locking is not correct"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3042.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3043",
              "name": "InconsistentLockOrder",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two paths that take the same pair of locks in opposite orders deadlock the moment both run. It survives review because the two halves are almost never on the same screen: each method reads as a perfectly ordinary pair of nested locks, and only the two together are wrong. Nothing in Roslyn or the `CA*` set reports it. The analysis is a lock *order* and nothing more — every `lock` whose target is a field of the type contributes an ordered pair for each lock it is nested inside, and a pair present in both directions is the finding."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One type nests the same two lock fields inside each other in both orders, which is the textbook deadlock."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Locks are taken in inconsistent orders"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3043.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3044",
              "name": "InconsistentlySynchronizedField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A field taken under a lock in nine places and written bare in the tenth is a race that testing will not find: the tenth path is usually the one added last and the window is usually nanoseconds wide. The analysis is which lock, if any, is held at each access — the same analysis `SK3043` needs, computed across the whole type rather than one file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private field is written without the type's one lock, and is accessed under that lock at least twice elsewhere."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The field is guarded on some paths and not others"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3044.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3050",
              "name": "AsyncVoidThrow",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `async void` method has nowhere to put an exception. There is no task to fault, so the compiler-generated builder hands it to whatever `SynchronizationContext` was captured — and where there is none, which is every console application and every thread-pool callback, that is an unhandled exception on a pool thread and the process ends. The caller's `try`/`catch` never sees it, because the caller returned at the first `await`. A `catch` that logs and then rethrows is therefore strictly worse than no handler at all: it turns a recorded failure into a terminated process."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `throw` inside an `async void` method or local function, where nothing between it and the body's edge can catch it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exception is thrown out of an `async void` body"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3050.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3051",
              "name": "AsyncMethodWithoutCancellation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SK3004` reports a token accepted and not passed on. This reports the step before it: the method accepts none, so there is nothing to pass on and no caller can stop the work. The two are the same argument at two points in the call graph — a cancellation chain is worth nothing until it is complete, and the place it breaks first is the frame that never offered the parameter. ⚠ **The fix appends the parameter and passes it to every call in the body that can take one, in one edit.** It used to append the parameter and stop, which left a signature advertising a cancellation the body dropped — and silenced this rule, because the rule looks for the parameter and the parameter was there."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `async` method that accepts no `CancellationToken` and calls something that would have taken one."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `async` method has no `CancellationToken` to forward"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3051.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3052",
              "name": "AsyncVoidLambda",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A lambda has no written return type: it takes the one the target delegate asks for. `Register(async () => await SaveAsync())` against a `Register(Action)` therefore compiles into an `async void` with every one of its consequences — `Register` returns the instant the lambda reaches its first `await`, the rest of the body runs unobserved, and an exception in it goes to the synchronization context rather than to anyone who could catch it. Nothing in the source says `void`, which is why reading the code does not find it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `async` lambda or anonymous method whose target delegate returns `void`, which makes it `async void`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `async` lambda is converted to a `void`-returning delegate"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3052.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3060",
              "name": "UnreleasedLock",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `lock` keyword compiles to `Monitor.Enter` followed by `try`/`finally` `Monitor.Exit`, and that pairing is the whole reason the keyword exists. Writing the two calls by hand and putting the release on the happy path means the first exception inside the critical section keeps the lock forever: the next thread to ask for it blocks, and the one after that, and the process deadlocks somewhere entirely unrelated to the throw. ⚠ **The failure has no diagnostic anywhere** — the exception propagates normally and the deadlock happens later, in another thread, in another file, so nothing connects the two."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `Monitor.Enter` or a `ReaderWriterLockSlim` enter call whose matching release is not inside a `finally`, so anything that throws in between leaves the lock held for the life of the process."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The lock is entered and not released on every path"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3060.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3061",
              "name": "IneffectiveLockTarget",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `lock` excludes exactly the threads that take the same monitor, and the monitor lives on the object — so which object it is, is the entire contract. Both shapes here break it in the same way and read as correct: a local initialized with `new` gives every invocation its own monitor, so the critical section excludes nobody and the whole construct is a no-op; a field the type reassigns gives whoever entered before the reassignment a different monitor from whoever enters after, so two threads sit inside the same `lock` body at the same time. ⚠ **Neither produces a symptom that points at the lock.** The code compiles, the critical section is right there in the file, and the corruption it was supposed to prevent shows up as a data bug somewhere else."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `lock` taken over an object created in the same invocation, or over a field the type reassigns, so the monitor it takes is not the monitor another thread takes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `lock` target is not the object other threads take"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3061.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3062",
              "name": "ConstructorPublishesThis",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A constructor's body runs before the derived constructors, before the readonly fields are visible to other threads under a weak memory model, and before anything the caller was going to do with the finished object. Publishing `this` from inside it hands somebody a reference to an object that is not there yet. ⚠ **The three destinations here are the ones where the second reader is not under the constructor's control**: static state outlives every instance, and a thread the constructor starts races the rest of the constructor by construction. On ARM64 the second thread can legally observe a field before its initializer, which is the failure that never reproduces on the developer's x64 machine."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A constructor stores `this` in another type's static state, hands it to an object reached through a static field, subscribes it to a static event, or captures it in a delegate it starts on another thread."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`this` escapes before the constructor finishes"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3062.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Async",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3501",
              "name": "DisposableNotDisposed",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A disposable that is never disposed holds its handle until a finalizer runs, if it has one, and for ever if it does not. The visible symptoms — a file that cannot be reopened, a connection pool that empties, a socket in `CLOSE_WAIT` — appear far from the method that leaked and under load rather than in a test. The repair is one keyword, because C# 8's `using` declaration disposes at the end of exactly the scope the local already has."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`var x = new SomethingDisposable();` where the local never leaves the method and is never disposed."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A disposable is created in a local and never disposed"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3501.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3502",
              "name": "OwnedDisposableField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type that constructs and retains a disposable resource owns its lifetime. If the owner cannot itself be disposed, callers have no reliable point at which to release the retained handle or asynchronously flush it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An instance field is constructed by its owner, but the containing type exposes no matching disposal contract."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type owns a disposable field but is not disposable"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3502.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3503",
              "name": "AsyncDisposableDisposedSynchronously",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type implements `IAsyncDisposable` because its cleanup does something that has to be waited for — a final flush, a graceful close, a return to a pool. Its synchronous `Dispose` therefore either blocks the thread on that work or skips it, and both are decided by the type rather than by the caller. Taking the asynchronous path is how the caller stops depending on which one this type chose."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A plain `using` on a type that offers `DisposeAsync`, inside a body where `await using` would compile."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An `IAsyncDisposable` is disposed synchronously"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3503.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.2",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3510",
              "name": "UsingVariableDisposedAgain",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `using` disposes its resource when the scope ends, on every path including the exceptional one, so an explicit `Dispose()` on the same variable is a second call that the language guarantees will happen. Double disposal is only harmless for a type whose `Dispose` is idempotent — the framework asks for that and not every type delivers it, and a second call that releases a handle number the process has since reissued corrupts an unrelated file rather than throwing. Deleting the call removes the second disposal without changing when the first one happens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Dispose()` is called by hand on a variable whose `using` disposes it at the end of the scope anyway."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A variable already owned by `using` is disposed again"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3510.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3511",
              "name": "UsingResourceObjectInitializer",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `using` protects what is assigned to its variable, and an object initializer runs after the constructor and before that assignment. So `using var x = new Foo { Bar = Baz() }` has a window in which a fully constructed `Foo` — with its handle, its socket, its pooled buffer — exists and nothing owns it: if `Baz()` throws, the object is unreachable, no `Dispose` ever reaches it, and the code reads as if the `using` covered it. The leak is invisible in every test where the initializer does not throw, which is all of them. Constructing first and assigning afterwards moves the assignment inside the scope the `using` protects, and it is a mechanical edit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`using var x = new Foo { Bar = Baz() };` — the object initializer runs before the `using` owns anything, so an initializer that throws leaks the constructed resource."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `using` resource is built with an object initializer"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3511.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3512",
              "name": "UsingVariableReturned",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `using` disposes at the `return`, before the value reaches the caller, so the method's signature and its body disagree: it says \"here is a resource\" and delivers one that is already closed. The caller gets an `ObjectDisposedException` on first use, from a method that reads as correct — or, for a type whose `Dispose` only returns a pooled buffer, no exception at all and silent corruption once that buffer is handed to somebody else."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`return x;` where `x` is owned by an enclosing `using`, so the caller receives an object that was disposed on the way out."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A variable captured by `using` is returned"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3512.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3530",
              "name": "DisposableFieldNotDisposed",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This is the ownership bug that looks finished. `SK3502` reports a type that owns a disposable and is not disposable, which reads as work somebody has not got to yet; here the interface is declared, the `Dispose` is written, and the handle is still never released — so the type reads as one that cleans up after itself to every caller, every reviewer and every tool that checks only for the declaration. What shows up instead is a file that cannot be reopened or a pool that empties, in a process that disposed everything it was supposed to."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The type implements `IDisposable`, constructs a disposable field, and nothing in the type ever disposes it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The disposable field is not disposed by `Dispose`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3530.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3531",
              "name": "DisposeAsyncWithoutBaseCall",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Asynchronous disposal is a chain: `DisposeAsync` calls a virtual `DisposeAsyncCore`, and each override finishes by calling the one it overrode. An override that skips the base breaks the chain at that link — the base type's final flush, its graceful close, its return to a pool simply stop running. Nothing says so. There is no compiler diagnostic, no test that fails, and the type still satisfies `IAsyncDisposable`; the resource is released when the process ends, or not at all. `CA1063` pins the synchronous pattern and `CA2215` pins the synchronous base call, and neither has an asynchronous counterpart."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `override` of `DisposeAsync` or `DisposeAsyncCore` never reaches the implementation it replaced, so the base type's asynchronous cleanup stops happening."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `DisposeAsync` override never calls the base implementation"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3531.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3532",
              "name": "RefStructOwnsUndisposedResource",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This is the one ownership shape the rest of the family cannot reach. A `ref struct` whose only disposal contract is a public parameterless `Dispose()` is disposable through the language's pattern rule and through nothing else — it implements no interface, so `SK3502`, which asks whether the owner implements the contract the field offers, has no contract to ask about and stays silent by construction. The owner constructs the resource, holds it, and offers its own callers no `using` to write: the release simply never happens, and no analyzer in the family reports it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `ref struct` constructs a field whose `ref struct` type is disposable through the pattern rule alone, and declares no `Dispose()` of its own."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `ref struct` owns a disposable and cannot declare it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3532.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3540",
              "name": "DisposeMethodWithoutInterface",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The cleanup is already written. It is public, it is spelled the way the framework spells it, and the base list does not say so — which means `using` will not bind to it, `is IDisposable` is false, a DI container's teardown walks past it, and every reviewer who sees the method assumes the release happens. This is the failure that looks finished from every angle except the one that matters. ⚠ **It is the other half of `SK3502`'s question, asked of a different declaration.** `SK3502` reads a *field* — the type constructs a disposable and offers no matching disposal — and says nothing about how the type cleans up; this reads a *method*, and says the cleanup exists and is unreachable. A type can be reported by both, and when it is, the two are saying different things: one that the resource is owned, one that the release is undeclared."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The type declares a public parameterless `Dispose()` that releases resources and does not implement `IDisposable`, so nothing the language or the framework does will ever call it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `Dispose` method the type never declares"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3540.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3541",
              "name": "ShortLivedHttpClient",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`HttpClient` is the one disposable in the framework whose correct lifetime is the opposite of every other one's. Disposing it closes the connection pool it holds, and the sockets sit in `TIME_WAIT` for the operating system's timeout afterwards — so a handler called under load exhausts the ephemeral port range and starts failing with `SocketException` on a call that worked yesterday, on a machine that is not the author's. ⚠ **The whole family this rule lives in says \"this is not disposed\" and this one says \"this is disposed\".** `SK3501`, `SK3502`, `SK3530` and `SK3532` each report a release that is missing; here the release is present, is what the shape of every other disposable asks for, and is the defect. That inversion is why it cannot be an exception inside one of theirs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `using` owns a directly constructed `HttpClient`, so the connection pool underneath it is closed once per call."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `HttpClient` is disposed at the end of the call"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3541.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK3542",
              "name": "DangerousHandleWithoutRefCount",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SafeHandle` exists to make one failure impossible: the finalizer releasing a handle while something is still using the value, after which the operating system reuses that number and every later call operates on whatever now owns it. `DangerousGetHandle` returns the value out from under that protection, and `DangerousAddRef`/`DangerousRelease` are what put it back for the duration of the use. A raw handle taken with neither is protected by nothing at all, and the failure it produces — a read from a file somebody else now owns — is not one a stack trace explains."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`SafeHandle.DangerousGetHandle` is called and the declaring type never touches the reference count that would keep the handle alive."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`DangerousGetHandle` with nothing holding the handle open"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK3542.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Lifetime",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4001",
              "name": "HotPathLinq",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "There is no reliable syntactic way to identify a hot path. This rule is disabled by default; enable dotnet_diagnostic.SK4001.severity in a chosen EditorConfig path section after measuring that code. LINQ is not inherently slow, and this rule does not claim every call allocates or should be replaced."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An explicitly enabled hot-path policy reports framework Enumerable calls for cost review."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review LINQ in a configured hot path"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4002",
              "name": "LoopClosure",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Capturing a foreach iteration variable or a local declared in a loop body can create a closure environment for each iteration. The diagnostic identifies the capture, not a measured heap allocation count: compiler and JIT optimizations vary. A hot path may benefit from an explicit-state API, but hoisting or reusing the closure can change retained values and is not automatically safe."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A lambda or anonymous delegate in a loop body captures fresh iteration-local storage."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review iteration-local delegate captures"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4003",
              "name": "ParamsSpanOverload",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A span-based call may avoid the temporary array. The analyzer speculatively binds an explicit ReadOnlySpan collection expression and verifies an accessible matching overload; it does not assume two overload bodies have identical semantics."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An explicit temporary array passed to a params parameter has an accessible ReadOnlySpan overload."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review a temporary params array with a span overload"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "12.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4004",
              "name": "ConstrainedBoxing",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A constrained generic call can often dispatch without boxing. An explicit cast to the already-provided interface introduces a boxing conversion. Removing it is not automatically safe: a mutating struct method may then modify the original value instead of the boxed copy, and overload selection can differ."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An explicit interface cast boxes a value-type-constrained type parameter before an interface method call, despite an existing matching constraint."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review boxing despite an existing generic constraint"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4006",
              "name": "ImmediateMaterialization",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The temporary collection is not retained or indexed; it is used only to enumerate once. Removing it may save work, but can also remove an intentional snapshot and change exception timing or interactions with mutations. The rule therefore offers a review hint without an automatic fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A framework ToList or ToArray temporary over a stable local or parameter is consumed directly by foreach."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review a materialization used only by foreach"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4006.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4007",
              "name": "LargeStructArgument",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repeated by-value calls can copy a substantial struct. This rule uses a target-independent lower bound from primitive fields and same-file nested structs, not an ABI layout guess or Marshal.SizeOf. A by-reference API may reduce copying but can change aliasing, mutation and overload behavior, so the rule is report-only."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A source struct with known field payload exceeding dotnet_code_quality.SK4007.threshold (default 64 bytes) is passed by value in a loop body."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review large struct arguments in loops"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4010",
              "name": "WhereBeforeLinqOperator",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Enumerable.Where` allocates an iterator that the next operator then wraps and drives, so every element crosses two `MoveNext` frames instead of one. `First`, `FirstOrDefault`, `Last`, `LastOrDefault`, `Single`, `SingleOrDefault`, `Any`, `Count` and `LongCount` each have an overload that takes the predicate directly and does the same work in one loop with no intermediate object. LINQ defines the two forms to select the same elements in the same order for the same predicate, so this is a rewrite with no semantic content — which is why it is worth a rule rather than a review comment: the longer form reads no better and costs an indirection per element."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`xs.Where(p).First()` is `xs.First(p)`: one fewer iterator object and one fewer delegate hop per element."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `Where` the next operator could have taken as its predicate"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4020",
              "name": "NoncapturingLambda",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A lambda that captures a local or `this` is compiled into a display class, and every evaluation of the lambda expression allocates one — or, where the compiler can cache it, keeps the captured object alive for the lifetime of the cache. A lambda that captures nothing has neither cost, and the compiler is free to cache one delegate instance for the whole program. `static` is the keyword that says so and the compiler that checks it: the modifier is a compile-time assertion that the body references no enclosing local, parameter, `this` or `base`, so a `static` lambda that compiles is a lambda that provably allocates no environment. That makes it the rare performance edit with no judgement in it — the fix is one keyword, the compiler verifies the claim, and a later edit that starts capturing is rejected at the point it is written instead of silently reintroducing the allocation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A lambda, anonymous method or local function that references nothing outside itself can be `static`, and then it cannot allocate a closure."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The lambda captures nothing and is not `static`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "9.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4021",
              "name": "StatelessPrivateMethod",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An instance call passes a `this` argument the method does not read and, through a reference, carries a null check the callee cannot avoid. Making the method `static` removes both, and the declaration then states the fact the body already established: this code depends on its arguments and on nothing else. That is the larger half of the value — a reader no longer has to read the body to learn whether the method participates in the object's state, and a later edit that starts using a field is rejected at the point it is written. The rule is deliberately restricted to `private` methods, where the change is invisible outside the type; `static` on a visible member is an API change and a different decision."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private instance method whose body never touches `this` can be `static`, which drops the hidden argument and the null check."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The private method does not use instance state"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4022",
              "name": "ImmutableStructNotReadonly",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The compiler cannot know that a struct's members leave it alone, so wherever the struct is reached through storage it may not write — a `readonly` field, an `in` parameter, a `ref readonly` return, a `foreach` variable — it copies the whole value before every member call. `SK2005` and `SK4007` report two consequences of exactly that. The `readonly` modifier is the declaration that removes the doubt: the compiler verifies that no member writes to the instance and then stops emitting the copies. The keyword is not a hint or a tuning parameter; it is a fact about the type that the type already satisfied and had not written down."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A struct with only readonly state and no member that assigns `this` can be `readonly`, which deletes the defensive copies."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The struct is never mutated and is not `readonly`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4022.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4023",
              "name": "RedundantCapacityArgument",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A capacity argument is how an author records a measurement: this collection is known to hold about this many elements, so do not grow it from nothing. An argument equal to the type's own default records no measurement and makes no allocation smaller — it reads as a deliberate tuning decision to the next person, who then has to open the framework source to discover that it decides nothing. Removing it is a deletion with no runtime effect at all, which is the unusual case where the value is entirely in what the code no longer claims."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`new List<T>(0)` and `new StringBuilder(16)` ask for the capacity the parameterless constructor already gives."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The capacity argument matches the default"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4023.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4024",
              "name": "ForcedGarbageCollection",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`GC.Collect` is almost always added after a memory graph looked wrong, and it is the one response that cannot help. The collector's generational assumption is that most objects die young; a collection forced at an arbitrary moment finds the young objects still live, promotes them to a longer-lived generation, and thereby guarantees that the next collection to reach them is a more expensive one. Meanwhile every managed thread is suspended for the duration. The cost is paid at every call and the benefit is a number in a profiler that looks better for one sample. What the call is standing in for is an allocation that should not happen, a buffer that should be pooled, or a handle that should be disposed — and none of those is an edit an analyzer can write, which is why this rule reports and does not fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A forced collection suspends every thread and promotes what survives, so it usually makes the pressure it was added to relieve permanent."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`GC.Collect` is called from application code"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4024.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4030",
              "name": "CollectionOwnMethod",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`FirstOrDefault`, `Any` and `All` are written against `IEnumerable<T>`, so they allocate an enumerator, drive it through `MoveNext` and call the predicate through it. `Find`, `Exists` and `TrueForAll` are members of the list, so they walk the backing array with an index and allocate nothing. The delegate is the same object under two names — `Predicate<T>` and `Func<T, bool>` have identical signatures — so the substitution costs the reader nothing either. `Any(x => x == v)` is a third case of the same argument and the sharpest: `Contains(v)` needs no delegate at all, and the comparison ends up somewhere the reader can see it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`List<T>` and `ImmutableList<T>` declare `Find`, `Exists` and `TrueForAll`; the `Enumerable` extension that answers the same question allocates an enumerator to do it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection's own method answers this faster than the LINQ extension"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4031",
              "name": "DictionaryKeyRelookupInLoop",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Enumerating `Keys` walks the same entries the dictionary would have handed over whole, and then throws the value away. Every `dict[key]` in the body hashes the key again, probes the bucket chain again and compares again — work the enumerator already did on the way past, multiplied by the count of the dictionary. Iterating the dictionary itself and deconstructing the pair is one pass with no hashing at all. `SK1033` reports the `ContainsKey`-then-index double lookup; this is the same waste inside a loop, which is where it is worth a `warning` rather than a suggestion."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`foreach (var key in dict.Keys)` and then `dict[key]` in the body: the entry was already in hand and is hashed and probed for again, once per element."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The loop looks up a key it is already holding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4032",
              "name": "SubstringBeforeIndexSearch",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Substring` is an allocation and a copy proportional to what is left of the string, and the only thing done with the result here is a search that already accepts the offset as an argument. `IndexOf(value, startIndex)` and `IndexOfAny(anyOf, startIndex)` begin exactly where the copy would have begun and never build it. `SK1028` makes the span-based version of the same argument; this one needs no new type at the call site at all, which is why it is the first thing to reach for."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`s.Substring(n).IndexOf(x)` copies the tail of the string onto the heap only to search inside it; `s.IndexOf(x, n)` reads the same characters in place."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`Substring` is called to feed a search that takes a start index"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4032.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4033",
              "name": "ConcurrentDictionaryExpensiveMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On every other dictionary `Keys` and `Values` are views and `Count` is a field read. On this one they are neither. `ConcurrentDictionary.Keys` acquires every lock in the table and copies the whole collection into a fresh `List<TKey>` before the caller reads anything at all, so `dict.Keys.Count` is a locking O(n) allocation. `Count` acquires every lock as well, because a consistent total means stopping the table. `IsEmpty` acquires nothing and allocates nothing. The receiver is what makes the difference enormous rather than marginal, and it is invisible in the shape of the call — which is exactly why it is worth a rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`dict.Keys.Count` locks the whole table and copies every key into a new list to produce a number `dict.Count` already has; `dict.Count == 0` locks it to answer what `dict.IsEmpty` answers for free."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `ConcurrentDictionary` member taken is the expensive one"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4033.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4034",
              "name": "SortBeforeFilter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`OrderBy` is not lazy in the way the rest of the pipeline is: it buffers the entire sequence, computes a key for every element and sorts all of them before it yields the first one. Putting `Where` after it means paying `O(n log n)` on elements that are about to be discarded, and holding a buffer and a key array sized to the input rather than to the output. Swapping the two makes every one of those proportional to what survives — the one rewrite in this range that moves an asymptotic term rather than an allocation count."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`xs.OrderBy(k).Where(p)` buffers, keys and sorts every element, and then throws some of them away; `xs.Where(p).OrderBy(k)` does all three to the survivors only."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The collection is sorted before it is filtered"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4034.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4040",
              "name": "CopyingProperty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Property syntax reads as a field read. That is the whole of the convention and it is why callers put a property inside a loop, inside a condition and inside an interpolation without thinking about it — and why `Items.Count` followed by `Items[0]` is two copies of the collection rather than one. The cost is invisible at the call site, it is proportional to the collection, and it is paid at a frequency the declaration cannot see. ⚠ **The reported shape is narrower than \"a property that copies\": the copy has to be one the property could simply have skipped.** Where the source's own type already converts to the property's type by identity or by reference, the materializing call is doing nothing but copying, and removing it is an edit that keeps the signature. Where it also converts — `int[] Items => list.ToArray();` — the call is doing work the type demands, there is no edit that keeps the declared type, and a finding with no available answer is one the reader argues with instead of acting on."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property's getter is a single call that allocates a fresh copy of a collection whose own type the property already accepts, so every read walks the source and allocates again."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The property copies a collection it could have returned unchanged"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK4041",
              "name": "UnreadStringBuilder",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every `Append` copies characters into a buffer, grows it when it runs out, and the whole of it is then collected. The work is real and the result is discarded. ⚠ **It is almost never a performance mistake by intent — it is a missing line.** The usual cause is a `return builder.ToString();` that was never written, or a caller that was deleted while the code filling the buffer stayed; both compile, both pass every test that does not assert on the output, and neither the compiler nor the SDK analyzers say a word, because appending to a local and dropping it is legal in exactly the way appending to a local and using it is."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local `StringBuilder` is constructed and appended to, and nothing in the member ever reads its text or hands it to anything that could."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `StringBuilder` is filled and never read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK4041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Performance",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5001",
              "name": "SqlFromRequestConcatenation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The injected text is not data to the database, it is source code. A single quote in a value ends the literal the query put it in, and everything after it is parsed as more SQL running with the application's own credentials — which is how one search box becomes `DROP TABLE` or a dump of every row the connection can see. The fix is not to escape the quote; escaping is a blocklist and blocklists are wrong at the edges, on every dialect, forever. The fix is to stop concatenating: a parameter is transmitted beside the statement rather than inside it, so the database never parses it as SQL and there is no edge case left to get wrong. Every client has the shape — `command.Parameters.Add`, Dapper's anonymous `param` object, EF Core's `FromSql($\"…\")` interpolated overload — and all of them are shorter than the concatenation they replace. ⚠ There is no fix on the diagnostic because the edit is not local: introducing a parameter means changing the text, adding a binding, and choosing the parameter's type, which is three decisions rather than a substitution."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A value that arrived from the request is built into SQL text that a command executes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data is concatenated into SQL"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5002",
              "name": "ProcessStartFromRequest",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`ProcessStartInfo.Arguments` is a single string that the child process re-splits on its own rules, so a quote or an ampersand in the value is not data — it is punctuation in somebody else's grammar, and the result is a second command running as this process's user. `FileName` is worse: it chooses the program. The replacement for `Arguments` is `ArgumentList`, which passes each element to the child verbatim — exec takes an array on Unix, and on Windows .NET does the quoting itself — so there is no syntax left to escape and no shell involved. The replacement for a tainted `FileName` is a `switch` over the programs this code is allowed to run, returning the constant path for each; an allow-list is the only thing that works, because deciding whether an arbitrary path is safe to execute is not a question with an answer. ⚠ There is no fix on the diagnostic: moving one `Arguments` string into `ArgumentList` means splitting a command line, which is exactly the parsing this rule exists to avoid guessing at."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A value that arrived from the request decides which program runs, or what it is told to do."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data reaches a process start"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5005",
              "name": "WeakCipherAlgorithm",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`DES` has a 56-bit key, which was already marginal when it was standardised and is exhaustively searched in hours on hardware anyone can rent. `TripleDES` fixed the key length and not the 64-bit block, so a collision becomes likely after about 32 GB under one key and the plaintext starts coming back out — that is CVE-2016-2183, \"Sweet32\", and it is a practical attack on a long TLS connection rather than a theoretical one. `RC2` has practical key-recovery attacks. ECB is a different failure and a more visible one: it encrypts equal blocks to equal ciphertext, so the shape of the input survives encryption — the standard demonstration is a bitmap of a penguin that is still obviously a penguin afterwards. Use `Aes`, and prefer `AesGcm`, which authenticates the ciphertext as well as hiding it, so an attacker cannot flip bits in a message you will decrypt and act on. ⚠ There is no fix, and that is deliberate: swapping the algorithm compiles and changes the key length, the block size and the ciphertext format, so everything already encrypted becomes unreadable. That is a data migration, not an edit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`DES`, `TripleDES`, `RC2` or ECB mode — none of them hides a plaintext any more."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A broken cipher, or a mode that leaks structure"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5007",
              "name": "CertificateValidationDisabled",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "TLS gives you two things and they are separable: the traffic is encrypted, and the party at the other end is who the certificate says. A callback that returns `true` for every input keeps the first and throws away the second — so the connection is perfectly encrypted, to whoever answered. That is the whole content of a man-in-the-middle attack: an attacker on the path presents any certificate at all, this code accepts it, and reads and rewrites everything in both directions while both ends see a padlock. Deleting the callback restores the platform's validation, which is chain building, revocation, hostname matching and the trust store, and is years of work nobody should reimplement. If one specific self-signed certificate genuinely has to be trusted — a device on a factory network, a local development server — then pin it: compare `certificate.GetCertHashString()` against the one thumbprint you mean and return `false` for every other input. That is four lines, it is auditable, and it fails closed when the wrong certificate shows up."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A TLS validation callback that returns `true` without looking makes the connection encrypted but unauthenticated."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A certificate callback that accepts everything"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5009",
              "name": "XmlExternalEntityResolution",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A DTD can declare an entity whose value is a URI, and a reader with a resolver will go and get it — so a document the process was merely asked to parse can read any file that process can read and reach any host it can reach, and hand the contents back inside the parsed result or out through an error message. That is XXE, and it turns an XML endpoint into a file-read and a request-forgery primitive at once. Two settings control it and either one closes the hole: `XmlResolver = null` keeps DTD parsing working for internal entities while resolving nothing external, and `DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Prohibit` refuses the DTD outright, which is right when the documents are not supposed to have one. ⚠ There is no fix, because which of those two is correct depends on whether the documents legitimately carry a DTD — a question about the data, not about the code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "DTD processing plus a resolver that goes to the network or the disk is XXE."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An XML reader that parses a DTD and fetches what it names"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5009.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5010",
              "name": "RegexWithoutTimeout",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A backtracking engine, asked to match `^(a+)+$` against thirty `a`s and a `b`, has to try every way of splitting those thirty characters between the inner `+` and the outer one before it can report failure — and there are 2^29 of them. That is the whole attack: no memory is allocated, no error is logged, nothing looks wrong on a dashboard, and one request holds a thread until the process is restarted. A handful of such requests is a denial of service against a machine that is otherwise idle, which is why this is a vulnerability rather than a performance note. Two things fix it and both are one argument in the same expression as the pattern. A `matchTimeout` bounds the damage without changing which inputs match: the engine throws `RegexMatchTimeoutException` when it has spent too long, and the caller decides what a too-slow input means. `RegexOptions.NonBacktracking` removes the cause instead of bounding it — it selects an automaton-based engine whose running time is linear in the input no matter what the pattern looks like — at the price of refusing lookarounds, backreferences and atomic groups at construction time, so it is the better answer where the pattern does not use them. ⚠ There is no fix on the diagnostic because neither answer can be chosen mechanically: a timeout value is a policy decision about how long this particular call is allowed to take, and `NonBacktracking` throws at run time on a pattern that uses a construct it does not support, so an edit that compiled would not be an edit that worked."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A nested unbounded quantifier makes a near-match take exponential time, and no timeout means nothing stops it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A pattern that can backtrack, with nothing bounding how long it may take"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5020",
              "name": "PredictableCipherInitializationVector",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "CBC exists to stop the thing ECB does: it chains each block into the next so that identical plaintext blocks encrypt differently. The chain has to start somewhere, and the initialisation vector is that start. If it is the same on every message, the chain begins identically every time — so two messages that share a prefix produce ciphertext that shares a prefix, and an observer learns that they are related and where they diverge, which is exactly the structure `SK5005` reports ECB for leaking. It gets worse than leakage: with a predictable IV an attacker who can get a chosen plaintext encrypted can test guesses about an unknown block, which is the BEAST attack. The IV is not a secret and it is not a second key — it must be *unpredictable*, and it travels with the ciphertext in the clear. `GenerateIV()` draws a fresh one per message from the platform's cryptographic generator; prepend it to the output and read it back before decrypting. `AesGcm` takes a nonce and does the same job while also authenticating the ciphertext, so an attacker cannot flip bits in a message you will decrypt and act on. ⚠ There is no fix on the diagnostic, and that is the range's \"rarely\" column rather than a gap: making the IV fresh means also writing it into the message and reading it back at the other end, which changes the ciphertext format — every value already encrypted becomes unreadable by the new code. That is a migration, exactly as it is for `SK5005`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The initialisation vector is a constant, an array of zeros, or the key — so it is not a vector, it is a second constant."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A cipher initialisation vector fixed at compile time"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5021",
              "name": "UndersizedAsymmetricKey",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The security of RSA and of finite-field DSA is the cost of factoring a number that the code chooses the size of, and that cost has been falling for forty years while the number stayed the same length. A 1024-bit modulus is estimated at about 80 bits of work; NIST SP 800-57 has required 112 bits — which is 2048 — since 2014, and CA/Browser Forum rules stopped issuing certificates below it at the same time. The distance between \"nobody has published a 1024-bit factorisation\" and \"nobody can afford one\" is not a security property, and it is not the sort of thing to find out about afterwards, because a key that turns out to be factorable retroactively invalidates every signature it ever made. The size is a single argument at the generation call, which is what makes this decidable where most cryptographic mistakes are not. Generate at least 2048 bits — or move to `ECDsa`, where a 256-bit key is stronger than a 3072-bit RSA one, the keys and signatures are an order of magnitude smaller, and generation is fast enough to do per-request."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A 1024-bit RSA or DSA modulus is a budget away from being factored, and the size is written at the call."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An RSA or DSA key generated below 2048 bits"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5030",
              "name": "XmlSignatureUnverifiedKey",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An XML signature is supposed to answer “who signed this”, and the parameterless overload answers “is this document internally consistent” instead. It takes its key from the signature's own `KeyInfo` element — a part of the document — so an attacker rewrites the payload, signs it with a key pair they generated a second ago, writes their public key into `KeyInfo`, and the check returns `true`. Every document can vouch for itself, which means the check distinguishes nothing. That is the whole of the SAML and XML-DSig signature-bypass family, and it is dangerous precisely because the call site reads like a real validation: there is a `CheckSignature`, it returns a `bool`, and the code branches on it. The fix is to say which key you trust — `CheckSignature(publicKey)` with a key from configuration, or `CheckSignature(certificate, false)` with a certificate you resolved and chain-validated yourself. ⚠ There is no fix on the diagnostic because the edit is a decision rather than a substitution: which key is trusted, and where it comes from, is the question the rule exists to make somebody answer."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`SignedXml.CheckSignature()` with no argument verifies the document against the key written inside the document."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An XML signature checked against the key the document carries"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5040",
              "name": "XmlResolverReenabled",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A DTD can declare an entity whose value is a URI, and a reader with a resolver will go and fetch it — so a document the process was merely asked to parse can read any file that process can read and reach any host it can reach, and hand the contents back inside the parsed result or out through an error message. That is XXE, and it turns an XML endpoint into a file-read and a request-forgery primitive at once. What makes this decidable where `SK5009` needed two facts is that these two receivers have only one switch. `XmlDocument` — and `XmlDataDocument` under it — has an `XmlResolver` and no `DtdProcessing` at all. `XmlTextReader` has both, and its `DtdProcessing` defaults to `Parse`. On both, `XmlResolver` has defaulted to `null` since .NET Framework 4.5.2, so the safe configuration is the one you get by writing nothing, and an explicit `XmlUrlResolver` is somebody deliberately putting external resolution back. The defect is written out rather than inferred. ⚠ There is no fix, and the reason is `SK5009`'s rather than a weaker one: the mitigation is to leave the default, but this code went out of its way not to, so the edit removes a capability the author asked for. If the documents genuinely reference external entities the answer is `XmlSecureResolver` with a restricted permission set, not `null` — and which of the two applies is a question about the data, not about the code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`XmlDocument` and `XmlTextReader` resolve nothing by default; assigning an `XmlUrlResolver` is a deliberate re-enable."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An XML resolver put back where the platform's default is none"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5041",
              "name": "FixedKeyDerivationSalt",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A salt is what stops one unit of work from breaking many credentials. It is not a secret and it does not have to be — it has to be *different for every credential*, so that an attacker who takes the store cannot precompute anything reusable. Fix it, and the property is gone in both directions: a rainbow table built once against that one salt breaks every password in the store at once, and any two users who chose the same password have visibly identical derived keys, so the store leaks who shares a password with whom before anything is even cracked. Draw sixteen bytes from `RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(16)` per credential and store them beside the derived key; that is what the verifying side reads back. ⚠ There is no fix. The edit changes what a stored credential record contains, so every password already written becomes unverifiable by the new code — the migration is to re-derive on next successful sign-in, which is a change to the program's behaviour rather than to its text. That is the same call `SK5005` and `SK5020` make."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "PBKDF2 with a hard-coded salt derives the same key from the same password on every machine, which is what a salt exists to prevent."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The key-derivation salt is fixed at compile time"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK5042",
              "name": "WorldWritableFileMode",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "World-writable means what it says: every account on the machine, including whatever a compromised unrelated service runs as, may overwrite the file. The damage is rarely the write itself — it is that this program will read the file back later and act on it, so a configuration file, a cache, a lock file or a script becomes an input an unprivileged local attacker controls. It is the kind of defect that never shows up in testing, because the developer's machine has one user and the container has one process. Drop `OtherWrite`; the owner and, where something genuinely shares, the group are what the bits are for. If this really is a shared drop directory where anyone may add an entry, say so by adding `UnixFileMode.StickyBit` — that is mode `1777`, what `/tmp` itself is, and it stops one user deleting or replacing another's entries. ⚠ There is no fix. Removing the bit is a one-token edit, but whether the remaining permissions are the ones this program actually needs depends on who else reads the file — a deployment fact the compiler cannot see, and a bit removed that something downstream depended on breaks at run time rather than at build time."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`UnixFileMode.OtherWrite` without the sticky bit lets any local process replace the contents of what this call creates."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A file or directory created writable by every local user"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK5042.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Security",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6003",
              "name": "AbstractTypePublicConstructor",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An abstract class cannot be instantiated, so its constructor is reachable only from a derived constructor's initializer — which is exactly what `protected` allows. Written `public`, the modifier claims a caller that cannot exist, and a reader scanning the type for its public surface has to work out that this member is not part of it. `protected` says what the constructor is for. The edit is one keyword, it removes nothing any caller could have been using, and it is verified by the compiler."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Nothing outside the type hierarchy can call it, so `public` on it states something that is not true."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An abstract type has a public constructor"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6008",
              "name": "ExtensionMethodOnObject",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An extension method is an API surface projected onto its receiver type. Using `object` projects it onto every string, integer, collection, task and domain value in the program, making completion noisier and making accidental calls easy. A narrower receiver also states the contract the implementation already relies on. There is no mechanical fix: selecting that receiver is an API-design decision."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The method appears in completion for every value, even though almost no extension is meaningful for every type."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An extension method extends `object`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6008.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6020",
              "name": "EnumConstraintWithoutStruct",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`where T : struct, Enum` is what the author of `where T : Enum` meant, and the difference is one type argument: `System.Enum`, the abstract base class. It satisfies the bare constraint by identity, so inside the method `T` is not known to be a value type — `default(T)` is `null`, every use boxes, and the type argument names a class that is not an enum and has no values. The BCL agrees: `Enum.GetValues<TEnum>()`, `Enum.TryParse<TEnum>`, `Enum.IsDefined<TEnum>` and `Enum.GetName<TEnum>` are all declared `where TEnum : struct, Enum`, so a method constrained the weaker way cannot call any of them and has to fall back on the boxing, reflective overloads it was written to avoid. Adding `struct` costs one keyword and makes the signature say what the body already assumes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`where T : Enum` admits `System.Enum` itself, so `T` is a reference type whose `default` is `null` and which no enum API accepts."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An `Enum` constraint has no `struct` beside it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6021",
              "name": "ExceptionNameWithoutExceptionBase",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Exception` at the end of a type name is not decoration; it is the one naming convention in .NET that a language construct depends on. A reader who sees `PaymentDeclinedException` writes `catch (PaymentDeclinedException)`, and if the type does not derive from `System.Exception` that line does not compile — the name has cost them the time it takes to find out why. Everything else that keys on the suffix is misled the same way: `throw`, exception filters, logging that unwraps an `AggregateException`, analyzers that check exception design, and the reviewer skimming a diff. The type is either an exception and should say so in its base list, or it is not one and should not claim the suffix. Neither answer is a mechanical edit, which is why the rule has no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `Exception` suffix is a promise that `catch (SomethingException)` will work, and a type that does not derive from `System.Exception` cannot keep it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type is named like an exception and is not one"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6022",
              "name": "TypeNameRestatesItsKind",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type's kind is written in its declaration, and every tool that shows the type shows the keyword: the IDE's completion list, the hover, the go-to-definition, the diff. Repeating it in the identifier spends a word on information the reader already has, and it costs the name whatever a real noun would have contributed — `OrderClass` is a worse name than `Order`, not a more precise one. The suffix is also the first thing to go stale, because it survives the edit that makes it wrong: a `class` that becomes a `record` keeps its `Class` suffix until somebody notices. This is not `CA1711`, which is about suffixes .NET has *reserved* — `Attribute`, `Collection`, `EventArgs`. These are not reserved; they are simply empty."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`class OrderClass` and `struct PointStruct` say the same thing twice; the keyword is already there, two words to the left."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type name repeats the keyword the declaration already carries"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6022.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6023",
              "name": "EmptyType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An empty type is usually one of four things, and three of them are legitimate patterns: a marker attribute, a marker interface, a named specialisation of a generic base, or the leftovers of an edit. Only the fourth is a finding, and the first three are all recognisable from the declaration itself — an attribute has a base or an attribute list, a marker interface is an interface, and a specialisation has a base list. What is left is a type with nothing attached to it in any direction: no members, no base, no interfaces, no attributes, no type parameters, not `partial`. That is not a marker, because nothing can key on it; it is a name and a pair of braces. Either it is about to become something and the declaration landed early, or it stopped being something and the members were deleted around it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `class Foo { }` that derives from nothing, carries no attribute and declares nothing is a declaration that was started and not finished."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type has no members, no base and no attributes"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6023.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6030",
              "name": "TypeInGlobalNamespace",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A namespace is how a type says which program it belongs to, and the global namespace says nothing. Every assembly in a consumer's dependency closure contributes to the same flat table, so two libraries that each declare a global `Settings` cannot both be referenced — and the error arrives in the consumer's build, naming two types the consumer did not write. The usual escapes do not apply: `using Configuration = Contoso.Configuration;` needs a namespace to alias, and `global::Settings` names the ambiguity rather than resolving it, so the only repair available downstream is an `extern alias` on the whole assembly. The namespace costs one line here; omitting it moves the cost to somebody who cannot pay it without a source change to a package they do not own."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The global namespace is one flat name table shared with every referencing assembly, and it is the one namespace a `using` cannot name."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type is declared in the global namespace"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6031",
              "name": "ReadonlyMutableField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`public readonly string[] Names` stops `Names = …` and stops nothing else. Every caller can still write `Names[0] = \"…\"`, and on a `List<T>` can `Clear()` it — from another thread, at any time, with no compiler complaint anywhere. What makes this worse than declaring the field without the modifier is the reader: `readonly` is the keyword C# has for \"this does not change\", so a reviewer who sees it stops looking for the mutation, and static analysis, code review and the type's own documentation all inherit the mistake. A field that must not be written through is either `private` with a `IReadOnlyList<T>` property over it, or typed as something that really is immutable — `ImmutableArray<T>`, `FrozenDictionary<K, V>`, `ReadOnlyCollection<T>`. `readonly` is neither of those and cannot be made into either by adding a keyword."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`readonly` constrains the field and never the object in it, so a non-private array or mutable collection is writable by every caller that can see it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`readonly` does not protect a mutable field's contents"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6032",
              "name": "AbstractTypeWithoutAbstraction",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A reader who meets `abstract class Report` expects to find the part a subclass has to supply, and goes looking for it. When there is no `abstract` member, no `virtual` member, nothing `protected` and no base type, there is nothing to find: no derived type is required to provide anything, none is offered anything it could not get from an instance, and the keyword's only effect is that `new Report()` does not compile. That is a real intent and there are three better ways to state it — a `static` class if the members are all static, an interface if the members are all contracts, or a concrete class if instantiating it is fine — and each of the three tells the next reader something. `abstract` alone tells them to keep looking for a hook that is not there."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`abstract` says both \"do not instantiate this\" and \"a derived type completes it\", and a class with no hooks, nothing `protected` and no base is only saying the first."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An abstract type declares nothing to implement"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6032.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6033",
              "name": "OnlyPrivateConstructors",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A private constructor is a normal thing to write: a singleton hands out one instance, a factory validates before constructing, a static holder predates `static class`. Each of those puts something beside the constructor — a `new` of the type inside the type, or a nested type deriving from it. The finding is the same private constructor with none of that: no creation anywhere it could legally appear, no derivation, and no `static` to say the type was never meant to be instantiated. The type compiles, ships, and cannot be reached, and no compiler diagnostic says so — the members read as a public API that no caller can ever call. `SK6003` reports the opposite error on abstract types; this is the same question about instantiability from the other end."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Nothing outside the type can construct it, and nothing inside the file does either, so no caller can ever obtain an instance."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type has only private constructors and is not static"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6033.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6034",
              "name": "PublicConstantField",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **This is a property of how the value is distributed, not of how the declaration is written.** The compiler does not emit a field read for `Limits.MaxRetries`; it emits the literal `3`, into the *consumer's* assembly. Ship version 2 of the library with the value changed to `5`, and every assembly that was built against version 1 keeps `3` — the load succeeds, no binding redirect fires, no analyzer complains, and the two halves of the program disagree about a number they both believe they read from one place. It is the one versioning hazard in .NET with no diagnostic at all behind it: a renamed member is a `MissingMethodException`, a changed signature is a `MissingMethodException`, and a changed constant is silence. `static readonly` is read from the field at run time and does not have this property, at the cost of one indirection and a type initializer. The reason to keep a `const` public is that its value is part of the contract and can never change — a protocol magic number, a format version, a mathematical constant — and that is a claim worth making on purpose rather than by default. ⚠ **`dotnet_code_quality.SK6034.frozen_constant_types` is where that claim is made.** It names the containing types whose constants are contract, it defaults to empty — so the rule reports everything until a project says otherwise — and the analyzer holds no opinion about which names are special."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The compiler copies a `const` into the consuming assembly, so changing it and shipping only your own assembly leaves every caller on the old value with no error anywhere."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A public constant is baked into every caller"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6034.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6040",
              "name": "UnusedOutVariable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An `out` argument is a second return value, and a declaration expression is how the caller receives it. When the receiving name is never read, the declaration is doing one thing only — satisfying the signature — and it does that while adding a name to the enclosing scope, a definite-assignment obligation, and a line the next reader has to check before concluding it does not matter. `out _` is the language's word for exactly this: the argument is required, the value is not wanted. It also removes the name from scope, so a later edit cannot accidentally read a value nobody meant to keep, and it makes the two `TryGetValue` calls in a file that differ only in whether the value is used visibly different from each other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`M(out var x)` where nothing ever mentions `x` again declares a name for a value the code has decided not to look at; `out _` says that."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An `out` variable is declared and never read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3",
                "languageVersion": "7.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6041",
              "name": "WiderForeachVariableType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The collection already knows what it holds. Declaring the loop variable as a base type discards that at the one point in the method where it was free, and everything downstream pays: the body cannot call the element's own members without a cast or a pattern, the reader cannot tell what is in the collection without following the collection back to its declaration, and where the element is a value type every iteration boxes. Naming the element type is not extra precision — it is the precision the collection was carrying anyway, written down."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`foreach (object item in names)` over a `List<string>` throws the element type away at the top of the loop and makes the body ask for it back."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `foreach` variable is declared wider than the element it receives"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6041.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6050",
              "name": "MethodReturnsAConstant",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This is the failure that compiles. A method with the right name, the right parameters and the right return type, whose body is `return 0;`, satisfies every caller, passes a smoke test, and is wrong — and it is exactly what gets written when the shape of the work is known and the work is not. On AI-written code it is the single most common way a stub survives review: nothing about it looks unfinished, because a `TODO` was never written and a `NotImplementedException` was never thrown. The parameters are the tell. Computing an argument and handing it to a method that cannot read it is work the program does for no reason, and it is visible in the declaration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `private` method takes parameters, reads none of them, and returns a compile-time constant — the shape a placeholder body has when the signature was written before the logic."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The method ignores its inputs and returns a constant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6050.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6051",
              "name": "IsCheckAgainstThis",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The dependency runs the wrong way. A base type is supposed to be the thing subclasses know about, and here it is the thing that knows about them: adding a subclass means opening the base and finding every `is` that has to learn about it. It is polymorphism written as a chain of `if`s, with none of the checking that makes an `abstract` member work — a missing `override` is a compile error, and a missing branch is a wrong answer at run time that nothing anywhere reports. The repair is nearly always a member on the base that each subclass overrides, which moves the decision to where the compiler can see it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A class asks whether `this` is one of its own subclasses, so adding a subclass means editing the base and nothing tells anyone when a case is missing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The base type tests `this` against a derived type"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6051.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6052",
              "name": "NullReturnedInsteadOfEmpty",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A sequence return type is a promise that the result can be iterated, and `null` breaks it in the one way the type system does not catch. The failure lands at a `foreach` in the caller, not at the `return` that caused it, and the stack names the wrong file. The cost of keeping the promise is nothing: `[]` for a `T[]` is `Array.Empty<T>()`, which is a singleton, and for a `List<T>` it is one empty list against one null check in every caller for ever. \"Empty\" and \"absent\" are also not usually different questions for a sequence — a caller that needs to tell them apart is asking about something other than the elements, and a nullable return type is how to say so."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A method whose return type is an array or a sequence interface returns `null`, so every caller has to null-check before iterating and the ones that forget fail at a `foreach`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Null is returned where an empty sequence was expected"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6052.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0",
                "languageVersion": "12.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6053",
              "name": "AsyncSuffixConvention",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `Async` suffix is how a caller knows a result must be awaited, and it is what makes a missing `await` visible in review rather than at run time — the fire-and-forget shape `SK3005` reports. Read the other way it matters as much: a name ending in `Async` on a method that returns nothing to await tells every caller to write an `await` that will not compile, or worse, to assume work is happening in the background that is not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A method returning an awaitable is not named `…Async`, or a method named `…Async` returns nothing to await. Ships disabled; turn it on per path where the convention is adopted."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The method name does not reflect its synchronicity"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6053.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6060",
              "name": "InvariantTypeParameter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Variance is the one piece of an interface's design that costs the declarer nothing and is invisible until somebody needs it. `IReadOnlyList<out T>` is why `IReadOnlyList<string>` is an `IReadOnlyList<object>`; an interface that could have said the same thing and did not forces every consumer into a copy, a `Cast<T>()` or a second overload — and the consumer usually cannot tell that the restriction was accidental rather than meant. ⚠ **The declaration is the only place the question can be asked**, because the compiler will not infer it and nothing in a call site says \"this would have worked\"."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An interface whose type parameter appears only in output positions can declare it `out`, and every assignment its callers currently cannot write becomes legal without any of them changing."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The interface's type parameter is invariant and need not be"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6060.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6061",
              "name": "CallerInfoParameterNotLast",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`[CallerMemberName]` is a promise that the caller does not have to say where the call came from. A parameter after it takes that back: to reach the later parameter positionally the caller has to supply the caller-info one too, and the value it then carries is whatever was typed rather than where the call is. The declaration reads as two independent conveniences and behaves as one that disables the other. ⚠ **The failure is silent in both directions** — the caller who wants the later parameter loses the location without a diagnostic, and the caller who wants the location cannot reach the later parameter at all except by name."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The compiler only fills a caller-info parameter the caller omitted, so an ordinary parameter declared after one can never be passed positionally without cancelling the substitution it sits in front of."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A caller-info parameter is followed by an ordinary parameter"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6061.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK6062",
              "name": "WriteOnlyLocalCollection",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Both halves look correct in isolation, which is why review does not catch it: the declaration is ordinary, every `Add` is ordinary, and nothing is missing from any line. What is missing is a line. The compiler has nothing to say — a collection that is written to is used, as far as it is concerned — and every test over the method passes, because the method's observable behaviour is exactly what it would be with the collection deleted. ⚠ **The usual cause is a removed consumer**: the loop that read the list was deleted or moved and the loop that fills it was left behind, still paying for every element."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A local collection is created, added to, and never enumerated, indexed, counted or handed anywhere — so the work that fills it is a feature that was started and abandoned."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local collection is filled and never read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK6062.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Design",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7001",
              "name": "CyclomaticComplexity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cyclomatic complexity counts the decision points in a body, and it is the number that predicts how many test cases the body needs to cover. It is computed from Roslyn's `ControlFlowGraph` rather than by counting keywords, so a `switch` expression, a pattern with a `when` clause, a conditional access chain and a `&&` short-circuit are each counted the way the compiler sees them rather than the way a regular expression would. It is the least interesting of the metrics on its own — a long flat `switch` scores high and reads fine — which is why it defaults to `hint` and why cognitive complexity is the one the gate reads."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The number of linearly independent paths through a member, from its control-flow graph."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cyclomatic complexity is over the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7002",
              "name": "CognitiveComplexity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cognitive complexity is the metric worth gating on, and it is the one SonarQube users actually miss. It differs from cyclomatic complexity in the two ways that matter: a `switch` over twenty cases costs one, because a reader takes it in at a glance, and a condition nested three deep costs four rather than one, because a reader has to hold three contexts to understand it. That makes it track the thing people mean by \"this method is hard\" rather than the thing that is easy to count. The implementation follows Sonar's specification — increments for each control-flow break, a nesting increment for each structure nested inside another, one increment per sequence of like binary operators, and increments for `goto`, recursion and each `catch` — so a number here is comparable to a number from SonarQube on the same code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "How hard a member is to read, by Sonar's published definition: nesting-weighted, with no penalty for a `switch`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cognitive complexity is over the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7003",
              "name": "MethodLengthInStatements",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Counting lines makes the metric a function of the formatter: the same body scores differently at a 100-column width and at a 160-column one, and wrapping a long argument list makes a method \"longer\" without changing anything a reader has to understand. Counting statements is invariant under formatting, which is the property that makes the number worth putting in a gate at all. Declarations, expression statements, and each statement nested inside a block are counted once each; a `for` header is one statement, not three."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Method length counted in statements, not in lines."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The member is over the statement-count threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7004",
              "name": "TypeSizeInMembers",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type's member count is the crudest of the metrics and still the one that finds god objects. Fields are counted separately from methods because the two mean different things: forty fields is a data carrier, which may be exactly right, while forty methods is a type doing forty jobs. The report carries both numbers and the rule fires on the member count. A partial type is measured per declaration rather than per symbol, because that is the file a person opens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Members declared on a type, with fields counted separately."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The type is over the member-count threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7005",
              "name": "ParameterCount",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A long parameter list is the one design smell that is unambiguous from the outside: past about seven the call sites stop being readable and start being positional puzzles, and every one of them has to change when the list does. Primary-constructor parameters count, because they are the type's constructor whatever the syntax; doc 07 § Metrics says so explicitly."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Parameter count, including a primary constructor's parameters."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The member takes more parameters than the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7006",
              "name": "NestingDepth",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Nesting depth is what cognitive complexity's nesting weight is built on, reported on its own because it is the actionable half: a body six levels deep almost always has an early return or a `continue` that flattens it, and a reader can see that from the number without reading the body. A lambda body restarts the count, because a lambda is a separate reading context."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The deepest block nesting inside a member body."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The member nests deeper than the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7006.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7010",
              "name": "PublicApiCommentDensity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Comment density is the metric most likely to be gamed and the one most worth having as a *number* rather than as a finding. As an aggregate it says whether a library's public surface is documented; as a per-member warning on a repository that has never documented anything it produces thousands of findings on day one and gets switched off by lunchtime. So it ships disabled — `defaultSeverity: none` — and a repository that wants it turns it on for the paths where it means something, exactly the way doc 08 § SK1000 describes for the hot-path rules. The aggregate is computed either way and appears in the report and in `skala trend`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Doc-comment coverage of the publicly visible surface, reported as a metric and — opt-in — as a finding."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Public API without a documentation comment"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7020",
              "name": "DuplicatedBlock",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Duplication is the one SonarQube feature with no Roslyn equivalent, and the AI workflow needs it more than most: agents copy-paste by nature, and a block pasted four times is four places a fix has to land. Detection is token-level type-2 clone detection — identifiers and literals normalised to a class, keywords and punctuation kept exact — so a block copied and renamed is still found, which is the copy people actually make. A clone group is reported once, at its first occurrence, with the rest as related locations; reporting it at every occurrence would turn one problem into n findings and make the count meaningless."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A run of at least `minTokens` tokens that occurs identically, up to identifier and literal names, somewhere else in the repository."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Duplicated block"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7030",
              "name": "FileLength",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "File length is a coarse review signal, not a claim that the code is incorrect. The deliberately broad 1000-line default is a configurable starting policy, not a measurement calibrated to this repository or a universal design limit. Comments, blank lines and inactive preprocessor text count. A terminal empty line after the final newline does not count; an empty file has zero lines. Splitting a file requires design judgment, so there is no automatic fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A file contains more physical lines than dotnet_code_quality.SK7030.threshold (default 1000)."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File length exceeds the configured threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7040",
              "name": "TodoWithoutIssueReference",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A TODO in source is discovered only by someone who happens to open that line. An issue can be assigned, discussed, searched and closed; the comment remains useful as the local pointer to it. The rule accepts a URL, `#123`, or a project key such as `SKALA-123` and does not attempt to invent an issue or remove the comment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An actionable comment has no durable place where its owner, context and completion can be tracked."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TODO or FIXME has no issue reference"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7040.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7050",
              "name": "PragmaWithoutJustification",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A local pragma is an explicit exception to a repository rule. Without a reason, nobody can distinguish a deliberate exception from a temporary silencing, decide whether it is still necessary, or safely remove it. The rule accepts a meaningful trailing or immediately preceding comment and never manufactures one, because a generated justification would be worse than none."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The source suppresses one or more diagnostics without telling the next reader why the warning is wrong here."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A warning-disable pragma has no justification"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7050.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7051",
              "name": "SuppressMessageWithoutJustification",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A suppression attribute survives refactoring and is easy to read past, so its reason is part of the code review record. A nonempty `Justification` tells a future reader which analyzer assumption does not hold and whether the exception can be retired. The tool reports the omission but cannot write the explanation on the author's behalf."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`SuppressMessage` or `UnconditionalSuppressMessage` omits a meaningful `Justification`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A suppression attribute has no justification"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7051.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7060",
              "name": "CommentedCode",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Disabled statements can become stale and obscure the active implementation. This is a deliberately narrow syntax heuristic: it requests review, not deletion, because a comment may be an intentional example or a record of an experiment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A standalone comment block parses as at least two code-like C# statements with substantial code punctuation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review commented-out statements"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7060.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7070",
              "name": "ObsoleteWithoutMessage",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An obsolescence is a migration instruction addressed to every caller. Without a message the compiler warning says only that something is deprecated, and the reader has to find the replacement by archaeology. This is the same requirement `SK7050` and `SK7051` make of a suppression, on the other side of the conversation: the annotation that changes what other code should do carries the reason. The tool reports the omission and never invents the replacement, because a manufactured message would be worse than none."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Obsolete` is applied with no message, or with a placeholder message, so no caller is told what to use instead."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An obsolete marker has no message"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7070.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7071",
              "name": "ExcludeFromCodeCoverageWithoutJustification",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An exclusion changes what the coverage percentage counts, and it does so permanently and invisibly: the number goes up and nothing in the report says which code stopped being measured. The reason is the only thing that lets a later reader decide whether the exclusion is still earned. This is the third member of the family `SK7050` and `SK7051` started, and it reports the omission without writing the reason, for the same reason they do."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`ExcludeFromCodeCoverage` omits a meaningful `Justification`, on a target framework whose attribute has the property."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A coverage exclusion has no justification"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7071.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7072",
              "name": "EmptySuppressionRegion",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The usual way this happens is an edit: the code the pragma bracketed was deleted and the bracket stayed. What is left is a suppression waiting for a subject, and the next statement written between the directives is exempted from a diagnostic nobody chose to exempt it from. No code inside means no diagnostic inside, for every analyzer that will ever run, so the finding needs to know nothing about which analyzers those are — and the fix is to delete the directives and nothing else."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `#pragma warning disable` region contains no code, so it suppresses nothing and silently covers whatever is written there next."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A warning suppression covers nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7072.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7073",
              "name": "EmptyRegion",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A region survives every deletion inside it, so an empty one is the residue of a member that moved or went away. It is a heading over nothing, and the outline it produces claims a structure the file does not have. The fix deletes the two directive lines, which is the whole of the edit: nothing else in the file changes and no token moves."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `#region` and its `#endregion` have only whitespace between them."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A region is empty"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7073.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7074",
              "name": "UnstructuredGoto",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ The position, because `goto` is legal and occasionally right. `goto case` and `goto default` are the only way C# expresses switch fall-through, they cannot leave the switch they are written in, and the flow they describe is already in front of the reader — reporting them would make this a style opinion about `switch` and get the whole rule turned off. A `goto` to a label is the other thing: its target is anywhere in the enclosing member, so what runs next can no longer be read off the nesting. Neither can it be computed: `SK7001` and `SK7002` derive complexity from the structure of the statements, and a label jump is an edge those numbers do not contain, which is why the rule sits in the same range as the metrics it distorts."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `goto` names a label, so control leaves the statement nesting. `goto case` and `goto default` are not reported."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Review a goto to a label"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7074.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7080",
              "name": "InheritanceDepth",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every level above a class is a place a member can come from and a place behaviour can be changed without touching the file in front of the reader. The number is worth reporting only for the part of the chain the repository actually wrote: `MyControl : Button` is one decision somebody made, and the eight framework types above `Button` are not. So the count stops at the first base class this compilation did not declare — which is also why the default is 4 rather than the textbook 5 or 6, since a chain of four source-declared bases is already unusual. Flattening a hierarchy is a design change with no mechanical form, so there is no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A class declares more base classes above it, within this compilation's own source, than dotnet_code_quality.SK7080.threshold (default 4)."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The inheritance chain is deeper than the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7080.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7081",
              "name": "TypeCoupling",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This is the metric `SK7001`–`SK7006` do not carry. A type can be short, shallow and simple in every one of them and still know about forty other types, and every one of those forty is a reason this file has to be reopened when something else changes. Counting the distinct named types a declaration mentions — bases, member types, locals, constructions, static receivers, attributes and the arguments of the generics it names — measures how much of the design has to be held in mind at once to change it, which is what people mean when they say a class is entangled. ⚠ The default of 80 is deliberately far above the textbook 30: on Skala's own tree — which is a Roslyn analyzer host, and therefore names one type per syntax-node kind before it does anything — a threshold of 30 fires on 125 type declarations and 80 fires on six, and those six are the god objects a reader would name unprompted. Reporting the shoulder of the distribution rather than its tail is the \"useless\" outcome this family's rationale names. Splitting a type is a design change with no mechanical form, so there is no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One type declaration mentions more distinct other named types than dotnet_code_quality.SK7081.threshold (default 80)."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The type depends on more other types than the threshold"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7081.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7082",
              "name": "NestedConditional",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Nested `?:` is the one expression form where operator precedence and reader expectation reliably disagree: `a ? b : c ? d : e` groups one way and `a ? b ? c : d : e` another, and neither grouping is visible without counting. ⚠ The measurement makes one distinction that decides whether the rule is usable at all: a right-associated ladder — `a ? x : b ? y : z` — costs **one** level, not one per rung. That shape is an `else if` chain written as an expression; it reads top to bottom, each condition is tested once, and charging it per rung would make the rule fire on the one nested form that is idiomatic. What costs a level is a `?:` anywhere else inside another: in the condition, in the `true` branch, or buried inside the `false` branch rather than being it. Rewriting a nest is a judgement about which branch deserves a name, so there is no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Conditional expressions nest more deeply than dotnet_code_quality.SK7082.threshold (default 1), counting a right-associated ladder as one level."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The conditional expressions are nested"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7082.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7083",
              "name": "RepeatedStringLiteral",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The same literal in several places is the same rename waiting to be done incompletely — the argument `nameof` makes for member names, applied to keys, formats and messages. ⚠ **It takes two options, and the second is what makes it usable.** A repeat count on its own reports `\", \"`, `\"/\"` and `\"true\"` long before it reports anything worth extracting, so a minimum length holds the floor and a literal must also contain a letter: a repeated separator or punctuation run is not a name anybody was going to give. ⚠ The default count is 5 — a literal must be written six times in one file — and that is the conservative end of a measurement with no wrong answer. On Skala's own tree a count of 3 reports 144 literals and a count of 5 reports 64; every one of the 144 is a real repeat, and the ones the higher default drops are the HTML fragments and fixture file names nobody was going to extract. The count is the option a repository lowers rather than raises, so the shipped number is the one that leaves room to. Choosing the name is the whole of the work, so there is no fix."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One string literal is written in a file more times than dotnet_code_quality.SK7083.threshold (default 5), counting only literals at least dotnet_code_quality.SK7083.minimum_length characters long (default 5) that contain a letter."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The string literal is repeated"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7083.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "hint",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7090",
              "name": "NotImplementedMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ This is `SK7040`'s requirement on the form that compiles, and it sits on Skala's premise rather than beside it. A model asked for an implementation produces a signature with a `throw new NotImplementedException()` body far more readily than it says it cannot do the work — and that body type-checks, binds, formats, and passes every analyzer in the build. It fails only when it runs, in a caller that had no way to know. A `TODO` at least announces itself to whoever opens the line; this announces itself to nobody. The requirement is therefore the same one `SK7040` makes: somewhere a reader will already be looking, name the issue that owns finishing this. The same vocabulary is accepted — a URL, `#123`, or a project key such as `SKALA-123` — read from the exception's own message, from the throw's own comments, or from the enclosing member's leading trivia."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A member's body is `throw new NotImplementedException()` and nothing names the issue that owes the implementation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A thrown `NotImplementedException` has no issue reference"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7090.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7091",
              "name": "ProcessExitOutsideEntryPoint",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`Environment.Exit` does not unwind. No `finally` runs, no `using` disposes, no buffered writer flushes and no `await` resumes. In a library, in a service, and in an executable's own helper class alike, it ends work that has nothing to do with whoever made the call — the caller's transaction is left open, its temporary file is left on disk, and its log line is left in a buffer that is never written."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`Environment.Exit` is called somewhere other than the entry point, so the process ends without unwinding."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The process is terminated outside the entry point"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7091.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7092",
              "name": "LoggedAndRethrown",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every layer that does this multiplies the log. One failure arrives as five entries with five different stack depths, nothing in any of them says they are the same event, and the layer that finally handled it is indistinguishable from the four that only watched it go past. Either handle the exception — and then it is not the caller's problem — or propagate it and let whoever handles it record it once."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `catch` hands the caught exception to a logging call and then rethrows it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The exception is both logged and rethrown"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7092.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7093",
              "name": "ConsoleInsteadOfLogger",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A console write cannot be filtered, routed, structured or correlated, and in every hosted environment — a container, a service, a function, a test host — it goes nowhere anybody looks. Complements `SK2016`, which assumes a logger is being used at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `Console.Write` or `Console.WriteLine` is made from code that has a logger in scope."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The console is written to where a logger was meant"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7093.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7100",
              "name": "DocumentationDuplicatesBaseMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A copied doc comment is accurate on the day it is written and has no mechanism keeping it so. The base member's prose is the one a reader treats as authoritative, and when it is edited the copy below silently starts describing the previous behaviour — which is worse than no documentation, because a reader has no way to tell which of the two is stale. `<inheritdoc />` cannot drift, because there is nothing for it to drift from: it is a reference rather than a copy, and it is exactly as long as the claim it makes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A member's documentation comment is word for word the one on the member it overrides or implements, so `<inheritdoc />` says the same thing and cannot fall out of step with it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The documentation duplicates the base member's"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7100.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7101",
              "name": "NonPublicMemberNotDocumented",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SK7010` is this rule over the public surface, and the two are meant to partition one population: the same predicates decide what a documentable declaration is, and the only difference is which side of the accessibility line it falls on. What is different is the volume. This is the highest-firing uncovered inspection in the whole parity measurement — 3 408 findings — and `SK7010`, which already ships, produces 1 868 on `Testing/corpus` at `warning`. So it ships **disabled**: `defaultSeverity: none`, the same as `SK7010`, turned on per path by a repository that wants it for the code where internal documentation earns its keep. That is a decision about a report, not about a rule: a finding nobody can see past is not a finding."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A declaration that is not publicly visible through its whole containing chain, and carries no documentation comment. Disabled by default; enable it per path."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A non-public member has no documentation comment"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": false,
                "level": "none"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7101.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "none",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7102",
              "name": "ReturnsDocumentedOnVoidMember",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Documentation is written by shape far more often than by reading the signature: `<summary>`, a `<param>` for each parameter, then `<returns>` — and the third slot gets filled because the template has a third slot, with a sentence invented to fit it. That is the plausible-but-wrong failure Skala exists for, in the one place where being wrong is invisible to everything else: the compiler checks `<param>` names against the parameter list (`CS1572`, `CS1573`) and checks nothing about `<returns>`, and a reader of the rendered page has no signature beside it to contradict the sentence. ⚠ **The element is not harmless filler.** A returns section is a claim about the member's contract, and a reader who believes it writes code that expects a value — or, worse, concludes the documentation is stale and stops trusting the rest of it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A documentation comment carries a `<returns>` element on a member declared `void`, or on a constructor or finalizer, so it describes a value no caller will ever receive."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `<returns>` element documents a member that returns nothing"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7102.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7103",
              "name": "InheritdocWithNothingToInherit",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "⚠ **`CS1591` is satisfied by the comment's mere presence.** A repository that enforces documentation coverage passes, and ships an API page that renders blank: this is a missing comment that specifically survives the check designed to find missing comments. `<inheritdoc />` is the cheapest thing to write that looks like documentation, and a model reaches for it when it has nothing to say — including on members that override nothing. It is also the shape a refactor leaves behind: the override is removed or the interface dropped, and the one-line comment stays. This is `SK7100`'s question turned round. `SK7100` asks whether the inherited text is duplicated; this asks whether there is anything to inherit at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `<inheritdoc/>` on a declaration that overrides nothing, implements no interface member and has no base type to inherit from, so it expands to nothing and the member renders undocumented."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `<inheritdoc/>` has nothing to inherit"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7103.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK7110",
              "name": "LoggerDeclaredForAnotherType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The type argument is the log *category*. A class holding an `ILogger<Other>` files every message it ever writes under another class's name, so a filter that selects this class misses them and a filter that selects the other one collects messages it never sent. Nothing fails, nothing is slow, and every log query about either class is quietly wrong — which is worse than noise, because noise is visible. The fix is mechanical: the category the declaration meant is the type the declaration is in. ⚠ **This is the objective quarter of issue #237, and the other three members are declined on purpose.** `S6669` (what the field is *named*) and `S1312` (whether it is `private static readonly`) are naming and declaration conventions, which are the most opinionated thing a linter can hold and a repository's to settle rather than a defect anybody can be shown. `S3416`'s `LogManager.GetLogger(typeof(Other))` shape is covered here only where the category is a declared `ILogger<T>`; Serilog's `Log.ForContext<T>()` is deliberately out, because choosing a context other than the enclosing type is a normal and intended use of that method rather than a mistake."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type declares an ILogger<T> field, property or constructor parameter whose T is neither itself nor one of its base types."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The logger is declared for a different type than the one that declares it"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK7110.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Maintainability",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8005",
              "name": "ThreadSleepInTest",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A sleep encodes a guess about how long the thing under test takes. On a developer's idle laptop the guess holds; on a CI agent running sixteen jobs it does not, and the test fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the code. The failure is intermittent, so it is triaged as flakiness rather than as a defect, and a suite with a few of these stops being read at all. It also costs the sleep on every run, forever, including all the runs where the wait was not needed. What the test wants is a signal — a handle, a task, a callback, a polled predicate with a generous timeout — and every one of those is both faster and deterministic. ⚠ There is no fix: the replacement is a change to what the test synchronises on, which is a design decision and not an edit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A test that waits for a duration passes or fails on how loaded the machine is."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`Thread.Sleep` in a test"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8006",
              "name": "SkippedTestWithoutReason",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skipped test is removed from the executable specification while the suite remains green. Its reason is the minimum evidence that the skip is deliberate and gives a reviewer somewhere to start when deciding whether it can be restored. No safe fix exists: the tool cannot know why the test was disabled."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An xUnit `Fact` or `Theory` is disabled with an empty or placeholder `Skip` value."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A skipped test has no reason"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8006.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8007",
              "name": "NondeterministicAssertion",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Live environmental input makes an assertion harder to reproduce. Prefer a controlled clock, a fixed identifier or an explicitly seeded random source where the test permits it. Real-clock and randomness-contract tests can be intentional, so the diagnostic requests review rather than inventing test data."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An xUnit assertion directly consumes a live clock, a new GUID or an unseeded random value."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use controlled assertion input"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8020",
              "name": "TestClassAttributeMissing",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This is the worst failure a test can have, because it does not look like a failure. MSTest's discoverer enumerates types carrying `[TestClass]` and only then looks at their methods; a class without the attribute is never opened, so every `[TestMethod]` in it is silently skipped. Nothing is reported as ignored, no runner prints a warning, and the count of executed tests simply does not include them — a suite that was never run and a suite that passed are the same colour. The signal is decidable from attributes alone, which is what separates it from `SK8001`: no call has to be followed and no assertion has to be recognised, so there is no undecidable middle for the rule to guess at."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A class declares MSTest `[TestMethod]` members and is not itself marked `[TestClass]`, so MSTest discovers none of them and the suite is green because nothing ran."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A class with `[TestMethod]` members carries no `[TestClass]`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8021",
              "name": "TestClassWithoutTests",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The attribute is a declaration of intent that the file no longer keeps. A fixture that lost its last test to a refactor — renamed away, moved to another class, commented out during a bisect and never restored — still looks exactly like a fixture that passes, because a class with nothing to run produces no output of any kind. It is `SK8020`'s silence approached from the other side, and like `SK8020` it is decided from attributes alone: the question is whether any method on the type or on a base type carries a test attribute, and answering it follows no call. That is what keeps it out of the territory `SK8001` was cut for."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A class marked `[TestClass]` or `[TestFixture]` declares no test method, in itself or in any base type, so the runner opens it and reports nothing about it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The test class declares no test"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK8022",
              "name": "SwappedAssertionArguments",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The test passes and fails on exactly the same inputs; what is wrong is the diagnosis. `Assert.Equal(count, 3)` fails with \"Expected: 0, Actual: 3\" when `count` is 0 — which says the code produced 3 and the author wanted 0, the precise opposite of what happened. Every reading of that failure starts from a reversed diff, and the reader who trusts it goes looking for the bug in the wrong half of the program. The signal is one asymmetry and it needs no guess about intent: a constant cannot be produced by the code under test, so a constant in the `actual` position is the wrong way round."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An equality assertion is called as `(actual, expected)`: the `expected` argument is not a constant and the `actual` argument is, so the failure message names the two the wrong way round."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The assertion's expected and actual arguments are swapped"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK8022.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tests",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": true,
                "fixIsSafe": true,
                "since": "1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9001",
              "name": "UnknownConfigKey",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Rider export carries roughly two thousand keys Skala will never implement. Reporting them at suggestion rather than warning is the difference between an audit and an uninstall."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A key in the `.editorconfig` chain is not in the option registry."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unknown configuration key"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9001.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9002",
              "name": "ConfigInheritedFromAbove",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Rider export ships without `root = true`, so the walk up from a source file keeps going past the repository, and a stray `~/.editorconfig` silently joins the chain — the same tree then formats differently on two machines. It is reported once per outside file, and it fires even when that file contributed nothing, because the report is about the chain's shape rather than about a value in force: the detail names the keys that came from it, or says that none did. If the inherited keys are wanted, copy them down into the repository's own file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An `.editorconfig` outside the repository is in the chain Skala resolved, so the effective configuration depends on a file the repository does not contain. Add `root = true` to the repository's top `.editorconfig` to stop the walk there; `skala config fix` adds it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The effective configuration draws from an `.editorconfig` above the repository root"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9002.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9003",
              "name": "StyleKeyInToolConfig",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "One style language, because two is how the IDE and the gate come to disagree: a value in `skala.jsonc` is invisible to Rider, so the code would format one way under the IDE and another under Skala. Aliases resolve too — `max_line_length` in `skala.jsonc` fires as readily as `skala_max_line_length` — and the detail gives the spelling to use in `.editorconfig`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A property somewhere in `skala.jsonc` resolves to an option in the registry. Style is configured in `.editorconfig` and nowhere else (ADR-001), so the property was ignored, and `skala config check` fails on it without `--strict`. Move the line into `.editorconfig` as `key = value`; the detail names the canonical key and its tier."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A style option was set in `skala.jsonc`"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9003.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9004",
              "name": "DuplicateOptionAlias",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Precedence between the spellings of one option is decided by specificity — a `csharp_` key beats a generic one — and when two spellings sit at the same rank there is no rule left to apply. The same id is raised at build time by `Rikarin.Skala.Options.Generator` when `options.json` itself gives one spelling to two options: that is the same concept on the other side of the registry, mirrored because the generator cannot reference Core."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two spellings of the same option are set at the same specificity with different values, so precedence cannot pick one and whichever wins is an accident of file order. Delete one of the two lines; the report points at the loser and names the other."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two spellings of one option are set at equal specificity and disagree"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9004.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9005",
              "name": "ContradictoryOptions",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two shapes share the id. One option under two spellings at different specificity: the C#-specific spelling beats the generic one, which is ReSharper's rule and Skala follows it, so the less specific line is reported and the winner is named in the detail. Or two *different* options that describe one behaviour, from a hand-kept table of three pairs — `trim_trailing_whitespace` against `skala_remove_spaces_on_blank_lines`, `end_of_line` against `skala_enforce_line_ending_style`, and `max_line_length` against `skala_max_line_length` — where both keys must be configured for the pair to fire."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two lines in the `.editorconfig` chain ask for different things about one behaviour. The message says which line wins and what is in force, and points at the losing line. Warning, so `skala config check` fails on it only with `--strict`; `skala config fix --resolve-contradictions` rewrites the loser."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two settings contradict each other"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9005.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9006",
              "name": "UnhonourableSetting",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The first three tell Rider to format against an indent it detects from the file rather than the configured one. Skala has no detector to switch off, so it formats against the configuration while Rider formats against whatever it inferred, and the two disagree on exactly the files where the inference differs (docs/plan/16 § Q1). The last two select ReSharper's previous formatting engine, which Skala does not reproduce. Neither is a fault in the code, which is why it is a warning about the configuration and not a finding against a file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One of `skala_autodetect_indent_settings`, `skala_apply_auto_detected_rules`, `skala_use_indent_from_vs`, `skala_old_engine` or `skala_use_old_engine` is set to `true`. Skala has no such mode, so the IDE and the gate would format the same file differently. Set it to `false`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A setting is on that Skala cannot honour"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9006.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9007",
              "name": "ToolConfigNotJson",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A tool configuration that half-loads is worse than one that does not load: an `exclude` that is silently empty means files the repository meant to skip are analysed, and a drift policy silently reset means a gate the repository relaxed is strict again. Reporting the parse failure and reverting everything to defaults is the honest state. Comments and trailing commas are accepted, so the file has a genuine syntax error at the quoted position."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala.jsonc` did not parse, so no tool configuration was read: the canonical drift policy fell back to `error` and the `exclude` list to empty. The message quotes the parser's own line and position. `skala config check` fails on it without `--strict`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`skala.jsonc` is not valid JSON"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9007.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9008",
              "name": "CanonicalDrift",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Drift is the gate condition of canonical distribution (docs/plan/03 § \"Canonical distribution\"). The canonical must physically be the repository's `.editorconfig`, so the only thing that stops it silently becoming a local fork is a hash the tool checks. An edit inside the block is lost on the next bump; an edit below the local marker survives every bump, because later sections win — which is why the remedy moves the line rather than deleting it. The hash is over the LF-normalised payload with one trailing newline, so line-ending churn and a trailing blank line are not drift, and a comment added inside the block is."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The managed block between `# skala:canonical begin` and `# skala:canonical end` no longer hashes to the SHA-256 its own marker carries: somebody edited it in place. Move the edit below `# skala:local begin`, where it survives every canonical bump, then `skala config sync --apply` restores the block. Exit 3 from `skala config diff --canonical` and exit 1 from `skala config check`, both without `--strict`; `canonical.drift` in `skala.jsonc` lowers it to `warning` or `off`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The canonical block has been edited"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9008.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9009",
              "name": "CanonicalBehind",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A canonical bump is a repository-wide reformatting commit, and eighteen repositories must not have to make it on the day it is published. So being behind is information rather than a gate — even `config check --strict` does not fail on it — and the two commands exist so that the bump can be priced before it is taken, and its commit recorded in `.git-blame-ignore-revs` once it is."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The managed block is intact and matches its marker, but it is not the canonical this build of Skala ships. Info by design, and it never fails anything. `skala config diff --canonical --options` prices the bump option by option; `skala config sync --apply` takes it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The repository is on an older canonical than the tool carries"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9009.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9010",
              "name": "FileDoesNotParse",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ADR-003, and the single most important safety property in the tool: a file Skala cannot parse is a file Skala does not touch."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The file has parse errors, so it is reported and left byte-identical."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The file does not parse"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9010.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9011",
              "name": "UnbalancedPreprocessor",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Roslyn parses one branch and hands back a tree describing only that one. Reindenting from it moves braces the other branch owns."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The member is emitted verbatim, because its brace structure depends on which branch is active."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A member's braces are split across a preprocessor branch"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9011.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9012",
              "name": "CanonicalVersionInToolConfig",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A version recorded away from the thing it versions is a version that drifts: the marker's `version=` and `sha256=` travel together with the payload they describe, and a second copy in another file is a second thing to keep in sync with nothing checking that it is. The only key under `canonical` is `drift`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`skala.jsonc` has a `canonical.version` property. The version lives in the `# skala:canonical begin` marker in `.editorconfig`, beside the bytes it names, and nowhere else. Delete the key; `skala config check` fails on it without `--strict`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`skala.jsonc` tries to pin a canonical version"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9012.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9013",
              "name": "CanonicalLocalOverride",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The canonical sits on top and the local block below it because later sections win in `.editorconfig`; that is what lets a repository keep a decision through a bump. The cost is that nobody sees the decisions unless they are listed, so each is, at the file line it lives on, and `skala config diff --canonical` prints the same list as `canonical X -> local Y`. The comparison is by exact key spelling within the same section name, falling back to the canonical's `[*]`, because comparing by option identity conflated an alias with a contradiction (`SK9005`) and `[*.csv]` with `[*]`, and both misfired on Skala's own configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A line below `# skala:local begin` sets a registry option that the canonical block also sets, in the same section, to a different value. Info: this is the mechanism working, and the list is the review artefact. Nothing needs fixing — delete the line to adopt the canonical's value, or keep it and the override survives every bump."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The local block overrides a canonical option"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9013.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9014",
              "name": "CanonicalUnmanaged",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A repository that has not opted in must not be failed by a command it did not ask for — `skala config diff --canonical` on Skala's own repository exits 0 with `UNMANAGED`, and a test pins that. Adoption is non-destructive by construction: existing sections move under the local marker, where later sections win, and a stray preamble `root = true` is folded into the canonical block and named in the applied list."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "There is no `.editorconfig`, or there is one with no `# skala:canonical begin` / `end` markers, so drift from the canonical cannot be detected. Info, never a failure. `skala config sync --apply` adopts it: the canonical goes on top and everything already in the file is preserved verbatim below `# skala:local begin`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The repository carries no canonical block"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9014.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9015",
              "name": "FileIoFailed",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A file the tool could not open is a hole in the run, and a hole that says nothing is indistinguishable from a clean file. It is an error rather than a warning because `format`, `arrange` and `verify` all exit `InternalError` on it, and the report has to agree with the exit code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An I/O failure, not a formatting one: the file was not inspected and was left exactly as it was, so it is not covered by this report."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The file could not be read or written"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9015.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9016",
              "name": "CanonicalSeverityChange",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Under `TreatWarningsAsErrors` a compiler severity raised by an `.editorconfig` commit is a build failure from a change that touches no code — it took one repository from 0 errors to 17. `SK9013` reports overrides for keys the option registry owns, and `dotnet_diagnostic` keys are deliberately not in it, so until this id existed the loudest thing the canonical could do to a repository was the one thing it did silently. A lowered severity is the widest kind of suppression there is (docs/plan/09 § \"--no-new-suppressions\"), so that direction is reported too, at info. To keep a severity the canonical would move, pin it below `# skala:local begin`: the local block wins and the change stops being reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Applying the canonical would change one or more `dotnet_diagnostic.<id>.severity` values, measured as effective value against effective value with the local block laid over the incoming payload. Warning when it moves a compiler diagnostic (`CS…`, `BC…`) up to `warning` or `error`, or newly sets one there; info when it lowers or drops a compiler severity, and info for analyzer severities in either direction. `skala config diff --canonical` lists every change before you apply it; `config sync` prints the summary before it writes."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Applying the canonical changes a `dotnet_diagnostic` severity"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9016.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9017",
              "name": "OptionValueOutOfDomain",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`SK9001` is info because the export carries some two thousand keys Skala will never implement and the user wrote nothing wrong. This is the opposite case: the key is in the registry, Skala owns it, the intent was recorded and then thrown away, and the code is formatted against a value nobody chose — for `skala_keep_existing_declaration_block_arrangement` that is the arranger rearranging a file on the strength of a setting it discarded. Non-negotiable #4 (docs/plan/00) says unknown configuration is a diagnostic and never a silent default; these were computed and reported to nobody until M9. It exits 3 rather than waiting for `--strict` because every other warning describes a configuration that means something and might mean the wrong thing, and this one describes a line that means nothing at all. `config explain` marks the row `(default) ⚠ SK9017 file:line`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A key Skala owns was set, on a line the author wrote, to a value the option does not accept. The value was discarded, and the message names the reason and what is in force instead. ⚠ Warning, yet it fails `skala config check` on its own: it is the one warning that fails the command without `--strict`, and the only diagnostic that exits it with 3 (configuration error) rather than 1. Correct the value or delete the line."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An option was set to a value outside its domain"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9017.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9018",
              "name": "FileNotDecodable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "#387. The file used to be decoded with U+FFFD for every undecodable byte, formatted, and written back with the original bytes gone. Neither unconditional promise saw it: the token comparison (`SK9099`) runs after the decode, where both sides hold the same U+FFFD, and U+FFFD inside a literal or a comment parses (`SK9010`). The token stream is only as trustworthy as the decode that produced it. It is an error where `SK9010` is a warning because nothing else will flag it: a file that does not parse fails the build, while a Latin-1 file compiles with zero warnings — `csc` decodes the bad byte as U+FFFD too, measured on SDK 10.0.401 — so a warning would let `format --check` and `verify` pass over a file Skala has never checked. Re-save the file as UTF-8 and it is formatted like any other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The file holds bytes its encoding cannot decode — a Latin-1 `é` in a file with no BOM, which is read as UTF-8 — so it is reported and left byte-identical. No verb writes it: not `format`, `arrange`, `fix` or `verify`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The file is not valid in the encoding it declares"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9018.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9020",
              "name": "BinlogStaleForFile",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Analysing what the build saw rather than what is on disk would report findings against code nobody has. Substituting the current text keeps the report about the working tree and says so."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The file on disk differs from what the recorded build compiled; the current text was substituted."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The binlog is stale for this file"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9020.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9021",
              "name": "BinlogMissingFile",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A new file that the binlog does not name is invisible to every compilation and is therefore silently unanalysed, which is the worst failure this tool can have. It is a warning and not an info for exactly that reason."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `.cs` file under the repository is in no recorded compilation. Rebuild."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The binlog does not name a file that exists"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9021.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9022",
              "name": "NoBinlog",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A binlog is the one load mode that reads what a build actually compiled — analyzer paths, generated sources, defines — so it is tried first, and its absence is worth a line rather than a silent fall-through into a mode that reads less. The detail lists the conventional paths that were searched (`artifacts/skala.binlog`, `artifacts/build.binlog`, `artifacts/msbuild.binlog`, `msbuild.binlog`, `build.binlog`), taken from the same array the search uses so the printed list cannot diverge from the searched one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The binlog load mode had nothing to read: no `--binlog` was given and none of the conventional paths exists, the named file does not exist, the SDK's MSBuild could not be located to read it, or the log itself would not open. Run `dotnet build -bl:artifacts/skala.binlog`, or pass `--binlog <path>`. Under `--load=auto` the load falls back to workspace and then loose, announced by `SK9025`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No binary log could be read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9022.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9023",
              "name": "NoSourceFiles",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An unmatched path is the same false clean as an unmatched `--rules` filter (#278, #346): a filter that selects nothing produces zero findings and a zero exit, and the reader takes it as a verdict on the code. Refusing costs one re-run; a clean report over nothing costs whatever the run was supposed to catch."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Nothing under the requested paths can be analysed. Under `--load=loose` it means no `.cs` file was found under them, reported at info, and the run exits 4 with nothing loaded. Under a project load it means none of the paths named on the command line is part of any loaded project — a typo, a path outside every project, a directory of generated sources — and `check` refuses with exit 3 rather than report a clean tree. Name the owning project with `--project`, or use `--load=loose`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The requested paths hold nothing to analyse"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9023.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9024",
              "name": "NothingToLoad",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "One id for the rung, because what the reader needs is which rung failed and why, and the message carries the why. The placeholder case exists because `MSBuildWorkspace` hands back a project with no documents rather than throwing, so a load that produced not one line of code was not empty, and the measured symptom was one failure diagnostic, one project, zero documents, zero findings, exit 0. Every outright failure marks the load failed, which stops the ladder when the caller named `--load=workspace` — or when `auto` chose it, which is `verify`, `arrange` and `fix` over any tree holding a project — and exits 4. Under the default `--load=binlog` ladder, where workspace is the middle rung, the ladder continues to loose with `SK9025` and keeps this diagnostic at error severity, and the reliability gate fails the verdict on it at exit 1 (#361): the syntactic half is reported and is not allowed to pass as the whole."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workspace load mode produced no compilation. Either nothing was found — no `.slnx`, `.sln` or `.csproj` under the root — or something was found and could not be opened: an explicit `--project` that does not exist, several candidates with none chosen, no MSBuild to be located, an assembly missing from the Skala installation, an exception from `MSBuildWorkspace`, or every project loading as a placeholder with zero documents. The same id also relays MSBuild's own workspace diagnostics verbatim, prefixed `workspace:`, at info or warning. Name the target with `--project`, or read a build with `--load=binlog`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No solution or project could be loaded"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9024.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9025",
              "name": "LoadModeFellBack",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Falling back is the right default for a tool an agent runs unattended, and it is also how a run comes to be measured under a mode nobody chose: a binlog run that quietly became a loose one has no analyzer references and no generated sources, and every semantic rule answers a different question. So the fall is announced, naming the mode that fell, so that a surprising result can be traced to the mode that produced it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The load mode tried first produced no compilation or could not run, and the next mode on the ladder was used instead: `binlog -> workspace -> loose`, or `workspace -> loose`. Info; the report's load summary says which mode actually ran. Name the mode with `--load=` to make its failure fatal instead of falling back."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The requested load mode produced no compilation, so a fallback was used"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9025.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9026",
              "name": "UnknownRuleFilter",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "It exists because its absence read as a clean tree (#278): `--rules SK3510,SK3511` bound a single string containing a comma, matched no rule, and exited 0 with no output — half an hour of an agent believing its analyzers were dead while they were fine. The comma is split now, but a mistyped id reaches the same false clean by another route, so the filter is checked against what actually loaded: the catalogue plus every loaded analyzer's supported diagnostics. A filter that is unknown in full is refused with exit 3 rather than reported here, because that run cannot produce a finding and its zero is not a measurement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`--rules` names one or more ids that no rule in this run can produce, alongside ids that can. The unknown ones contribute nothing to the report. Warning; the run continues and its exit code is unaffected. `skala rules list` names the allocated ids; repeat the option or separate ids with commas."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`--rules` names an id no rule in this run can produce"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9026.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9027",
              "name": "PartialAnalysis",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A cancelled unit returns zero findings while every other unit reports normally, so `partial: true` legitimately appears beside a large finding count — a sweep observed it next to 211 findings and could not account for it (#309). The run-level flag stays aggregate because the gate needs one answer, and this diagnostic names the unit so the reader does not have to trace it by hand. A baseline written from a partial run would accept the silence of whatever did not run, and every later run would compare against it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A compilation unit was cancelled before it finished and contributed no findings, so the run saw only part of the tree. The reliability gate fails on it unconditionally, under every gate definition, and `skala baseline create` / `update` refuse to write from such a run. Re-run without cancelling; there is no flag that accepts it."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A compilation unit was cancelled, so the run saw only part of the tree"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9027.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9028",
              "name": "GateInputUnavailable",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "These four were reported as `SK9030` \"an analyzer threw\", which none of them is. Sharing the id meant `SK9030` could not be read as \"a rule died\" — the one question #295 wanted answered — and the gate could not be made to fail on a crash without also failing on a missing baseline file. Failing in the safe direction is deliberate: an absent baseline makes every finding new rather than every finding accepted. `skala baseline create --apply` writes one; a shallow CI clone is the usual reason a `--since` ref does not resolve."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An input the gate's scoping depends on could not be read: `--since=<ref>` did not resolve, the baseline the gate names does not exist, the baseline exists but will not parse, or `--no-new-suppressions` could not compare against its reference. The gate does not fail on this id itself — but its inputs change: a missing or unreadable baseline counts every finding as new, and an unresolvable `--since` leaves findings unscoped, so a `newIssues` gate then fails with `0 accepted, N new` and this line is the only explanation."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An input the gate's scoping depends on could not be read"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9028.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9029",
              "name": "AnalyzerAssemblyMissing",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "#336, and it is the worst shape a silence can take: not a missing finding but a wrong one, carrying a fix that breaks the build. A fresh clone built only in `Release`, read through the workspace, reported 353 files to rewrite — every finding `SK0210` usings — and the same clone through a binlog reported 0. Neither generator had run, so `RuleCatalog` and every `Rikarin.Skala.Options` type did not exist, and `SK0210` was correct about the compilation it was handed: taking its advice deletes `using Rikarin.Skala.Rules.Metadata;` from a file that calls `RuleCatalog.All`. The absent file is the signal, not a failure to load one — `SK9031` covers an assembly that is present and throws, and is never fatal — and withdrawing only the affected rules is not decidable, since no rule declares which symbols it depends on. The binlog case differs in kind: its paths come off a command line `csc` actually ran, so the assembly did its work and the tree was cleaned afterwards, the same class of fact as `SK9020`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The load names an analyzer or source generator assembly at a path that does not exist, so whatever it would have contributed — generated types included — is absent from the compilation. Under `--load=workspace` this refuses the load: the units are emptied, not merely flagged, and the run exits 4. Under `--load=binlog` it is a warning and the load proceeds. Build the `Debug` configuration, which is what `MSBuildWorkspace` evaluates, or use `--load=binlog`, which reads what a build actually compiled."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The load names an analyzer or source generator assembly that is not on disk"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9029.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9030",
              "name": "AnalyzerThrew",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A third-party analyzer that throws on one syntax shape must not be able to end the run early, green or red. It must not pass it either: a crashed rule produces exactly the zero a clean tree produces, so the gate fails on this at warning severity and the agent report opens with it rather than with `OK`. Roslyn does not disable an analyzer that threw — it keeps being invoked and keeps throwing — so the message counts how many times it did and names the rules whose zero means nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An analyzer raised an exception. The run finishes and every other rule's findings are kept, but the rules the analyzer carries reported nothing wherever it threw, so the verdict fails."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An analyzer threw"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9030.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9031",
              "name": "AnalyzerFailedToLoad",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ADR-008: third-party rules are hosted, never bundled. A package that is missing, is for another framework, or drags a conflicting dependency is a configuration problem to report, not a reason to produce no report at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A hosted analyzer package named in `skala.jsonc` could not be loaded. Never fatal."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An analyzer package failed to load"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9031.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9032",
              "name": "DocumentationDiagnosticsOff",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "#384. With the property off the compiler parses no documentation and reports nothing about it, so `skala check` over a `<param name=\"old\">` on a method whose parameter is `current` returned exactly the clean report it returns over correct documentation. A zero from a check that did not run and a zero from clean code are the same zero, and the `SK9xxx` range exists to say which one a report holds. It is a note and not a warning because leaving the property off is a legitimate choice — a warning would fail the gate of every repository that has made it — and it is one line naming every project rather than one line per project, because Skala's own solution has dozens of them and a wall of identical notes is the noise `SK9001` is info to avoid. `config check` was the other candidate home and was not taken: it reads `.editorconfig` and `skala.jsonc` and loads no project, so it cannot see the property, and the statement belongs in the report whose zero it qualifies."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One or more loaded projects do not set `GenerateDocumentationFile`, so the compiler never computed its documentation diagnostics for them — `CS1572` for a `<param>` naming no parameter, `CS1573` for a parameter with none, `CS1570` for malformed XML and the rest of `CS1570`–`CS1592` and `CS1710`–`CS1739`. Info, once per run, naming every such project. Skala's own documentation rules are unaffected: they read documentation parsed whatever the property says. Set `<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>` (and `<NoWarn>CS1591</NoWarn>` if undocumented public members are not the question) to have the compiler check what is written."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A project does not ask the compiler for its XML-documentation diagnostics"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9032.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "suggestion",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Compilation",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9095",
              "name": "ArrangementRuleThrew",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The sibling of `SK9030`, and it exists for the same reason: a rule that throws must cost its own rewrite and nothing else. Before it, an exception out of a rewriter left `Arranger.Arrange` and took the caller with it — the whole report for a nightly fuzz run, the whole process for `skala arrange`. It is a warning rather than a silent skip because \"the tool declined to do something and did not say so\" is the failure mode the safety layer exists to avoid."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "One rule of the arrangement catalogue raised an exception. That rewrite did not happen; the rest of the catalogue still ran and the file is arranged by the others."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An arrangement rule threw and was skipped"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9095.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9096",
              "name": "ArrangementRevertedSymbolChanged",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Safety layer 3 (docs/plan/06 § \"Safety\"). The rewritten code still compiles and now calls something else, which is the one class of damage a diff review is least likely to catch. Re-binding every surviving identifier and comparing the symbol is the only check that can see it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Skala bug by definition. An identifier that survived the rewrite resolved to a different symbol afterwards, so nothing was written and a reproduction was dropped under `.skala/crash/`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An arrangement was reverted because an identifier now binds to something else"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9096.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9097",
              "name": "ArrangeAndFormatDidNotConverge",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Arranging and formatting are one operation and each feeds the other, so the property that has to hold is `pipeline(pipeline(x)) == pipeline(x)`. `MaxPasses` is a bound that reports rather than a net that hides: reaching it means a rule and the formatter disagree about this file. Measured over `corpus/real/`, the observed maximum is 2 — one pass to arrange and format, one to prove nothing more wants to change."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The arrange-and-format pipeline still wanted to change the file after four passes, so the file was left untouched."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Arrange-and-format did not reach a fixed point"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9097.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9098",
              "name": "ArrangementRevertedNewDiagnostics",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Safety layer 2 (docs/plan/06 § \"Safety\"): a rewrite that cannot prove its precondition does not run, and \"it looked right\" is not a proof. The two refusals share this id because they are one decision — the layer either answers \"no new diagnostics\" or it does not answer at all, and in both cases the arrangement is discarded. `CrashRefusal` in the reproduction says which of the two it was: `DiagnosticDelta` or `RebindThrew`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "This is a Skala bug; the file was left untouched. Re-binding the rewritten document produced a compiler diagnostic it did not have before — or the re-bind itself threw and could not answer whether the rewrite was safe — so nothing was written and a reproduction was dropped under `.skala/crash/`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An arrangement was reverted because it produced new diagnostics"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9098.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Semantic",
                "requiresSemantics": true,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "SK9099",
              "name": "TokenStreamChanged",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ADR-005. The check is one extra parse per file, it costs about 15 % of formatting time, and there is no flag that turns it off."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Skala bug by definition. Nothing was written and a reproduction was dropped under `.skala/crash/`."
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The formatter's output was not token-equivalent"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Rikarin/Skala/blob/main/docs/rules/SK9099.md",
              "properties": {
                "defaultSeverity": "error",
                "category": "Tool",
                "scope": "Syntax",
                "requiresSemantics": false,
                "hasFix": false,
                "fixIsSafe": false,
                "since": "0.2"
              }
            }
          ],
